        <p>Tel. No. 123. ESTABLISnED BO YEARS (in Warrington). Tel. E. Forshaw &amp; Son, L ENGINEERS, BOILERMAKERS, IRON &amp; BRASS FOUNDERS,</p>
        <p>Mersey Boiler Works, Mersey Street, Warrington. Makers of IRON CASTINGS from the smallest size up to 20 tons weight. Clean Well-moulded and of the Highest Quality. To buy the best finished castings is to frequently save 50 per cent. in labour. BRASS CASTINGS ( any weight) ; Gunmetal, Copper, Aluminium, Phosphor Bronze, Damaxlne, Acid Proof Metal and all other Alloys. CONSTRUCTIONAL IRON AND STEEL WORK, llorrugated Iron Roofs and Buildings, Farmers' Hay Sheds, Barns, Store Sheds, Am, Iron and Steel Tanks, Cisterns, Steam Jaeketted Pans, Boilers, Be. Repairs by Experienced and Skilled Workmen to all kinds of Machinery and to Steam Boilers, Gas Engines, Traction Engines, Motor Cars, Dynamos, Steam Engines, 011 Engines, Road Rollers, Motor Waggons, Electric Motors. ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING. Speciality Work. Drawings and expert advice submitted free. t• Heavy Machining and Forging done for the trade. Turning up to 111 feet diameter. Planing up to 5 feet square. Slotting up to Winch stroke. Milling, Punching, Shearing and Bending, Pressing, Dishing, Flanging and Rivetting by hydraulic pressure. Unlimited Tackle and Special Appliances for efficiently dealing with Repairs and Breakdown work, accumulated during an establishment over 60 years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>14E PRACTICAL POULTRY FARMING. MEV -M-37+..M1V-M-lENETTASIOVE</head>
        <p>14E PRACTICAL POULTRY FARMING. MEV -M-37+..M1V-M-lENETTASIOVE</p>
        <p>[BY REDCAP]. First of all, "A Happy and Prosperous New Year" to all my readers. It is rather late to extend these good wishes, but since it is my first opportunity of communication with you the delay will be pardoned.</p>
        <p>TIIE PA,C YEAR. Before we "toe the lice" proper, a brief review of the year ju-t closed may prove helpful and interesting. It is very gratifying to report that steady progress has been made. More people have taken up the culture of poultry farming than ever. The fancy side of poultry farming has been very marked, and 1 am glad to say that the utility side has made good headway. Farmers are now stocking their yards with a much better class of stock than has hitherto been the case, and more scientific methods of poultry farming are being applied, with the inevitable result that better prices for produce are being obtained. The value of dead poultry and eggs has increased and both have fetched very good prices during the past year. PURE BRED STOCK. It is always very interesting t 3 review the sale prices of pure bred stock ; as this is always a good guide as to whether the poultry pursuits are proving profitable or not. Every year Messrs. Lythall and Walters (auctioneers) hold an annual sale of pure bred poultry in Birmingham. 'this great sale which is held in January and which is just over, has proved a success. The following are some of the prices realised :—Plymonth Rocks, from 20s. ta 478. (ii.; Wyandottes, from 208. to 375. Gd. ; Indian Game, from 255. to 305.; Orpingtons, from 20s. to 405.; Leghorns and Minorcas, from 225. Gd. to 605.; and Andalusiaue up to 329. 6d. Ducks and turkeys also realised big Egures, ranging from 278 6d. to as much as 609. At this great sale 1,400 pens were staged, and the prices realised showed an upward tendency. In fairness let zee say that I have quoted the pikes of some of the best bales.</p>
        <p>•</p>
        <p>MN</p>
        <p>.</p>
        <p>IIONEY IN POULTRY. The foregoing remarks go to prove that there is money in poultry. At the same time it will be perfectly obvious that these prices do not apply to common stock. The sale is made up of stock birds sent in by our best breeders of pure bred poultry. But again let me point out that if 1,100 pens can be so disposed of at one sale, surely this is proof enough that there is a demand for good stock. And all this should encourage the futther development of a much 213glected industry. NATIONAL POULTRY ISSTITUTE. At the present time there is a movement on foot for the establishment cf a National Poultry Institute. The Development Commissioners have made a very substantial allowance, and ere another year closes, the long-talked of institute may be an accomplished fact. When this National Institute commences operations, it should prove the biggest boon to our p.ultr,v industry ever yet achieved. As I have often written, the greatest drawback to successful poultry farming in this country is the result of two causes. Excessive railway rates and high rentals in land. If one follows the pursuit where land is cheap, he is in turn handicapped by railway rates because of the distance from a good market town. On the other hand if you run a poultry farm near a gcod market town, you find that the land is so dear that one has a hard job to pay the rent, and make ends meet. Therefore, while I feel perfectly certain that the poultry industry is making headway, it can never be the success we would have it with these two great difficulties to face, and until a remedy can be applied in this direction, we shall be a long time in substantially reducing that eight millions which we spend annuZy for foreign produce.</p>
        <p>POPEL kn VARIETIES. Not a few may be interested in knowing which of the many varieties of poultry have been most popular during the year 1911. The most popular breed has undoubtedly been the Orpington, and of these the whites have predominated. J here is little wonder, because the White Orpington is one of r ur most useful general purpose fowls. They are good layers and also good table birds. Large consignments of these fowls have found their way to foreign parts, and a big business has been done with this variety. One breeder alone chartered a special train to convey one consignment which were ultimately bound for America. I held no brief for bo.maing the White Orpington. All I am doing is just stating a few facts which prove that if the foreigner does a large export trade in dead poultry and eggs, we Britisher s can easily beat them in our export trade of pure breeds and exhibition stock. I might mention that ether coloured Orpingtons have certainly not suffered much; and this ever popular fowl continues ta make excep- tional progress. It is pleasing to note a revival in some of the older breeds of poultry. We should, however, do well to cultivate their breeding on an even more extensive scale. Perhaps as the craze for new breeds dies down this may be done. There is not a great deal to report on new breeds and varieties as far as 1911 is concerned. Of these perhaps the White Faverolles and Cuckoo Orpingtona have made most headway. I have had the pleasure of seeing a number of these varietiee'and I feel sure that they will yet make a name. There is no real objection to a few new breeds providing they are useful. But one dces not like to witness their advent to the detriment of our good old breeds. TIIE PRESENT YEAR. SO much then for the past year, what of 1912 ? 'While admitting that the weather conditions are not at all in accordance with the poultry keepers' wishes, it will soon be time to commence hatching operations in real earnest. EARLY Ci[IiENS. One reason we do not get a supply of fresh eggs all the year round is because so many people hatch all their chicks at one period of the season. To get a somewhat constant supply it is necessary to commence hatching chickens from February to June. It stands to reason that if y, u only hatch your chicks in May and June you cannot expect a constant egg supply. At the same time one has to admit the many difficulties involved in early hatching. Therefore if we deal with the subject of early hatching it will be the most practical. First of all though let me say that at this inncture it is only necessary beet eggs from the heavy breeds such as Orpingtons, Faverolles, Rocks, Brahmas, Dorkings, Langshans, Ac. They take longer to mature than the lighter varieties such as Leghorns, Miaorcas, Anconas Ac , so you will see the importance of early hatching. And as it takes 21 days ere the downy ones arrive, it will be February before they are out. If you can get a few batches of February and March chicks of the heavy breeds, yOur next winter's egg supply is assured; that is, of course, if you take into con- sideration every ether precaution, such as hatching eggs from a good strain, right feeding and good Lousing. Very well then, let us set to work. Assuming that my advice is to tha amateur, I would recommend the use of a broody lan in preference to the use of an incubator. Broody here at this season ,of the year ale almost as scarce as resh eggs. You may have some difficulty in obtaining them. The plan to adopt when in search of a reliable broody is to scan the columns of a poultry journal. Often enough you will find therein a number of them advertised. You may be tempted to think that the pr;ces asked for these are too high. Don't forget, however, that a few extra shillings thus expended will repay 3-cu ten-fold later on in the season. After you have obtained the bloccly hen don't be in too great a hurry to place the eggs under, allow her sufficient time to settle down in her new quarters, after the lapse of *bent two or three days you will be able to tell if she</p>
        <p>means busicess. Having thus far proceeded and satisfied that the hen is alright you may place the eggs under ; before doing so, however, give both the nest and the hen a good dusting with Keating's powder so as to destroy all existing vermin. One important point to be observed in early hatching is, not to place too many eggs under one hen. The weather being cold and perhaps harder weather still to come, it will necessitate more than ordinary conditions if a success- ful batch of chicks are brought cif. Nine or ten eggs will be ample for the Srst sittings, and see also that the place where you set the broody is warm and absolutely free from draughts. Always remembering that she has to sit there for 21 days with the exception of the time off for meals, and again, let me remind you not to let her stay off the nest too long. Given time to feed, drink, and a shuffle in the dust bath, and on she must go, and if she is not over-inclined thitherward, gently remind and assist her back to duty. Ido not propose treating the subject of chicken management in this article, but before the chicks are numerous I will deal with it. You may expect something like a full treatise on this all- important part of the work. The matter shall be so written as to apply to chicken management at all periods of the breeding season.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
