      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>• • lIIIIMIIIMMIIMIIIIIII I</head>
        <p>• • lIIIIMIIIMMIIMIIIIIII I</p>
        <p>and cowardly—pretence that that reporter has either misunderseood or proved incapable of doing them justice. I had myself inferred many things of these objectionable people, and transparent as it generally is the device is quite an old one. A person with the gift of the gab finds himself holding fin th in a con- genial atmosphere, and encouraged by the cheers of admirers, yields to the temptation of the moment and launches upon a tirade which does not lo le nearly so' well in cold print as it sounded to the flattered and self-conscious speaker himself and in the ears of his gratified friends during actual delivery. Given absence of all scruple, what so easy then, as to pretend that the reporter has perpetrated a successim of gross errors ? To be sure bearing in mind the entire dis- interestedness of the scribes, it needs colossal "cheek" to assert that two or three professional steno- graphers of generally acknowledged ability have at one and the same time been betrayed into identical mistakes; but numbered among my own pet aversions are at least two such impudent and dishonest mortals, and, judging by the timely resolution of the associated journalists of this and neighbouring towns, the scandal is on the increase. To glance at another branch of the subject, I once attended the fortnightly meetings of a Board of Guardians consisting largely of uneducated men with a " guid conceit o' thersels," and my brethren of the quill (or ought I to write "of the fountain pen"?) agreed that it would be a pity to allow them to expose their ignorance to the world, therefore we conspired to freely summarise the speeches, and systematically outlined them in our own language. But this arrangement did not suit the self opinionated orators, and one day the chairman plainly gave us to understand that unless we were prepared to write extended reports of the debates our presence would not be as welcome as formerly. In the following day's issue of local rapers there appeared verbatim accrunts of the proceedings, the speeches being reproduced in all their naked hideous- ness ! A deputation immediately waited upon the reporters and begged them in future to use their own discretion absolutely, even if this should mean reversion to the practice which had led to the mistaken protest. Then there are the absurd threats of action for libel which some are so ready to indulge in on the slightest provocation and even in the absence of all ground for real complaint. Concerning these I have many personal narratives, for I have been peculiarly unfortunate in this particular ; but I have already outrun my allotted space, and must here cease for the- present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>ANGLING.</head>
        <p>ANGLING.</p>
        <p>Warrington Workingmen's Association (Northern Anglers' Centre).</p>
        <p>At the general meeting held at headquarters,Gladstone- Arms, the following officers were elected for the year 1912 :—President, H Mather ; chairman, J Osborne treasurer, E Brooks ; secretary, A Stout ; assistant secretary, J Potter ; committee, Messrs. J Bryres, W Ellison, J Warburton, W Bailey, W Whitfield, H Eyres, W Taylor, H Moores, T Isherwood, W Walker. The balance sheet was passed, and the society is now in a good financial position to meet the year 1912. The opening match takes place at Village Green on Sunday, July 28th; the annual match at Newbridge,near the Salt Works, on Sunday, September 15th. Resolved that no weight be accepted from other societies in match fishing. The year 1911 has been rather a heavy one as regards expense, and the membership now running up to the 100, the society are looking forward to a good season.</p>
        <p>Several anglers journeyed t 3 the Govvey on Sunday last, the conditions being most unfavourable, the stream running at such a very great pace that angling was almostimpossible. Reports from Acton Bridge show a slight improvement and after the recent rain the canal should improve greatly. However, anglers can pass their oan opinion as soon as the weather becomes right for eel fishing. . . .</p>
        <p>Members pleaEe note that-Club nights i; future- will be the second and last Monday in each month and rule 5 will ba strictly enforced.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>HOMING.</head>
        <p>HOMING.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the West Lancash ire Saturday Federation of Homing Pigeon Societies was held at the Lion Hotel, Warrington on Saturday. The Burton- wood Homing Society were elected members. All clubs represented except Lymni F.C. The balance sheet for 1911 was unanimously passed. Officials for 1912 were elected as follows :—President, 11,1 r C E Linaker, Sutton Weaver ; vice-presidents, Mr James Lowe, Lowton ; Mr Fred Boyle, Warrington ; Mr Thomas Mason, Warrington ; vice-chairmau, Mr Sidney Taylor ; convoyer,Mr Sidney Taylor; reserve convoyer, Mr Fred Boyle ; secretary, W H Paine. 14, Miller- street, Latchford ; auditors, Messrs T Nicholls and George Crank ; Lancashire Combine delegate, president and secretary. Race dates and stages old birds Worcester, May 11th ; Bath, May 18th ; Bournemouth, May 25th ; Weymouth, June Ist ; Jersey, June 15th ;. Nantas (with Lancashire Combine) July 10th ; young birds, Worcester, August 3rd ; Gloucester, August 10th ; Rath, August 17th ; S:vindon with Lancashire Combine), August 24th. Slight alterations to rules were made. A hearty vote of thanks to the chairman for presiding, and also to tha 1911 officials, closed one of the most successful years and meeting the Federa- tion has had.</p>
        <p>While hunting with the Kirkham Harriers at S near Preston, on Saturday, Miss Hollins, the eldest daughter of Sir Frank Hollins, was thrown by her horse, which swerved at a fence. One of her legs was frac- tured, and she was taken borne in a motor-car. It was- afterwards reported that she was progressing favourably.</p>
        <p>The Coming Change</p>
        <p>in the</p>
        <p>Bto</p>
        <p>.09 I. /</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <p>/</p>
        <p>Many people take their least nourishing beverage at breakfast—the very meal when they need the greatest amount of nourishment, and have, as a rule, the least time in which to take it. That the addi- tion of a really nourishing beverage to the meabwould be of great benefit, is at once consistent with the dictates of reason and the opinion of the medical profession.</p>
        <p>Elect Cocoa constitutes an almost ideal food, resembling milk, in that it contains a full proportion of proteid,carbo-hydrate, and fat, the three forms of food that build tissue, provide energy, and maintain warmth.</p>
        <p>Breakfast may be late, or one may be late for breakfast, yet there is always time to fortify oneself for the day's work with a cup of Elect Cocoa. Many a breakfast 11,411 be a better breakfast for the addition of</p>
        <p>ELECT COCOA with the</p>
        <p>OtNOW</p>
