      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>fk •</head>
        <p>fk •</p>
        <p>Mai*</p>
        <p>It is understood that the Aesociated Society of Engineers and Firemen, of which Mr. Albert Fox is the secretary, was not represented at the meeting, and the scheme was therefore adopted only by the executive of the Amalgamated Society of Railway Servants, the General Railway Workers' Union, and the Union of Signalmen and Pointsmen.</p>
        <p>_ Lord Weniock, at one time member of Parliament for Chester, and afterwards Governor of Madras,diell in London, on Monday. He was 33 years of age.</p>
        <p>-</p>
        <p>AO °MI faa</p>
        <p>The weather, at the time of writing, is enough to make the Club treasurer lose his temper, and devote himself to euchre and other safe solaces exclusively. tietv are the funds of the club to be re-imbursed, With another home match due and a foot of snow on the ground? There has not been a paying "gate" mince the beginning of December, if we except the one the Australasians took the cream off. And the 'weather has been more to blame than the team— which is bad enough, itself, in all conscience. But the War rington crowd will rally round if the condi- tions are tolerable, and a good substantial gate would be assured for the Salford match to-day if things were better underfoot. Probably the best thing to hope for is a postponement to brighter days.</p>
        <p>I am inclined to think that the team is improving notwithstanding the three successive defeats since t(he new year dawned, and the apparent slaughter at 31dham last Saturday. 'Warrington were by no means as, bad as the score of 30 points ta 10 would suggest. Oldham found everything coming off in the first half, and aided by some weak tackling on Warrington's Part and some wonderfully sure goal-kicking by Wood they ran up 20 points to nil before the interval. After- wards it was a fine game with Warrington just as good as Oldham. Both sides scored ten points in. the second "forty," and at any rate Warrington avoided the humiliation of being "nilled " once again. As a matter of fact their two tries were well worked for, and finished off by fine efforts on the part of Wo Aburn avd Renwick— the latter of whom got his first try for Warrington. most gratifying feature of the Wirepullers' "Play was the fine form of Brooks at outside half, •a, lad the consequent improvement in combination. I lia.,ve thought more than once that Brooks and Creevey right change places with advantage dating a match, Or both are really outside men. If they took turn abo:ut at working the pack there would be more variety in their play, and both would be fresher for the necessary "burst through." Dickenson and Renwick both placed a great game on Saturday, and really the former Cannot be displaced on present form, though admittedly too slow.</p>
        <p>The gr,at weakness was forward. Wa rr 1 ngt on's ,Pc'lloy— of securing nippier forwards is perfectly sound, nut at present the team is suffering from the fact bat the latest acquisitions are young, belt, and laexperienced. Time will bring the necessary 'lmprovement, no dcubt, but they were certainly no 111atoh for the burly and speedy sextette at Oldham. The command of the ball which the home side had all through the game tended to frequent scoring,especially 'as lunch of the earlier tackling by Warrington was realt. Mclntyre played a sound, plucky game, but has not yet found the length at punting which 'used to distinguhrh him.</p>
        <p>The Oldham men played well throughout. Lomag as dangerous as ever at half-back, Davies ran cleverly at centie, and Wood, as he always does ,gainst Warrington, displayed international form. He kicked six goals fiom seven attempts. A year ago he anded seven goals against Warrington, who have '-earned to respect the ex-llugby Unionist.</p>
        <p>-- Should conditions permit of footbill to-day. Warrington will have a team available which should Prove too good for Salford, notwithstanding the ratter's excellent record at Wilderspool and the defeat Wigan last Saturday. Warrington will be at full -otrength, the selection being as follows:</p>
        <p>:Mclntyre, full back; Bradshaw, Dickenson, Ben- -1,171011 and Frawley (or Woodburn) : Creevey and ."rooks ; Thomas, Slaugars, Cheaters, Tranter, Pearnley and Skelliorn.</p>
        <p>lt is hoped that the resigning of Frawley will be "eemplets by tc-day. He scored in every match for the Colonials, and owes Warrington a bit in the way gocd service. Arnold has been relegated to the team bra week, and Skelhorn gets a promotion "Inch was well-earned by his fine all-round display last Saturday against Oldham " A.</p>
        <p>team to go to Salford is : Brown.. ack ; Cllull are, Vernon, Smith and Waywell ; Datntitn -and Jordan; McCabe, Harmer, Gibbs, Arnold, P. Boardman and Brachla w.</p>
        <p>-- The Salford officials have selected the following team 14:- Fl Launce ' • A T Lovelock, W Thomas, G Callender. —.M€BleY ; J Downie, W D John; E J Thomas, R C Rees, G Thom, J Bevan and G Currie. Reserve : W Brady. Referee: J W Whiteley, Wakefield Trinity.</p>
