      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>CANADIAN NATIONAL ANNUITIES SCHEME.</head>
        <p>CANADIAN NATIONAL ANNUITIES SCHEME.</p>
        <p>Attractive Conditions.</p>
        <p>The announcement that the Canadian Government is contemplating legislation in regard to old ago pensions is another proof of the practical spirit which animates the new and progressive countries in protiting by the experience of the old. Old age pensions in C nada would supplement and round off the existing oppor- tunities already in operation under the Canadian Government Annuity Scheme, which enables all persons to make a safe and sum provision for old age at the lowest possible cost. S -If-help is the keynote that dominates all classes in a country like Canada, where local conditions make possible and commend the voluntary principal in such matters. By an Act of the Dominion Legislature of 1908 provision for old age can be secured in the form of Government annuities on terms so favourable that it must come as a revelation to anyone who has thought at all upon the subject. The particulars of this scheme are interesting at the present time when the insurance of the working classes is so much in the air, and will serve as an obj.ct lesson to s.udents of this special problem. The basis ;of the scheme is that the whole of , the sums paid into the Government by any intending annuitants are accumulated with compound interest at ' 4 per cent, per annum (free of any charge for manage- ment expenses) and such annuity as these amounts will produce at the date fixed for the annuity to begin, will le paid to the annuitant as long as he may live. The annuities may range from £lO to £125 a year ; no less and no more. They may be eontracted for at any age between the ages of five and 85; but except for invalidity or disablement no annuity is pay- able before the aga of 55. It is provided by statute that the annuity cannot be seized for debt of any kind ; that it cannot be forfeited, and that it is inalienable. The premium may be paid weekly, monthly, quarterly half-yearly or yearly as preferred. There are no lapses or penalties, and if the contributions are interrupted by sickness, loss of employment or other causes, the annuitant can resume payment at any time. The individual may provi le by single or periodical payments for annunities for himself, his %vile and his children, and he can so contract that the payments made shall be returned to him or his legal representatives should the annuitant die before the annuity is payable. The annuitant may contract under ons of the two plans laid down, namely, plan (a) that if be should die at any time before annuity becomes payable all payments made with compound interest at 3 percent. per annum will be returned to his heirs. Under the plan (h) a larger annuity for the same pa,ments can be obtained if return benefits are not desired. Employers of labour may costract for annuities for employees or may assist in the purchase of annuities. Similarly, any association of persons for fraternal benevolent, religious or other lawful purposes may contract for annuities for its members. No medical examination is required. Thus under plan (b), which will be more readily uuderstood in this coontxy, the payments made by an annuitant from his twentieth to his sixtieth year of age of 3d. a day, namely, .C 4 10s. 10d, a year will receive an annuity of £62 per annum. Payments of 61. a day or .£9 Is. Bd. per annum, will secure en annuity of £124 pr IinDUM. One is not surprised to hear that the Canadian Government Annuities Scheme is meeting with great favour. The department administering the scheme employs a large number of travelling lecturers throughout the country for explaining its advantages and the press an 4 even the pulpit are insistent in urging its principles upon the public at large.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>USEFUL TO THE HOUSEWIFE.</head>
        <p>USEFUL TO THE HOUSEWIFE.</p>
        <p>Our women are ftequently the subject of adverse criticism and unfave urable comparison with those of other nations. The British housewife is accused of lack of thtift and said to be wanting in the practice of economy, especially in regard to the proper and judiciou-e use of too 'stuffs. Perhaps there is some truth in the statement, and it is indeed an open ques- tion whether all wives and mothers of England take sufficient interest in the household budget. Do they, for example, appreciate to the fu'l the wonderful food value of Currants ? In many instances they certainly do, but all too frequent'y the important facts concern- ing Currants are overlooked. Everybody knows bow savoury and delicious are all preparations whe rein Currants appear, but there are other things which, every woman ought to know ; as for instance, that Currants are without rival in nutritive power. They do not merely fill, but feed and nourish and build up the body. That every particle of the Currant is worth eating. That the physician of Kings recommended them to be used frequently in liberal quintities. That they are easily digested. That they are the cheapest food you can buy. That currants are the food of Nature. That those who eat Currants are boue..d to enjoy robust health. Make a note of these important truths and bear in mind that there are many more ' Currant " facts worth knowing.</p>
        <p>FUSION OF RAILWAY UNIONS. The joint executive of the four railway unions resumed its session on Wednesday morning on the subject of amalgamation. The conference concluded at 1.30, and Mr. J. H Thomas, M.P., informed a representativoof the Press that the committees had unanimously agreed upon a scheme for the fusion of the four unions, which will be submitted to the members. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>DEBATE ON LIBERAL POLICY.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Parliamentary Debating Society under discussion on Tuesday evening a review of the work accomplished by the Liberal Government and a forecast of the measures to be introduced this session. There was only a sparse attendance on the GMvenmene benches, but the Opposition presented a fairly strong muster. Two questions to Ministers were submitted but Councillor Hawthorn (Chancellor of t Exchequer), the only member of the Cabinet in attend-, clime, asked that the answers might be deferred. , Mr. W. H. STANSFIELD (C.,Leader of the Opposition), called attention to the cavalier manner in which the Ministers were treeting tie House by their absence. ' He thought they should endeavour to answer the ques- tions or make an effort to provide answers for the Minister who undertook the arduous tisk of occupying the whole of the front Bench. (Laughter). Councillor J. Ilawmorte (L., Chancellor of the Exchequer) : I can only suggest that their salaries be docked. (Laughter). Mr. DoNE (C., Eddisbury) remarked that a little while ago the Prime Minister was insistent upon having questions answered at the time they were put. Now that he was in office he ought to abide by his own rule Councillor IlAwniOrtN, after expressing regret at the poor attendance of Liberals, passed under rapid review the work of the past session, lie said the Government might congratulate themselves on the rich harvest of legislation reaped in a comparatively short period—in fact they presented a good example to the " Mother of Parliameuts " in the rapidity with which they passed Bills. The King's speech was regarded as a forbidding piece of confectionery, dubbed " resurrection pie " (laughter)—but it, had more than justified expectations and had proved a savoury dish. The Government had removed some of:the anomalies of the electoral system and had dealt with ini qualities of riting. They had also carried a resolution in support of the National Insur- ance Act. Although there was a great deal of organized opposition to the measure, he believed it would be pro- ductive of much good and help many a worker to tide over the most difficult periods of his life. The Terri- torial Army Amendment Bill not only showed the desire of the Government to strengthen our defensive forces but afforded proof, if any were needed, that the , Government was as earnest as ever in the cause of Army Reform. The work of Liberal Ministers like Cardwell, Lord Spencer and Lord Haldane, had done more for the improvement of the Army and Navy than anything which had been attempted by Conservative Ministers. The Government had done justice to Ireland by granting Home Rule. So far as Ireland had had a chance of showing her fitness for self-government she had more than justified expectations. They saw that in the complete success with which she had used the large powers granted to her in the Irish County Councils Act passed by the Conservatives in 1893. Home Rule would not only give pleasure to the self-governing dominione of the Empire but make for Anglo-Sixon unity by turning into friends of this country the millions of Iris's in the United States. With regard to the future, one of the first matters that would claim attentioa would be a non- party measure. Acting on the suggestion of the Chief Constable, Mr. H. Nicholls, they intended to introduce for discussion a proposed amendment to the law respect- ing the pessession of goods believed to have been stolen. Tne Association of Chambers of Commerce had presented a memerial to the Horne Secretary in favour of such an amend.nent, and Warrington had supported it. Under the general law as it stood at present the obligation lay upon the police tee prove the uulawful possession of goods before effective action could be taken against suspected persons, and as a result the stealers and the receivers of enormous quantities of goods went unpunished. It was now proposed to give greater powers to the police to enable them to arrest suspected persons without being first forced to prove unlawful possession by identification or finding the lawful owner. There were several important measures mentioned in the King's Speech not already dealt with, and to which attention had yet to be devoted. One or those measures related to industrial disputes. Without anticipating too much the provisions of the Bill, he might say all were no doubt convinced that it would be much better if there was compulsory arbitration on points of differ- ence before a strike was declared rather than efforts at a settlement after a costly indus- trial war. Unemployment was another serious problem which would have to engage attention The establishment of labour exchanges had done some- thing to bring a man in search of employment and the employer into closer touch than formerly and tho unemployment section of the Insurance Act would help to relieve the difficulties of the unemployed in their hour of need. It was hoped to introduce a Develop- ment Bill to develop the resources of the country. They would, however, never be sable to deal with unemployment effectively uutil the present trend of migration from the country to the town had been stopped by making the coltivation of land more attrao- tive. It. was further intended to ask the House to consider a thoroughly national system of education. They were still in the denominational morass in regard to that question although feeling was not so high as it was in 1902 when the Act destroying School Boards was passed. All true lovers ' of education would be glad to EOO the question lifted above the cloud of sectarian bitterness and jealousy ' and placed on a truly national basis. The question of ‘Velsh Disestablishment would also be dealt with. The broad practical ground on which the case for Disestablishment rested was the fact that the Church in Wales was only the Church of a small m:nority of the Welsh people. Every test, both religious and political, that could be applied, proved that to be true. The religious test showed a three to one preponderance on the side of Nonconformity—(Question)—and by return- ing an overwhelming majority of their representatives to the House of Commons in favour of Disestablish. merit, the people of Wales had said over and over again, in the most emphatic way, that they wanted it. Tne Government was not content to let the licensing questi in drop with the fiat of Lansdowne House. Tnere ought to be B time limit to the freehold which was given for whet was only a leasehold in a licence. There should also be earlier closing of public houses. In this connection, the speaker quoted the marked effect of the earlier closing of public houses in Liverpool during the strike, as shown in the great diminution in the cases of drunkenness and the improved trade dons by shopkeepers in the poorer neighbourhoods. The Government was also anxious to deal with Poor Law administration, but he was afraid there would not be sufficient time. The last two nights of the session would be devoted to the Budget. The LEADER OF THE OPPOSITION (air. W. H. Stansfield) said it often happened that Bills which were conceived and passed in a hurry were worthless. He maintained that some of the Bills passed by that House had not received sufficient consideration and, if put into actual operationawould result in a farce. The Liberals prided themselves upon having remedied the anomalies of the electoral system, whereas they had only tinkered with a few points in order to get votes for their own side. The Insurance Bill was ill-cenceived, ill-con- sidered, and it was rushed through the House in such a way that half the members did not know what its scope was. Referring to the Imperial Parliament, be contended that the constant re-shuffling of posts in the Cabinet was a sign of weakness in the Liberal party. He did not agree that the Liberals bad strengthened the navy. The late First Lord of the Admiralty was such a conspicuous failure that they had to shunt him in order to make way for a stronger man. As Irish Ministers they had had several failures and the present occupant of the office was a jolly old gentleman whom everybody liked, but who was all wind, bluff and good humour. (Laughter). In the education department, Mr. Runcimen raleed a storm of indignation throughout the country by the snobbish proposals of the Holmes circular. for which he was responsible. Not a particle of good to education had filtered through from the ministers of the Government. Mr. Balfour's Act of 1902 was still par excellence—an Act that could not be beaten. It was said that sectarian bitterness arel jealousy hid largely died down. But where did the bitterness come from ? Certainly not from the Conservative benches. The bitterness and jealousy were shown by the Radicals on every possible occasion, not only with regard to the schools but to the Church. Another Bill, which would be pro-!nced by nothing else but sectarian bitterness and fore-shadowed. The me nibttey constantly twh aems, aendoewt taohsna eat pb rye peovnedrey, a nt ce set of Nonconformity that ought to be made, because on many occasions the Church people in Wales had asked for a religious census and who had opposed it? And what had been the test? It was proved that the figures produced on behalf of the Nonconformist section to the Royal Commission were untrio,tworthy and were admitted as such by their own statistical witness. The figures, in fact, were so unreliable that the Royal Commisston practically had to ignore them, whereas the Church figures were com- piled from the actual names and addresses of communicants. When reference was made to every test it was not fair, because every test bad not been applied. Even if the Radicals proved what they wanted to prove, that was not a sound argument for the destruction of the Church in Wales. The present-day legislation of the Radicals was not conceived in the old-fashioned Radical spirits They were not Liberals as they understood the word Liberal, and all their efforts were tending to Socialistic legislation. Oaly recently Mr. Jellicoe, who fought in the Liberal interest against Mr. F. E. Smith in Liver- pool, had warned the Liberal party that they would ruin the country by the Socialistio trend of their legislation. There was promise of some attempt at a solution of industrial disputes. 'I he question was a very pressing and serious one. There never was a time when there was such eert unrest, and one must end did look for it as the logical result of the unsettling legislation of the Radicals. Employers could not find any recurity for their capital atrhooamtserndhiclelatshseselatbaoguarinmstenclwaessrebybetihneg hurled a la t hei r throats party No wonder that industrial disputes, were rife. Another very urgent matter was unemployment. The Conservative Party had pointed out the solution and the only solution to those problems, which was to be found in a scientific system of tariffs. Radicals might</p>
        <p>kone " ) decrei</p>
        <p>try to burke that question and try to laugh it out of existence, bul there was no doubt it would have to be faced. If, in dealing with the education question, the Government intended in effect to eliminate moral and real religious teaching from the curriculum then he bade them pause for they bad done a great deal of harm already, but by doing that they would place the cop!ng on their iniquities. Referring to the _ease in drunkenness in Liverpool during the strike, he maintained that temperaece was not brought about simply by closing public-houses. If eople wanted drink they would get it in other ways. Liverpeol woe one of those places where denominational instruction was very strong and where the children were taught in the schools to exercise—their moral sense. Was it not possible that during the period of fierce unrest, those lessons came back to the populace and they exercised a moral restraint and showed that they could be respectable, clean-living and well-conducted citizens I With regard to the land question, the speaker said his experience of small holdings he a certain district led him to think that he would rather be a criminal and work in a prison than be employed on those holdings. It was slavery of the worst type. Mr. W. KILDUFF (N., Dublin Harbour) said capital and labour ought to be guided by conscience and not merely by questions of pounds, shillings and pence. If the present strike in the cotton trade was allowed to develop on the lines of the railway strike and become so menacing that soldiers had to be sent down, it would disgrace us, as a supposed Christian and civilized countryen the eyes of foreigners The Education Bills of the Liberal Government bad always meant a time of great trial to the Nationalists. They were democrats but they put religion and con- science higher than social life. Real education must concern the soul of the child, and he must be taught to avoid evil from higher motives than keeping out of the clutches of the police. He trusted that any measure which the Government brought forward would be " liberal " in the true sense of the term and would he based neon the highest ideals. Mr. R. BRISCOE (Hereford), contended that the Government would do more to ensure the prosperity of Ireland by introducing a system of tariffs for the United Kingdom than by patching up Irish administra- tion. By tariff reform they would get to the root of the matter. Mr. CHAMBERLAN (SOO , Gorton). said tariff reform could be no settlement of industrial difficulties. It was merely a trick on the part of the plutocracy further to enslave the workers. He totally denied that the Liberal Government was working on Socialistic lines. lines. No Government that was capable of Socialistic legislation would have sent the military arm to Liver- pool to shoot down the strikers. Mr. C. Peiesros (C., Darlington) : You admit they shot the strikers, do you ? Mr. CHAMBERLAIN : There is no disputing it With regard to the proposed Disestablishment of the Church in Wales the speaker main- tained that there were other matters of more pressing importance awaiting attention. The classes were naturally antagonistic, without the aid of the Liberal Government. The capitalistic class was out to obtain the cheapest labour and to exploit men to the fullest extent, while the workingrnae had no capital hut his labour and he wiseed te obtain the best price for it. Class war resulted. His remedy for iniu strial disputes was to socialise the means of production. With regard to education, he contended that in the past it had been dealt with too much from the stand- point of the well-to-do, while the working-classes had been neglected. Economic conditions forced the . workers to take their children from school just when they were beginning their education. Mr. IL DONE (C., Eddisbury) regretted that the question of unemployment had been given a minor place in the Government programme. There was no doubt that unemployment was the greatest evil in this country and also the principal factor in producing a low standard of morality. "Satan finds some mischief for idle hands to do." If a man was out of work for a considerable time he deteriorated morally, physically and intellectually. Richard Cobden foisted Free Trade on this country in order to get cheap labour. He was the greatest opponent of trade unions that ever existed. On the question of Welsh Disestablishment, the speaker denied tint the Welsh elections were a true criterion of the views of the Principality, and pointed out that the majority of the Liberal members made no reference to the question in their election addresses. Mr. W. WATSON (Soc., E. Birmingham), said the Government had treated the House disgracefully by assembling in such small numbers. He agreed with Mr. Done that a man sank from being a useful member of society when he was out of work for a long period. The remedy for unemployment WU a reduction of hours of labour, but in order to do that they must have a measure of tariff reform. It would be useless to shorten the hours and increase wages If our trade was to be stolen by other exporters. At the same time, we did not want to go the whole hog and get the German or Russian system of tariff reform. No Labour man would agree to food taxation in any shape or form. The reason it was proposed was that any loss in revenue should he made up by the poor. In the measures contemplated by the Government be did not detect any of the " rare and refreshing fruit " which had been promised. Mr. C. PLINSTON (C., Darlington), said that if, as he believed, the great majority of the Irish Nationalists were out and out Protectionists, they would not rest content with a Home Rule measure which forbade them to raise tariffs. Instead of the Home Rule Bill being a settlement of the Irish question, they would only have dispute after dispute between the two Goverments. It had been contended that the labour exchanges were ful- filling their object but whereas, during the past twelve months, the applications for work had numbered 1,192,000 employment had been found for only 375,000 and even for them the employment was not regular. Tbe most distressing lenture was that thousands never applied to the exchanges because they knew it was hopeless. It was not "rare and refreshing fruit "which the multitude required ,but. work and bread. With regard to education, the question with the Government in devising their schemes was not : "Is the principle we are advocating just ?" and" Will it tend to the better education of the people ? "but rather " Will it please the passive resister ? " The Liberal Government to-day, as the party had been for thirty years past, was an organised hypocrisy. C runcillor IlAwTiron's, in replying to the debate, said the Tories were loud in their advocacy of army reform, but when they got into power their reforms took the form of the Brodrick cap. (Laughter). Dealing with Welsh Disestablishment,the speaker quoted figures which be contended, proved a three-to-one pre- ponderance of Nonconformity. Disputing the assertion that the Government was Socialistic, he referred to Mr. Philip Snowden's vigorous opposition to the Insurance Act. If the Government were Socialists, how was it that when elections came, the Socialists opposed them? Mr. STANSFIELD : Because they think they can squeeze you. Councillor Ilewrnonee in a passing reference to New Zsaland politics, said Mr. Ward had practically lost his majority through trying to force on the people a national service scheme which a great many of them deeply resented. (A voice : " Liberals bEtIF3TO:') YO3, Liberals and Conservatives too, might beware. At tbe present time tariff reformers were not anxious to refer to the ideas iu vogue in Germany. (Laughter.) The recent election results had shown that where tariff reform existed, Socialism seemed to thrive oven better than in a Free Trade country. As to the effect of the early closing of public houses on drunkenness, he was surprised that the headmaster of a day school shout d claim that the peaceful conditions in Liverpool during part of the strike period were due to denominational teaching. If there was one city where the denomina- tionists flew at each other's throats it was Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>SORE HANDS FOR FOURTEEN YEARS</head>
        <p>SORE HANDS FOR FOURTEEN YEARS</p>
        <p>" For fourteen years I suffered with my hands. The skin came cfl in patches, leaving my hands raw. They itched terribly and if 1 rubbed them, water oozed out leaving them worse than before. My thumbs and first fingers were the worst, the skin came off, leaving a red, sin-oth surface. They itched and burned terribly. Other parts of my hands became affected, the skin first drying up and peeling off. They were drawn together and I could not grasp anything. "I tried various ointments, but they did me no good I went to five different doctors, having medicine and ointment from each, but all to no purpose. One of the doctors told me they would never be cured, another said the oil was all dried up out of the skin. Not one gave me any hope of ever being cured. A friend thought one of my fingers was withering away. I tried various ointments recommended by friends, but they all failed to cure. After some .time, a friend told me about the Cuticura Remedies, having tried them with success for hands similar to mine, 1 tried them, and I found that nothing had such a soothing and cooling effect. They acted wonderfully, and I noticed the soothing effect from the first. My hands were completely cured by the Cuticura Soap and Ointment. It has stood the test of a dozen years. It was wonderful, I never saw any- thing like it. Other members of the household have benefited by it, and we are never without a box." (Signed) Mrs. Alice Blornley, 129, Durham-street, Rochdale, England, January 3rd, 1911. Cuticura Soap and Ointment are sold by chemists everywhere. A sample of each with 32-p. book post-free from nearest depot : Newbery, 27, Charterhouse-square, London; R. Towns &amp; Co., Sydney, N.S. W. ; Lennon, Ltd., Cape Town ; Muller, Maclean &amp; CO., Calcutta and Bombay ; " Cuticura," Boston, U.S.A. Tender. faced men should shave with Cuticura Soap Shaving Stick:</p>
        <p>M. Vedrines made a remarkable Sight at Pan on Saturday. He flew a distance of 150 kilometres (93 miles) in lhr. 2min. 43secs., covering a distance of 142 kilometres 430 metres (89 miles 469 yards) in the hour.</p>
        <p>HOUSE FURNISHING.</p>
