      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>TOUTING FOP, TRADE. According to the minutes considered at last week's meeting of the Town Council, the Cemetery Committee have taken steps for the suppression of canvassing mourners on the spot in relation to memorial stones, &amp;c. I have had some talk with an acquaintance who has made a competency in that way, and he appeared to be incapable of understanding how anyone could object to such practices. I observed that it seemed a delicate task to approach bereaved relatives at the graveside with a business proposal. "low do you lead up to the subject?" I ventured to ask and, as he was evidently in no hurry to satisfy my curiosity, I went on—" I suppose you will have to begin with an appropriate pissage of scripture, such as All flesh is grass, and, after expressing sympathy, gradually work round to the real point." Looking rather hurt, my friend passed on without another word. I note that the registrar has been instructed to withdraw the list of interments hitherto on view. It is impossible, of course, to discontinue the exhibi- tion of notices of impending marriages, and I know of districts where these are exploited in an extraordinary manner. Young people who are on the eve of their wending are bewildered by receipt of advertisements and even personal visits from enterprising tradesmen anxious to supply them with household furniture, &amp;c. Some of these modest couples, desirous of going off quietly, may have been careful not to take even intimates into their confidence and they naturally wonder how a secret which they have jealously guarded from persons who, in ordinary circumstances would have been among the earliest to he taken into their confidence, had become the common property of butcher, baker, candlestick- maker and the reit. I encountered a postman on his morning round, and he informed me with a leer that a certain townsman was contemplating matrimony. " See this ?" he continued, indicating a packet which he had separated from the others in his hand. 'That's from 'a, tho jeweller. How do I know ? I've handled some stock of 'em in my time. There's a card enclosed, and it's punched with boles of various sizes for the convenience of customers. Lots of young 'uns don't like being seen at the shop, 'cos it sort o' gives the show away, you see ; so the tradesman sends 'em one of these cards,and all they've got to do is to mark the hole that fits the girl's ring finger. That's how I tell 'em ; I can feel the holes when I press the envelope —then I turn to the address where it has to be delivered, and I think d to myself, 'Urn, ah, yes, just so! ' " After all, the explanation is quite simple. Shopkeepers who culti- vate these methods make frequent calls at the offices ef the superintendent registrar of marriages for their respective areas, and scrutinise the notices there dis- played—as anyone has a perfect right to do. A REVOLUTIONARY SUGGESTION. One cannot but regard Councillor Craik as either an extremely innocent public representative who will know batter in a while, or a positively dangerous innovator and iconoclast. He actually wanted to know (vide" Examiner" report of the Council meeting) why the tram lines at the Cemetery terminus had not undergone the needful and expected alteration while the road at that point was up for waterworks purp ses ! -lust fancy ! Why, I thought that every dweller in a civilised country was by this time aware that what an individual would in his own private business recognise and unhesitatingly follow as accord- ing to the dictates of sound commonsense, to say nothing of ordinary economy, in the opinion of the , same individual and other men of undoubted business ability acting together in a public capacity, becomes • rank heresy, if not a high crime and misdemeanour. Under administrative authorities throughout the ' country, it is the invariable rule for committees to work independently and to ignore the existence : of their fellows at work in other sections. Were it not so, the professional jokers on the staffs of the humorous papers would find themselves minus • valuab:e and never-failing stock-in-trade. Let me • illustrate by one or two instances from my own ' experience. The Highways Committee of a Town Council as hose meetings I regularly attended for years, took advantage of the opening up of a big estate to construct a broad thoroughfare amid what was being : prepared for residential purposes. By the time they I ban completed their good job, houses were being run up all along the route. So the Waterworks Com- mittee's myrmidons came along and ripped up the fine new road from end to end, in order that pipes might be inserted for the supply of the indispens- ; able fluid.• As soon as that gang had covered up their handiwork, their comrades in the gas department . undid it all to lay dowa gas mains ; and when they had finished and gone away, the electrical engineer • did a little touting with the result that a cable had to be put in for a number of customers who preferred ; the newer illuminant. That sort of thing is con- stantly gring on everywhere. The other day I was ? Passing along the main road of a Lancashire borough, and noticed that the whole had been paved since I saw it before. On enquiry I found that the work had only been done a couple of months ago, nevertheless, ' the granite blocks —which had been laid by experts, duly concreted, tarred, and completed in a workmanlike and wholly creditable manner—were "up " in half-a-score of places within a distance of a r mile or so, different committees having as usual sent their men to work their sweet will upon it on various I pretences. Where has Councillor Craik been living not to know of these things? Perhaps he did know, and had some Quixotic notion about tilting at the vicious system ? Vain and-delusive hope. AN OCTOGENARIAN DEAN. A week ego it was noted in these columns that Dr. Pigou, Dean of Bristol, was one of three " octogei aniums " (as I heard an old fellow call it), among English Cathedral Clergy. A while ago I narrated in this column an incident at a Spirituolisic seance in which Dr. Pigiu, then vicar of Halifax, figured prominently and amusingly. I there had other experience of the doctor which was not so pleasant. The latter had, from his pulpit at the Parish Church, delivered a sermon which resembled in a startling degree what Mr. Gladstone had written in his "impregnable Rock of Holy Scripture," and extracts from both were published in parallel columns in the local Liberal paper on whose staff I was at the time. The wicked perpetrator of this cruel joke did not scruple to suggest that it was a case of plagiarism, to say the least, it was a curious and most unusual coincidence. While this outrage was fresh I was sent to interview the pained clergy- man respecting something he was reported to have said in another parish. The evening was fairly well advanced when I was announced by the maid, and the genial master promptly proclaimed in a voice loud enough to be quite distinct in the hall where I stood that it was " very late." Then we entered upon a contest of rudeness. I persisted, pleading that I should not detain him a moment, and at length the doctor yielded, crying out in studied tones of resigna- tion, " Ah, well, let him come in." On entering the room, I f, und him seated on a low hassock before the fire, and as he was planted in the middle of the hearthrug and did not turn his head, I could not see his face. " Well, said he, "What is it ?" I told him I merely wished to verify a statement imputed to him in a newspaper cutting which I held in my hand. He asked to see this, at the same time putting one hand over the shoulder nearest the door. I poked the extract into his fist, and after a glance at it, he returned it in the same way, adding that it was "all right." I was glad he thought so, but I much preferred the capital stories he was able to tell when in the mood. Some of my readers must have come across his published anecdotes, some of which e ere gleaned about the period and in the locality to which I have referred ; one of them, indeed, formed the story for the day in the " Daily News "last week, and had to do with an old verger whom I knew very well, Sager by name. JOURNALISTS AND PUBLIC SPEAKERS. As stated in last Saturday's issue, the reporters of the Warrington, Widnes and Runcorn Press, have at last been provoked formally to protest against the too common and unfortunately growing practice among public speakers of disclaiming newspaper records of their utterances on the shabby—nay, cruel</p>
      </div>
