        <p>The Salford captain (W Thomas) is first reserve for Wales in the .International match at Oldham, and in the event of his inclusion in that team, Downie will fill the vacancy in the three-quarters, and Brady, whose hand is now sound, will partner John. Pugsley ip, Os) in the Welsh side, and Ritchie has returned flom Hesham to take his place. Salford have a unique record at Wilderapool, having won four matches there, lost four, and drawn two.</p>
        <p>the play, and after Turtill had improved a penalty kick Wyatt crossed, Molyneux failing to improve. On the resumption Treowitb, the St. Helens half-back, and Edmunds, Ebbw Vale, collided, the latter player having the misfortune to break his jaw bone. Thus weakened Ebbw Vale's defence was severely taxed, and Wyatt handed to Scholee, who ran over with an unimproved try. Jenkins attempting to mark near his own line lost the ball, and Mavitty had Ii tie difficulty in crossing for Turtill to improve. Thomas, Ebbw Vale, was ordered off, and Mavitty, Waddell. and Greenwood added further tries for St. Helens, whilst Thrall kicked two more goals. Result :—St. Helena, 5 goals, 8 tries (34 points), Ebbw Vale nil. Teams and officials : St. Helens—Turtill, back ; Flanagan, Greenwood, Scholes and Wyatt, three-quarter backs ; Trenwith and Molyneux, half backs ; Prescott, Waddell, Daniels, Butterfield, Mavitty and Saunders, forwards. Ebbw Vale—Dai Davies, back ; Llewellyn, Jenkins, Duncan and Billingham, three-quarter backs ; Dai Thomas and Higgins, half-backs; Foley, Dal Jones, Staike, Ilitchens, Cox and Edmunds, forwards. Referee, Mr. T. Walsh, Hunslet. Touch judges, Messrs. J. Jones, Wigan and H. Yates, Runcorn.</p>
        <p>The League table up-to-date is as follows : For. Against. Per- P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. c'nege Eluddersfrld22 19 2 1...82 138 578... 27 31 147 8863 Wigan 2l 18 3 0... 59 76 316... 21 20 102 8511 Hunslet 22 15 6 1... 36 SO 312... 33 35 172 7045 Oldham 22 15 6 1... 62 84 376... 37 46 212 70.45 Hull K. R 20 14 6 0... 64 61 311... 26 36 160 7000 St. Helens 21 14 7 0... 47 72 310... 34 36 176 6666 Halifax 21 13 6 2... 40 64 272... 20 36 148 6666 Wakefleld T 20 12 7 1... 40 55 245... 40 51 233 62.50 Hull ...... 24 13 9 2... 45 73 309... 28 47 197 58.33 Batley 2O 11 9 0... 36 37 183... 21 28 126 55 00 Widnes 2O 10 8 2... 30 37 171... IS 31129 55300 Rochdale H. 20 11 9 0... 37 44 208... 30 53 219 55.0 C Dewsbury 21 11 9 1... 46 60 272... 36 47 213 51.76 Broughton. 2l 11 9 1... 28 38 170... 28 32 152 5419 Warrington.22 10 11 1... 25 46 188... 38 46 214 4712 Leigh " 10 11 1... 37 44 206... 36 43 201 47.72 Reighley lB 8 9 1... 33 43 195 23 59 233 47/2 Swinton 01. 8 10 3... 31 36 170... 35 43 199 45.23 Leeds Cl 9 12 0... 34 58 212... 36 58 246 42 85 York 19 8 11 0... 27 34 153 46 51 215 42.10 Salford 21 7 11 3... 27 36 162... 37 39 191 40.47 Barrow l9 6 12 1... 19 33 137... 47 57 265 34.21 Runcorn 2O 5 14 1... 20 28 124... 38 48 220 27.50 Coventry 21 4 15 2... 27 26 132... 51 92 378 23.80 Ebbw Vale 19 1 15 3... 14 16 76... 54 81 351 1315 Bramley l9 0 17 2... 14 16 76... 68115 534 5.26 Bradford 2l 1 20 0... 19 23 107... 63 97 417 416</p>
        <p>_ LEIGH V. WIDNES: FtNE FORWARD RUSHES. The 3,000 spectutors at Leigh saw a game which was somewhat scrambling and uninteresting, though relieved from sheer monotony by some fine rushes on the part of the forwards. Leigh had the best of matters in the first half, and Johnson twice got over the line, but he was adjudged on both occasions to have stepped into Ouch. Swinton, the Widnes wing, missed a great chance, after charging down a kick, by knocking on. At the interval there was no score. Lally, the Widnes half, was injured shortly before the interval, mid did not play again. Nevertheless, the Widnes forwards played so well that Leigh had the worst of matters in the second half. About five minutes from the finish Whittaker scored a smart try for Leigh, Donohue tail- ing. Result: Leigh I try (3 points), Widnes nil. Teic.s and officials : Leigh.—Bolewski, back S Johnson, T H Johnson, D Davies and Donohue, three-quarter backs: Gainey and Whittaker, half backs; Lowe, Cartvrright, Lee, Meiling, Woods and Barton, forwards. Widnes.—Jones, back ; Swinton, Laws, Taylor and Reid, three-quarter backs; Lally and O'Gara, half-backs; Lloyd, G Aspey, S Aspey, Johnson, Higgins and Cosgrove, forwards. Referee, Mr A flestford, Broughton. Touch judges, Messrs F Ratcliffe, Swinton, and El Ball. Wigan. LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>The " A "team to entertain Warrington " A " will be:—</p>
        <p>F Hughes; J Ratcliffe, F Higbam, W Barnes, R J Hough ; J Hampson, J Scott; G Cleary, R Skeldon, W Battersby, A J Thompson, J Knott, and A N Other. Reserve : G Russell.</p>
        <p>The heavy snowstorm on Wednesday morning caused the match between the Northern Rugby League and Australasians, which should have been played at Fartown, Huddersfield, to be postponed. In conse- quence of the tourists' many representative matches during the next fortnight the match may be abandoned.</p>
        <p>I WARRINGTON "A" v. OLDHAM "A."—There was a capital attendance at Warrington on Saturday for the match with Oldham L" A." Some disappointment was felt at the non-appearance of Smith, a new centre, and in consequence Unswortla played centre, and Harmer half-back. Warrington pressed hard at the outset and Harmer was thrown into touch when in the act of scoring. Oliver and Jackson, for Oldham, by clever parsing, removed ;play to tl.e Warrington end, where another passing bout by the Oldham ba:ls resulted in Owen receiving at midfield, running splendidly, and beating Wavwell, he scored under the posts. No goal resulted. Warrington pressed from the restart, and Waywell, receiving from the scrimmage, scored cleverly. No goal was kicked. Oldham now attacked and Oliver scored under the posts. White kicked a goal. After some loose play a forward dribble was started by the Warrington pack from which Skelhorn scored. Povoy failed at goal. Wayvvell was tackled on the verge of the line when he looked all over a scorer. Half-time Oldham " A," eight points; Warrington " A "six points. • Play was very exciting in the second half, and the smart determined play of the locals was loudly cheered. Smart play by Daintith and Harmer resulted in Waywell showing his heels to the visitors and scoring near the posts, but Gibbs failed at goal. Having got the lead Warrington played with redoubled energy, and Harmer forced his way over from a scrum near the line. Then Oldham rallied and a couple of minutes froml time White soured near the posts. The goal kick was easy and would have given Oldham a" snatch " victory but the scorer missed the object badly, and Warrington retired winners of a capital game by 4 tries (12 points), to 1 goal, 3 tries (11 peinte).</p>
        <p>The draw for the first round proper of the Northern Union Cup will take place on Tuesday next.</p>
