      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <p>Warrington had a full team available for the first time in several weeks. on Saturday, when they visited Oldham, but as usual ,they were easily out-pointed at Watersbedding. The day was fine, but the ground was heavy and holding. About 12,600 spectators aseembled— a capital gate considering the Association Cup-tie in the town.</p>
        <p>Warrington—Mclntyre, back ; Bradshaw, Dickenson Renwick and Woodburn, threequarters ; Brookes and Jordan, balces ; Tranter, Arnold, Cheaters, Fearnley, Thomas and Sbugsrs, forwards. Oldham—Wood, back ; Smith, Cook, Davies and Young, threequarters ; Brice and Lomas, halves ; Ferguson, Avery, Wright, Wiltshire, Smith and Jardine, forwards.</p>
        <p>Referee, Mr. E. H. Smirk, Wigan. The game opened with some punting exchanges Wood having the advantage over Mclntyre and giving his side a footing in the Warrington half. Oldham's backs soon got the ball out, and Smith received on the wing, but was downed by Bradshaw and Brooks. Warrington made a brief excursion to the other end but combined poorly, and Oldham were soon hack again as the result of superior play. A scrummage near the lino brought the opening score as Lomas got hold and made light of the Warrington tackling, Wood adding a goal from the try. Good play by Renwick on the Warrington side, and Devlin an the other, provided sparkling incidents and Wood did well to save his line from one Warrington attack. A fine rush by the home forwards drove Warrington back again, and Lomas coming up at the lest minute got another try as the ball was going dead. Wood again kicked a line goal with the heavy ball Warrington did better than the scoring suggests, but could not get through. Brooks was vary active but the threequarters could not get into strike and \Wood cleared. Jardine added another try as the result of good play by Brice, Wood sealing easily. Just on the interval fine passing led to Young racing over atithe corner, and Wood kicked a great goal from the touch- line. Half-time: Oldham, 2 points; Warrington, nil. The resumption was attended with a fine opening movement, for after Oldham had been called back, Jordan served Brookes from the pack at half-way, and the international made a floe opening on the left, Dickenson bluffed Cook and Smith, and sending on to Woodburn, the ex-Coventry man best Woodward after a fine run, and scored. Mclntyre missed the goat shot. Toe borne side immediately went to the Warrington end, mainly through the instrumentality of Wood, and twin the Warrington goal had narrow escapes. Follow- ing on a tall kick by Wood, the Warrington defence wri..s weak and Avery scored in the simplest manner. This time Wood experienced his first failure of the afternoon The game for a long time was confined to the War- rington end and after Wiltshire and Davies had led the way to the line, Brice from some scrambling play was awarded a try. Warrington were by no means disheartened at the rapid scoring, and fast forward work and clever play by Brookes caused the game to be strenuously fought at the Oldham end. Bradshaw had an easy chance to cross, when he was badly fouled by Smith, and the sub- sequent penalty was successfully taken by Thomas,who dropped a goal from a difficult angle. Oldham bad to cuatinually defend and after Wood had made one effectual clearance, Warrington again raided the home line, and a fine passing movement started by Brookes and taken part in by Arnold and Dickenson, led up to Renwick scoring, Thomas adding the goal. In the closing stages both sides showed signs of falling away, for the pace slackened. The home side reached the Warrington end and off-side play got the "Wirepullers" penalized, Wood having little difficulty to place the goal from . the easy. position. Result : Oldham, six goals six trTi (30 points) ; Warrington, two goals two tries (ten points). YORK SURPRISE THE LINNETS.</p>
        <p>There was no gainsaying the fact that York were the better team on the Cheshire ground. The best department was perhaps the half back line, Daniels and Boggles playing a splendid game, whilst the three- quarters were always dangerous. They also received great assistance from the forwards, who heeled out R from the pack most consistently. nncorn were first to score through lioworth. After a hot attack by the Yorkshiremen Boggins picked up rather easily and scored. Daniels gosling. Massey then added another unimproved try for Runcorn, and a try from Oliver completed the scoring in the first half. The only score in the second portion was a try for the Yorkshiremen scored by Boggins. Results :—York 1 goal, 3 tries (11 points), Runcorn, 2 tries (6 points). Teams : Runcorn—Shaw, back ; Massey, Butterwortb,Steven- son, and Hozworth, three-quarter backs; Cawley and Watton, half backs ; Peacock, Richardson, Inman, Donoghue, Doggett, and Padbury, forwards. York—Moore, back ; Milnes, Oliver, Dillon, and Duckworth, three-quarter backs ; Daniels and Boggles, half backs ; Balmforth, Calvert, Wilson, Lupton, Nickson, and Moseley, forwards. Referee, Mr J W Whiteley, Wakefield. ST. HELENS v. EBBW VALE.</p>
        <p>The return League game only attracted a moderate attendance to the St. Helens ground. For the first quarter there was very little to choose between the respective sides, until Trenwitb slipped away and banded to Greenwood, who ran over tor Turtill to goal St. Helens burst away, und Saunders paved the way for Prescott, Turtill failing. With the exception of several spasmodic bursts by Ebbw Vale,St. Helens monopolised</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>ALTRINCHAM DISTRICT LEAGUE. Fl RST DIVISION. RESULTS TO SATURDAY. Goals.</p>
        <p>0 C HTRING TON PARK V. MOBBERLEY.</p>
        <p>At Hea-tley. Play opened fast and ,even there being very little to choose between the teams. Result : Mobberley, 4; Oughtrington Park, 3.</p>
        <p>ALTRINCHAM LADS V. LYNIINt. At Stamford Park. At the commencement Lymm were the smarter team and after a capital run by Owen, Percy Webb op, nod their account and Burns got a second. Half-time : Lymm two, Altrincham Lads one. The first twenty minutes of the 8.-...cond portion were fairly even, several opportunities being thrown away by both sides. When about 15 minutes to go Ross put in a capital centre for Walker to equalize. Thus encouraged, the Lads began to have the major portion of the game and no surprise was evinced when Bagnall gave his side the lead with a capital shot. From now till the close Lymm were a beaten team time arriving with the score, Altrincham Lads three, Lymm two. OUGIETRINGTON AMATEURS V. SALE ALMON. •</p>
        <p>After gradually settling down the Amateurs made several good attempts and scored through H Hoult. Still having the major portion of the game, Drink- water increased their total to twp. Sale Albion on several occasions broke away by aid of their left wing hut were held well in check. Half-time: Oughtrington Amateurs, two ; Sale Albion, nil. A very interesting game ended : Oughtrington Amateurs, four ; Sale Albion. one.</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD V. PENKETH.</p>
        <p>- ------- At Latchford. These teams met in their return engagement. At their previous moulting at Periketh Latchford won very easily. The most noticeable feature about Saturday's game was the improved form shown by Penketh. They gave one of their best exhibi- tions this season, and in the end Latchford only gained the verdict with the score Latchford, two ; Penketb, one.</p>
        <p>SECOND DIVISION. LATCHFORD RECREATION V. LATCHFORD CHRIST CHURCH.</p>
        <p>On the ground of the former. The home team won the toss and took advantage of what breeze there was They were the first to press. The Church defence was sound. After clever work by their forwards the Recs. opened their account following with a second shortly after. Still holding the balance of the play the Recreation increased their leid to three goals before the interval. The second half showed the Churchmen in a better light but the home defence was equal to all demands until after one hour's play when the Church- men reduced the lead. Straight from the kick-off the Recreation scored again and were once more three in front, whilst just on time they added a fifth'and ran out winners of a fast and exceedingly clear game with the score : Latchford Recreation, five goals; Lstchford Christ Cutirch, one goal. WARRINGTON MEDAL COMPETITION. ST. BARNARAS' JUNIORS V. SACRED HEART.</p>
        <p>The above teams met on the first round of the Junior Medal Competition at Lovely-lane on Satu-day. In the opening stages both goals had narrow escapes. The visitor's right wing broke away and from Leah's centre Walker scored. St. Barnabaa' were the next to press and Callaghan bad several shots to stop. Syres handled within the area and the home team were awarded a penalty. Spruce took the kick and equalised. Shortly afterwards Spruce kicked through his own goal. Interval: Sacred Heart, two goals; St. Barnabas', one goal. During the second half the visitors were continually on the defence, From a centre by Oxley, Forbes headed through and again placed matters even. Sacred Heart played hard and forced a fruitless corner. On the general ran of the play Sacred Heart were very fortunate to make a draw. Result : St. Barnabas' two goals ; Sacred Heart, two goals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>DRAW FOR SECOND ROUND.</head>
        <p>DRAW FOR SECOND ROUND.</p>
        <p>Winwick or Moore v St. Barnabas' or Sacred He- -t referee, G Lang. Bank Quay Rees v " Guardian " Apprentices ; G Tomlinson. Newton Farm School or Orford Athletic v Orford St. John's ; J J Wilson. Farnworth Wesley Guild, a bye. To be played on the ground of the first-named on January 27th.</p>
        <p>ARCIIBISHOP AND THE M.P.'S. C 57 OF THEM IGNORANT_ OF EDUCATION'S</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>INS AND OUTS.</head>
        <p>INS AND OUTS.</p>
        <p>The Archbishop of Canterbury, speaking at Croydon on Monday, said that crass ignorance prevailed upon the subject of education in most circles, and most of all in the House of Commons ; to give a generous estimate, thirteen men in the House of Commons understood the subject, and 657 knew practically nothing about it. They did not know its ins and outs or the practical requirements of little children. In this controversy it was vital to understand not only the cause they wished to promote, but what were the difficulties and the obstacles legiti% mately felt by other people.</p>
        <p>lirement, 'vital to</p>
        <p>On the other side, a man of the type of Lord Mac Donnell holds that fiscal union is the one security for Ireland against an impoverished Government. But here as elsewhere an examination of first principles will aid clear thinking. An autonomous Government to the advocates of fiscal union is simply" an agency by which the adminis- tration of the United Kingdom carried out the policy of the Imperial Parliament in Ireland. 'The fiscal union is needed, accord- ing to these critics, to maintain our standard of living and our standard of administration. "Standard " here means of course a money standard. That we could have as full a life in Ireland and a far more efficient adminis- tration for less money than is spent on life and government in wealthy industrial England is a thought which the mind of this kind of critic refuses to harbour. Fiscal liberty, moreover, its opponents say, is a first step towards separation. We should be stupid enough, so it would seem, to quarrel with our best customer. And, at any rate, we are further admonished, we must not merely consider what is best for Ireland, but we must subject our consideration of our " best " to the principle of that most curious tag of history the Act of Union. That what is best for Ireland should also be best absolutely for England is an idea that great English statesmen have entertained in the paTA, and the bogey of a separation (as mad as any human thought can well be) will now be permitted to intimidate Liberal statemen from following out their own traditions.</p>
        <p>GLADSTONE'S PROPHECY. Looking over the Irish finance and its main broad outlines, one plain out- standing feature cannot fail to weigh with an English student, and that is the fulfilment of the Gladstonian prophecy that the Unionist Government would prove irreconcilable with economical administration in Ireland. The first Home Rule Bill was defeated by the Unionist asser- tion that the land scheme with which it was accompanied would involve British credit to the tune of £180,000,000. Bass of that day won Derby at the election by a poster repre- senting the train required to carry the gold to buy out Irish landlords. But Unionism has done the very thing which it was a main charge against Mr. Gladstone that he intended, and has done it by a scheme deliberately designed to inflate artificially the price of Irish land. Home Rule, genuine Home Rule, is a policy that has economy on its side. The fuller it is the greater will be the security of the British taxpayer against an increase of his losses. The present system advantages nobody and nothing in Ireland but officialism, which grows with the rapidity of a fungus, and having consumed all the revenues of Ireland now threatens England too.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>HOME RULE FINANCE.</head>
        <p>HOME RULE FINANCE.</p>
        <p>As might be expected, the recent dis- cussions on the financi 11 aspect of Home Rule have revealed a considerable measure of disagreement among the experts. We feel no surprise at this. The fiscal Union has produced some wonderful results during the last eight years and these results have not rendered any easier the task of creating a responsible Irish Government. The British taxpayer has seen his revenue out of Ireland disappear altogether and a growing deficit take the place of his former comfort- able profit. On the other hand, the Irish Nationalist and the reforming_ statesman are confronted by the fact that what was a comparatively simple sum of addition and subtraction for Mr. Gladstone has become much more complicated by the extravagant consequences of the legislation passed since 1902. The advocates of fiscal autonomy are not affrighted by those' difficulties. They believe that, time being given, the economies possible upon the, present inflated expenditure on Irish Govern- ment will affect the necessary balance between Irish income and Irish outlay and that there is little security for the Irish taxpayer or a rational finance system as long as Ireland is budgeted for indiscriminately with Great Britain. They think that genuine Home Rule can be madc ultimately to pay both Ireland and Great Britain. Liberty to them seems to be as good an investment for Ireland as a system of doles that entails the subordination of Ireland's industries and commerce to fiscal policies in which these interests cannot receive the smallest possible consideration. They do not like the risk involved to Irish material pro- gress in the policy of British Chancellors— whether of the Lloyd George school or Austen Chamberlain school. FISCAL LIBERTY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>STAGE THUNDER AT BELFAST.</head>
        <p>STAGE THUNDER AT BELFAST.</p>
        <p>We had intended to leave space to talk about the latest move of Carson, Craig and Co. in their bewildering resolution to prevent the Churchill meeting in Belfast. Finance is better than force and so the space is not available for this last piece of insanity. What an extraordinary cause it must be surely when its adherents seriously and publicly move the suppression by force of those who differ from its tenets? But the corner boys who have already made themselves ludicrous in the extreme before all fair minded men will find that this is not the day of the penal code when the few descendants in Ireland had their own way in all its tyranny, and they may wake up in time to discover that their stage thunder and traitorous clamouring is helping on the sane gospel of Irish self-government. We should have imagined Sir Edward Carson to have been shrewd enough to have drawn a distinction between even backward Ireland and Cossack-governed. Russia.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>SOCIALIST VICTORIES AND THE FARMER.</head>
        <p>SOCIALIST VICTORIES AND THE FARMER.</p>
        <p>What the vast increase in the Garman .9ocialistic vote at the General Election may mean in the world of politics we leave others to determine ; but, obviously, it is likely to have a considerable effect on English agriculture. The FSocialists in Germany are largely made up of the industrial populations which have , grown from the devolopment of German commerce. llf they are set on one thing more than another, it is to have access to the markets of the world, in order to procure food more cheaply. They have done this in regard to poultry and eggs, with the result that these commodities have risen in price all over the world. They are dertermined 8180 to obtain meat more cheaply, and, no doubt, the Government will be obliged to gratify them in this respect, especially if they are balked in some of their other aspirations. German competition in the cheap markets of the world will very soon make them dearer, and it is therefore by a very plain train of reasoning that we arrive at the conclusion that the success of the socialists in Germany is likely to have the effcct of raising the prices of agricultural produce in Guilt Britian.— Country Life."</p>
        <p>• RESERVOIR OVERFLOWS. All work on the Stockport Corporation waterworks at Kinder was rendered impossible by the tierce gale and blinding snow on Wednesday. It was found impossible for men to cross the embankment. Several essayed to do so, but were driven back by the blizzard. The heavy rains preceding tke snow had the effect of completely filling the reservoir, with the result that late on Tuesday night it Lbegan to overflow, and the 'following afternoon there had been an overflow of 12 million gallons.</p>
        <p>PA DG ATE SCHOOLS. The CLERK read a report ty Mr. Young, }LM. Inspector of Schools, after visits to the Padgate Schools on October 29th and November 14th and 12th. It pointed out that the educational difficulties of the institution were many. After making due allowances for those, it was a matter for regret that so few children reached the top of the school where the teaching was the most intelligent and the syllabus most liberally interpreted. While habits ot steady industry were formed, it nJuld not be said that much enthusiasm was displayed by the children in their work or that their deficiencies were overcome with any great success. The periodical examinations, which were carefully conducted by the superintendent, had revealed regular. though slow progress in the children's attain- ments and both the records of the teachers' lessons and the lessons themselves showed that both teachers and scholars worked with dogged perseverance and patience. Several suggestions were made and the I report concluded by stating that in view of the fact that very few of the boys subsequently adopted the trade in which they were being trained, if that training was to be of any lasting value, constant and varied opportunities should be aff.rded them for experimental work. The report was referred to the Cottage Homes Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
