      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS</p>
        <p>The Accommodation for Lunatic and Phthisical Cases.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>STRONG CRITICISMS.</head>
        <p>STRONG CRITICISMS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held at the Workhouse on Wednesday, Mr G Mather presiding. There were also present : Mrs Simpson, Rev A M Mitchell, Messrs E L Bent. A J Jolley, A Brooks, James Evans, W Houghton, J A Rushworth, W C Brooks,W Farness, R Lee, J A Hughes, W Flanagan, NI W Seton, A Bottomley (clerk), R W Peacock (deputy clerk). REV. A. M. MITCHELL'S PROTESTS. .</p>
        <p>When the minutes of the last meeting were submitted the Rev A M Mitchell complained that there was no reference to his protest against the contemplated large expenditure on the enlargement of the kitchen and dia- ing hall. The CLERK said he could not enter any protest on the minutes unless it was the desire of the Board. They were the Board's minutes and whatever decision was arrived at, it was his duty to record it. If any member desired to make a protest,it was only with the authority of the Board that it could be entered on the minutes. Mr. A. Baooxs : There is another omisssion. You have not recorded the unwarrantable suegevtion of inaccuracy on the part of the porter made by Mr. Mitchell at the last meeting. The CHAIRMAN : Order, order. Next business. The Bev. A. M. MITCHRLL: There are some omissions in the time tible.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>AN INFIRMARY INCIDENT.</head>
        <p>AN INFIRMARY INCIDENT.</p>
        <p>At a meetini-o'f—t-h;TIO-u-se—Co-uliiiteo the Clerk reported that a min had appeared before the Newton Relief Committee and complained that on the 30th ult., he was roughly handled by the hospital porter in the workhouse grounds whilst paying a visit to his wife who was a patient in the Union Infirmary. A report (written) by Sister Ward in relation to this matter was read to the committee and hospital porter Burke related the circum- stances which led to the man having to be forcibly ejected from the Itfirmary. After conisdering the matter very carefully, it was moved by Mr. JOLLEY, seconded by Mrs. SnuPsoN and recommended that, having investigated the circumstances of the alleged assault, the committee were satilflad that such violence as was used was justifiable. Mr. SETON said the charge was a serious one to make against the Guardians and he hoped publicity would be given to the committee's finding.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>PRESENT._</head>
        <p>A PRESENT._</p>
        <p>The hearty thanks of the Board was accorded to Mr. W. C. Brooks for his gift of a gramphone for the enter- tainment of the inmates.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>IN:31 ATE 3 AND PENSIONS.</head>
        <p>IN:31 ATE 3 AND PENSIONS.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN read a report that the number of persons of the age of seventy and upwards admitted to the Workhouse from January Ist, 1910, to December 31st, 1510, was miles 50, femtles 18. For 1911 the figures were : males 57, females 3l—an increase of seven males and 16 females. Mr. A. BROOKS : That is in spite of the old age pension. The CHAIRMAN : Yes, and in spite of the concession made to recipients of out-relief. The Rev. A. M. MITCHELL : It shows that the pension is not sufficient. It should be increased to 7560. Later the CIIAIRMAN said he was informed that 22 inmates left the Workhouse in 1911 to take up old age pensions and eleven (7 men and 4 women) returned. Mr. A. BttooKs : If you are going into the matter of the effect of old age pensions, would it not be well to get the number over 7Q who arrived in the Workhouse in the year 1903 ? Undoubtedly the prospect of the pension would begin to influence the coming into the Workhouse of people over 70 early in June 1909. I know cases that did not seek Poor Law relief because of the disqualification in 1910. If we are to have some- thing like the true effect of old age pensions we ought Ito begin with January Ist, 1908. Then 1 think we could form a fairly intelligent idea of what their effect has been on the number of inmates. The CHAIRMAN : I will try to get that. A QUESTION Or VACCINATION. The National Anti-Vaccination League wrote on the question of payment for Workhouse vaccinations and rtquested that when next filling a vac tncy for Work- house Medical Officer, the Board should arrange that he be paid a salary which should include the performance of any vaccination at the Workhouse or in the Infirmary. The CLERK said the question did not appeal to the Board at the present time, bezause they were not filling up any appointment. The Rev. A. M. MITCHELL : At the present time Dr. Taylor (resident assistant medical officer) gets nothing for vaccination. Ile vaccinates but Dr. Hodgkinson (visiting medical officer) takes the fees. Is not that so? I think the man who does the work ought to get the money. The CLERK : In defence of Dr. flodgkinson, I must state that I am quite satisfied that Mr. Mitchell is not correct in his statement. The Rev. A. M. MircliEm. : Is not Dr. Hodgkinson your medical officer and does he not take the fees for vaccination? The CLERK : And he does the work. Tbe Rev. A. M. MITCHELL : I understood that Dr. Taylor vaccinated. Will you go 83 far as to say he does not ? The CLERK : I say that Dr. Hodgkinson vaccinates at the Workhouse and I do not think there is a single case since Dr. Taylor has been here where he has I performed the vaccination, unless it has been when Dr. Hodgkinson has been away. I think I can say that he has not vaccinated three cases. The Rev. A. M. MiTcRELL : You are only assuming. In any case, it is quite evident that Dr. Taylor does act as deputy. What lam anxious to know is whether or not vaccination is not more generally practised by the resident doctor than you imagine. The CHAIRMAN : I take it this way—that Dr. Taylor is assistant to Dr. Hodekinson, exactly as a curate is assistant to the vicar. If the curate takes a funeral he takes it for the vicar and the vicar gets the fee. The Rev. A. M. MITCHELL: That is precisely my point. The vicar takes the lee and the curate does the work-. . . .</p>
        <p>The CLERK: It may be so in the case of a vicar, but Mr. Mitchell's information is altogether wrong. The Bev. A. M. MITCHELL : I am asking for information. Mr. JAMES EVAKS: I don't think Mr. Mitchell is asking for information. He is making statements and ho very handily takes up the clerk for a surmise. I have not heard anything from Mr. Mitchell this morn- ing that has not been on the same basis—surmising from a report from somebody that that is so. May I ask Mr. Mitchell what evidence he has of the statement</p>
        <p>_ being true? The Rev. A. M. MITCHELL: Dr. Taylor attends as a rule at the House Committee meetings and gives the eports about vaccination. If be were not conversant</p>
        <p>11l r. W. C. BROOKS said the policy of "wait and see " might suit some cases, but if it were adopted in this matter it would end in disaster. It was their duty as Guardians to see that the cases referred to were properly housed. There were 23 phthisical cases accommodated under all kinds of conditions that were not satisfactory. Mr FURNESS said be was under the impression that a report had already been submitted to the Board. The CLERK said the question was considered some time ago but the Board dec;ded to defer it until the new infirm block was completed. Then came the question of a conference with a view to joint action being taken for the provision of accommodation for that class of patient. The Building Committee had also in hand the alterations at the Workhouse and the question of heating and hot water supply. They thought it would be better to delay any new scheme until some of the work was got out of band. Mr. FURSKSS : 1 believe you have had a report from the Loeal Government Board with reference to the conditions.</p>
        <p>The CLERK From the Luna 37 Commissioners, and it was the Board's decision that the matter should be considered %hen the other work was in hand.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. C. BROOKS : The report was a strong one and if all the members got it they would understand the conditions as seen through the spectacles of an out- sider.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN: You don't put a great deal of stress on that, do you ? They are paid for making these reports. They don't make them as light as they can.</p>
        <p>Mr. JOLLEY said Mr. Brooks had raised the matter over and over again during the last few years, but nothing had been done, beyond referring it to corn- mitces. It was understood that the Building Committee would report at an early date, but it had not met since it was known that a conference of Lancashire Unions might touch upon the ques- tion and make suggestions which would prevent the necessity of the Board embarking upon a costly scheme. He shared Mr. Brooks's scepticism on that point. He did not think anything was going to result from that line of action. He had, however, for several years taken the line that they should wait, because he felt very strongly that that class of cases ought to be dealt with by representatives of a wider area than the Unicn. There scorned a probability three or four years ago that as an outcome of the Royal Commission on the Poor Law, Boards of Guardians might be abolished and some more comprehensive methods , adopted of dealing with the problem of the treatment of the insane. But it did not look as if anything of that kind was going to happen. The Guardians wore charged with the duty of looking after the insane. That was one side of their duty which they had not discharged properly for several years. There was no doubt whatever that the least satisfactory side of their work was the treatment of the insane. Mr Brooks was not exaggerating in any way the conditions under which some of the insane cases were treated and the injury that resulted occasionally in the hospital to other cases, from the fact that dangerous and almost violent lunatics, for whom they had not been able to get prompt removal tct asylums,were detained in the ordinary hospital ward and caused annoyance and even serious injury to the ordinary type of hospital patient. The questiou was a big ono and would have to be dealt with on bold lines which while involving expense, would, be ventured to think, lead to a very marked improvement of the conditions which had been a disgrace to them as a Board for five or six years. It was simply shocking to think of the figures which had been 61151Ditted. They had not proper accommodation for thirty, let alone ninety certified patients. Mr. JAMES 'EVANS, in supporting the resolution, said he did not think the Board could consider two more important subjects. He would like to emphasise the necessity of dealing with phthisical cases, from the health point of view, as far as the town and the Union were concerned. Ile found there were cases which in all probability were a positive danger but there w.s a feeling of reluctance which was not to be wondered at ; patients wou:d not. seek admission to the workhouse, for the simple reason that there wereno sanatorium co mlitions there. The treat- ment of such cases should not be left altogether to the health authority. The Guardians ought to make such provision as would induce people to avail themselves of it. Mr. RUSIIWORTII, in supporting the resolution, said that when one entered the imbecile wtrd it appeared more like a dungeoa than anything else. The atmos- phere was very unpleasant and the Board ought at once to go into the question and give the inmates healthier and more pleasant conditions. There had been many complaints at different times and the question was as that could he considered. pointed out that on May 12th the report urTgehnetcarsEaßnic y of the Government Inspector was considered by the House Committee. After careful coasideration, the committee desired to state that the existing accom- modation was, in their opinion, insufficient and that suitable accommodation fcr , the male and female epileptics and iral soils patients was needed and should be provided. They recommended that the report be referred to the sub-committee for consideration and</p>
        <p>report. The CITAIRMAN : TS not the resolution we have before us superfluous? Mr. JOLLEY : No, for this reason, that it includes pbthisical patients. The resolution was unanimously agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <p>with what takes place and H he does not himself adrninster the vaccination. I don't think he is quite in order in appearing at the House Committee. The CHAIRMAN: This is really sickening. I\lr. JAMES EVANS : It is exceedingly weak. TiE CHAIRMAN: Nest business, Mr. Clerk. It is really sickening, this sort of thing.</p>
        <p>_ LUNATIC AND PIITFIISI-CAL CASES. STRONG CRITICISMS OF THE ACCOMMODATION. THE "LEAST SATISFACTORY" SIDE OF THE BOARDS</p>
        <p>WORK. Mr. W. C, Brooks moved a resolution to the effect that the question of lunatic and phthisical accommoda- tion be referred to the Building Committee for immediate oonsideration and report. The state of things that existed at present exiled f ,r strong comment and immediate attention. In 1936 the total number of certified patients (male and female) in the lunatic ward was 63. At present it was 99 an increase of 33 in five years. The accommodation had, however, remained the same and it must be obvi ius to everyone that it was not adequate in any shape or form. The male accommodation was a complete death trap while the females were scattered ,bout in various portions of the hospital. Phthisical patients and lunatics were mixed up in the hospital and they had raving lunatics' mild lunatics and epileptics against the bedsides of dying patients. It was the duty of the Board to see that an improvement was carried out immediately. The question of a site would be one for the committee to go into. A committee had been appointed to consider feeble-minded cases from the national standpoint and that Board sent two delegates. The Chairman at that time said the Board had better stay their hands to see what was the outcome of those deliberations.He did not know whether the delegates had attended any meetings. The CHAIRMAN: The first meeting will not take place until Tuesday next. Mr. W. C. BROOKS : I take it there will be a series of meetings and they will possibly take up another two or three years before there is any attempt to do any- thing. When anything is done, assuming we fall in with the idea—and in all probability we won't—it will only relieve us of the feeble-minded. It would not relieve us of lunatics, epileptics and those kind of cases. We should still be in the same position. There is also the question of the depreciation of the property, which is very dilapidated now. The CHAIRMAN suggested that Mr. Brooks should defer any further remarks until after the County meet- ing next Tuesday. The Building Committee would meet the following Friday and would have before them a report of the conference' . . _</p>
      </div>
