        <p>Retribution.</p>
        <p>TO COMMENCE IN</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, FEB. 3.</p>
        <p>We may look with some BLUSTER confidence for nothing but AND good results from the INTOLERANCE, methods adopted by the</p>
        <p>_ enemies of Home Rule in A Belfast. When common sense is plainly I thrown to the winds, and intolerance is en- throned as a god, and threats take the place of arguments, the cause which is served in such a temper is obviously on its last legs. The Ulster Unionist Council has de- cided that Lord Pirrie, and Mr. John Redmond, and Mr. Winston Churchill, and Mr. Joseph Devlin shall not be permitted to hold a meeting in the prosperous city which is the capital of Ulster. What this means is not at present quite certain. The Unionist Council has resolved to "take steps." What steps? This question is answered in different ways. One account is • that some thousands of the supporters of . the Council will fill up the hall which has been, or will be, engaged for the occasion. That may mean nothing but a strong and very disagreeable form of passive resistr .., 1 ance. It is what was done in many in- 4:* stances in the early days of the Boer War. There was shouting, and singing, and groan- -, ing, and no speaker could be heard. Some- times the platform was stormed; but, as a , rule, no harm was dohe to anything but free speech. The Orangemen may intend nothing worse than this. It is evident, however, that they are not all of one mind. Some of them talk loudly and excitedly about bloodshed; and probably there will be bloodshed if the , resolution of the Ulster Unionist Council is I acted upon. .</p>
        <p>Mr. Joseph Devlin is one of the Members for Belfast. He is forbidden by Lord Lon- donderry and Captain Craig and their sup- porters to address a meeting in the city which he represents in Parliament. Lord Pirrie is the main cause of the prosperity of the Belfast people. He is at the head of the most productive shipbuilding - firm in the world. He is to be prohibited by the Ulster Unionist Council from presiding over a meeting of citizens who owe most of their prosperity to his enetgy and capacity for business. It might be supposed from this that all the people in Belfast are of the same political persuasion; but in that case why take steps to prevent any meeting being held? It would be sufficient, surely, to listen to what was said, and then to vote down any resolutions that might be put. But Ulster is neither wholly Conservative nor wholly Prostestant. It sends Mr. Devlin to Parliament, as we have said, and one- third of its population is for Home Rule. Belfast is almost entirely Protestant in the distribution of employment on its public bodies. The Belfast Harbour Board has a salary list of .Cll,OOO, and it only employs one Catholic. The Belfast Corporation has a salary list of close on ,C 69,000 and it employs nine Catholics among its officials. Out of a salary list of ,ClO,OOO, the Belfast Poor-Law Board awards £45 a year to one Catholic official. These figures are a measure of the toleration, and the amount of comntOri fairness, that is to be found among the Unionists of Belfast, who are now strenu- ously preventing the extension- of Lord Pirrie's business, thus cutting off the nose to spite the face.</p>
        <p>Throughout all the Irish troubles we have uniformly advocated reliance on the ordinary law. We shall not cease to do so because of the bluster of Captain Craig and his friends; though it is clear that the ordinary law must be at a disadvantage in a city where the public bodies take politics and religion into consideration in the distribution of employ- ment. The police are in such a case very likely to be partisans. Nevertheless, we think the ordinary law will be sufficient in the conditions that may arise out of the resolution of the Ulster Unionist Council. It must be made clear that the law applies to Lord Londonderry and Sir edward Carson and Captain Craig to just the same extent as to any other of the King's subjects. They must be held responsible for the mis- chief they engender. We are not sure that the law against conspiracy ought not al- ready to have something to say to them'. But there need be no hurry about prosecu- tion. The most judicious thing will be to give these gentlemen sufficient rope; and they will infallibly hang their cause; even if they do no particular Itarm to themselves.</p>
        <p>At the Howley Ward Women's tea meeting cn Monday, Mrs. Crosfield testified to her keen interest in the work going on here, by a letter to the " chair " which gave a practical, eonamonsense epitome of present-day politics as view,2d by a woman's eyes. * * According to the " World " the season at Cannes is in full swing. Once more A. H. Crosfield has distinguished her- self in the lawn tennis tournament there. gith Prince Philip of Bourbon as partner, Mrs. Crosfield won the mixed doubles in two sets, 7_5, 7..5.</p>
        <p>* * * ; The oyster gourmand will doubtless appre- g elate the story of a visitor to a New York restaurant, who was provided with an oyster se large that it almost choked him when he • attempted to swallow it. "Ah ! " said the U• lniPathetic Irish waiter who helped to save el from asphyxia, "You're the sixth man " eS tried to swallow that and couldn't." * * • The Princess Royal, the Duke of Fife, and their daughters, who, have been recruiting after the unfortunate circumstances attend- their outward voyage to Egypt, are g°lng up the Nile. The Princess Royal is uoue the worse for her experiences, and soon • recovered from the chill which was the inevitable consequence of the exposure and !, drenching which she suffered at the time of • the wreck of the Delhi. * * g' There is said to be a little soreness between 4 the leaders of the Wesleyan body in Padgate ari d the Vicar of the parish, as the result of the burial of a well-known Wesleyan worker in the Parish Church graveyard last week. The rights of the Nonconformist body to take Part in the service form one of the chief Points at issue, and an explanation from either one side or the other seems to be required. * * * The second millionaire estate to pay duties In. the new year is that of the late Sir Wm. .C.r.suthhert Quilter (70), first Baronet, of 15awdsey Manor, Woodbridge, Suffolk, and • 1:1 South-street, Parklane, W., ex-M.P., formerly of the firm of Messrs. Quilter and .C°., stockbrokers. Sir Cuthbert Quilter 's a. 'Property is valued for probate at £1,220,639 • gr°se, and of this the net personalty amounts ; 'to £1,035,974, and his estate will pay in duties over £195,000.</p>
        <p>* The most interesting bequest is one to his _ • soh, Sir William Eley Cuthbert Quilter, now • ,second baronet and Unionist M.P. for West ?tiffolk, to whom, in addition to other oequests, he left " A further annuity of 2,000 for each year or part of a year during the first fifteen years after my death that he MaY be the representative in Parliament of, 0r the prospective candidate for, any division of the county of Suffolk," and he directed that t his trustees should be the sole judges as to 'Whether, and when, he might be considered a _Prospective candidate." The Forum," a prominent New York •'cLuarterly, recently published an article by 51r. Sydney Brooks on the Irish question, and the prospects of Home Rule. Mr. Brooks strikingly says that Great Britain has . .allowed her Irish policy "to be dictated bY the extremists of Ulster for the last time, The Power of Ulster Unionists to influence British opinion has progressively declined `during the last twenty years. That and the }louse of Lords are the two chief obstacles removed. The Irish mind during the last plvo decades had taken a novel and most epeful turn towards the concrete and con- structive. Ireland is more prosperous than 8,110 has ever been; and, difficult as the -411glo-Irish financial question will be, it has 'never been less difficult that now.' * * Even with strikes in existence, and a coal- , strike in the air, we fare better than our Staple fared before coal became the fuel. Wood was the chief commodity -In use, both in its natural state and as char- coal. Charcoal was prepared in the country • and suburbs of London and brought into th</p>
        <p>_ 'LINSEED} COMPOUND' for Coughs, Colds, and Bronchial troubles. 9id., Is. lid. 01 Chemists.—Adv</p>
        <p>Mr. Frank Richmond. On the completion of his indentbres with the Richmond Gas Stove and Meter Co., Ltd., Mr. Frank Richmond gays a farewell dinner at the Masonic Hall, Bold-street, on the 12th inst. The gatheting which included Mr. Ransome (managing director), and Mr. Slack (secretary), numbered about 70. A very enjoy- able dinner was served by Atkineon's Cafe, Limited. The toast of the "health and prosperity of the young host "was proposed by Mr. Hansonne, seconded by Mr. Slack, and supported by Messrs. Sher burn, Tickell, King and Mackay. In rising to respond Mr. Richmond was received with musical honours. He thanked his friends for the kind things which had been said of him and for their good wishes fir his future prosperity. He would never forget the kindness and courtesy which had been extended to Lim by all connected with the firm, during h's apprenticeship.—Mr. Richmond will be one of the representatives of the Richmond Gas Stove and Meter Company, and his career will be watched with interest. During the course of the evening an excellent musical programme was gone through. ANTHRACITE STOVES, Ecomonical, clean, cheer ful Price from 37/6.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, to 55, Bridge St. Warrington.—Advt. Professional Singer's Actl3n The hearing was resumed at a special sitting of the Warrington County Court yesterday, before his Honour Judge Stanger, K.C., of the claim of Miss Annie Felicia Sutton, professional vocalist, of War- rington, for 19s 5d., rent and rates alleged to be using by John Morton, Friars' Green House, for rooms sub-let to him at Trinity Chambers. Defendant counter-claimed £lO as damages for wrongful dis- turbance of the tenancy. The case for the plaintiff as stated by Mr. A. Browne was that a suite of rooms were taken by the plaintiff at Market Gate Chambers from Mr. T. J. Lee, draper, at a yearly rent subject to a month's notice from any time. in April 1909 Miss Sutton sub-let one of the rooms to Mr. Morton at :.£.lO per year and each to pay their portion of the rates and taxes. He was told by Miss Sutton that she was subject to a month's notice at any time, in view of any objection being raised by the other tenants on account of the singing. Notice was given to quit on December Ist, but defendant did not go until six days later. For the defence Mr. H. J. Longton contended that nothing was said at all about a month's notice. Miss Sutton had admitted:that she told defendant'that the rent was pay- able quarterly. The rent was paid quarterly and that was evidence of a quarterly tenancy. Mr. Morton in evidence said no mention was ever made to him about a month's notice.—His Honour said it was clear that Mr. Morton knew that there was a superior landlord and he must therefore have known as a business man, that Miss Sutton could not grant him a longer tenancy than that to which she was bound. He disallowed the counter-claim and gave judgment for the plaintiff for ..£4 10s. sd. Old Students' Reunion Former studatts of the Chester College held a reunion at Warrington on Saturday, The gathering was arranged in order to celebrate the fact that Chester College was founded in Warrington 73 years ago, and secondly for rurposes of srcial enjoyment. During the afternoon a visit was paid to the *Parish Church schools. It is interesting to mall that in that building a very largely-attended meeting, called by the Chester Diocesan Board of Education, was held onJanuary 25th, 1839, when it was decided to found a college at Chester. The Head Master (Mr. 0-. W. A. Brown) and Mr. Fred Daintith conducted the visitors over the school and pointed out traces of the apace opened in the floor of the upper room in order that speakers in the upper room could be heard by the assembly in the lower room. Forty-four former students afterwards sat down to tea at the Winmarleigb Cafe. Later in the evening the meeting was addressed by the Principal of the Collge, the Rev. R. A. Thomas, who related the circum- stances which led to the now historic meeting in Warrington, and also by the Rev. Canon Willis, a member of the College Committee, who briefly traced the history of pri.xiary education in this country during the last hundred years and referred especially to the activities of his two immediate pre- decessors as Rectors of Warrington, the Hon. and Rev. Horace Powys and the Rev. William Quekett. It WAS decided to form a branch of the Chester College Club for Warrington and district. Mr. El S. Smith, Beamont Council School, was appointed secretary, and Messrs. John Wright (Daresbury), Pettener (Winwick) Lindsay (Runcorn), Armstrong (St. Ann's), Daintith (Parish Church) and Ireland (Widnes) as the committee pro tem. A concert was then given, over which the Ps incipal presided. Mr. Ardern, general secretary of the club, and Mr. Lunt were the accompanists. The hest thanks of all are due to Mr. Armstrong for his very satisfactory arrangements.</p>
        <p>suite I</p>
        <p>Court 138, H Emmet mn Court 240, E Clayton and NV James Court 430, A Sankey Court 431, W Orrett Court 401, J Grayson Court 475, E A Jones repre- senting Ladies Court 9335, Peter Peacock District Treasurer, Robert Thornley astrict Secretary, atisisted by P.D.C.R. J Walsh, District Trustee, P.D.C.R.'s G Maybury, J Chrinoes and W Hipkiss, P.O 11's H Perdue and A R Baines. There were 25 new members and three members wives returned ; four Members died during the quarter, thus bringing up the total to 1218 members with over £3,651 District Funds. Various matters of great importance were discussed including the National Insurance Bill and the Special High Court to be held at Leicester on February 6th. It was unanimously decided that the Warringt m District be represented at the Special High Court, that Brother Thornley, district secretary be delegate. The meeting was favoured by a visit of two members of the Manchester High Court Committee, Brother G. Smith of Manchester, and Brother W. Latham of Earlestown. Both brethren made excellent speeches and expressed the hope that the splendid support which Warrington gave to Manchester in , securing the High Court for 1913 would be maintained I and continue to the end. S weral delegates took part in the discussion and promised to bring the matter prominently before their various courts. A very pleasant and profitable meeting was then brought to la, close by the D.C.R. formally closing the Court. Loyal Order of Ancient Shepherds (A.U.) Warrington District. This district held its quarterly meeting at the Ironworkers Home Lodge,Alexandra Vault 3, Scotland- road on Saturday last. The District Chief, Bro. T. S. Champion presided and he was supported by the Peputy Chief 8r,,, J. W. Pomfrey, P P,C.S. Bro. W. Taylor, District Treasurer, Bro. James Parr, and the District Secretary, Bro. Peter L, Edgar. The accounts for the quarter were presented by the Secretary and after the auditors had given their report they were passed. Bro. S. Blythe of Rose Bud Lodge was elected auditor for the next six. months. It was decided that the di-trict should be represented by two delegates at the special delegate meeting which is to be held in Manchester on the 8:h March next, and the , result of the ballot was the election of the District 'Secretary and Bro. L. Weir to be the delegates to each meeting. The Secretary presented the Annual Report and Statement of Accounts of the Lodges and District for 1911. Considerable financial gains had been made by many of the Lodges during the I past twelve months and the numerical strength of the district had also improved. —The District Examiners (Bros. L. Weir and David Harris) reported that they had made the usual examination of the books belonging to the various Lodges in the District, and had found everything in order. The meeting considered the recommendation from the Order Executive, that Districts should abolish entrance fees, and after some discussion the delegates unanimously agreed to adopt the suggestion. Bros. T. Adams, L. Weir, W. Ball and F. Massey were appointed along with the District Officers, to transact the business relating to the Convalescent Home Fund during the next twelve months Steps were also taken to form the necessary committee to carry out the District Bowling Main this year.—During the after- noon P.P.C.S. W. Miles, in well chosen words presented, on behalf of the District, to P P.C.S. Bro. Taylor, a beautiful clock, in recognition of his services to the District as Prov. Chief Shepherd during 1910-11. The presentation was warmly supported by Bros. Joseph Mather, L. Weir, F. Massey, Thomas Miller, Joseph Ashton, W. Peers, Joseph Adams, the District Chief, Treasurer and Secretary, and Bro. Taylor very suitably replied.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <p>The "fat crude" figures WANTED—A of the rrade Returns TARIFF mmnusi have so upset the P LEDGE! Tariff Reformers that they have preferred to say as little as possible about them. The trade figures have their corroboration, as an index of prosperity,, in the figures for I, unemployment. The percentage for the last Year is the lowest recorded since 1900. In 1910 the percentage of unemployment was 4.7, and in 1909 it was 77. The percentage , of unemployment for the whole of 1911 was 4 Per cent. Will Tariff Reformers pledge ' themselves to lower that figure if their Policy is adopted?</p>
        <p>The shipbuilding UNSCIENTIFIC figures for the last TARIFFS. year are stupendous. We built more vessels than the rest of the world combined. Indeed, last year we produced a tonnage of ship- building for foreign owners alone which fell little short of the total output of Germany and the United States combined. That is not so surprising when it is remem- bered that the Protectionist system, which • Tariff Reformers represent as the acme of scientific administration of tariffs, makes Shipbuilding much dearer for the tariff-blest countries than for those which welcome ibroign imports as raw materials that help to compete triumphantly with foreign Ting&amp;</p>
        <p>The" Times" publishes 110 W ENGINEERING a review of the Is "GOING." engineering trade, including shipbuilding, -in 1911, based upon official statistics and the reports of two hundred firms. A few Phrases from its own summary are worth quoting. "The most noteworthy instance 13f Prosperity regained is to be found among the shipbuilders, 'with a record of construc- tion: The output of steam locomotives from one firm is described as the highest on record • • • Progress continues to be made in the -aPPlication of electric driving to the running Tf mills and the operation of cranes, and there °as been a remarkable movement in this direction in the textile industries during the year. The trade in road vehicles has also established a record in demand in certain classes." The summary closes with the observation that "from first to last the Ports are singularly free from complaints .(3f foreign competition." The reports them- selves show that not only was 1911 an rcellent year,but that the firms look forward 10, and in many cases are indeed already assured, an excellent year in 1911. This is pretty good (says the Daily News) " for an Industry which Mr. Chamberlain marked f(3l°;ivn among the dying, which he said was eing annihilated by foreign competition and could be saved only by a tariff. One can ~e",fess to no surprise that the " Times" I;7lew is severely ignored by its Tariff eform contemporaries.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>The Examiner Saturday.</p>
        <p>City in carts, the chief marts for its sale being at Smithfield and on Cornhill. By art enactment of Richard 11. it was decreed that ' the price of charcoal should be fixed at ten- pence per quarter from Michaelmas to Easter, and eightpence per quarter from Easter to Michaelmas, the regulation of its sale—as well as that of firewood—being committed to the Mayor and Aldermen. Among the poorer folk fern, reeds and stubble were also used for fuel, and a common method of keeping warm was to sit over pots filled with hot-water.</p>
        <p>* In a recent publication the Board of Agriculture gives some interesting compara- tive statistics with regard to the agricultural holdings at June sth last in each administrative County of Great Britain. Yorkshire leads the way with 26,360 holdings in the West Riding and 12,989 in the North Riding. Lancashire comes next with a total of 19,406. The holdings are classified in under different heads and sizes, ranging from 1 to 5 acres, and 300 acres and over. The average size of the holdings is about 50 acres in Yorkshire, and 40 acres in Lancashire. In a purely agricultural county such as Berwick, the average size rises to 218 acres,but the number is only 550. The Isle of Man has 1819 hold- ings averaging 51 acres, while in Jersey and Guernsey, the average size falls, naturally, to 10 acres.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <p>rf Dr. Clifford, whom Mr. Lloyd-George has described as "the modern Cromwell," was overwhelmed with ;congratulations on Sun- day on the occasion of his golden wedding. Essentially a self-made man, he began life in a lace factory in the Midlands, when he was eleven years of age. His father was a village factory hand, and John Clifford was the eldest of a family of a six. As a boy he was employed as " jacker off" in the factory. "Many a time," he says," I went on working from Friday morning right on to Saturday evening without a break." When he was 18 he became a local preacher. At 22 he accepted a call to Praed-street Chapel, Paddington. All his spare time was spent in studying at University College, workinc, twelve to fourteen hours a day during the week and preaching on Sundays. Out of his stipend of .£lOO a year at Praed-street he spent £27 on his classes at college. * * "The liberty of the subject" will doubt- less be much to the fore at the weekly meet- ing of the Parliamentary Debating Society on Monday next, when on the suggestion of the Chief Constable (Mr. Martin Nicholls) the following resolution passed by the Association of Chambers of Commerce at a meeting held at Trinity College, Dublin, will be under discussion : " That in the opinion of this House it is desirable that the law should be amended and extended so as to pi ovide that any person brought before a court of summary jurisdiction and charged with having in his possession or on his premises with his knowledge any goods or things which may be reasonably ttuspected of having been stolen or unlawfully obtained and Who shall not satisfactorily account to the Court f,ir his possession of the same shall he liable on summary conviction to a penalty not exceeding 1.5, or in the discretion of the Court to be imprisoned with or without hard labour for any pet iod not exceed- ing two months, and that such goods shall ba retained in the hands of the police and be confis- cated, unless within a period of six years they are claimed by the true owner." Similar powers are conferred upon the police by local Acts in force in Liverpool, Glasgow, Salford, Oldham, and other places at the present time. The Warrington Chamber of Commerce has expressed itself in favour of the suggested amendment.</p>
        <p>* * Perhaps the fact that no particular measure was under discussion accounted for the very meagre attendance on the Government benches in the Parliamentary Debating Society on Tuesday evening. Wha- tever the cause, it was to be regretted that only five Liberal members saw fit to put in an appearance when an important statement of policy was being placed before the House. Councillor Hawthorn (Chancellor of the Exchequer) had sole possession of the front Bench and had to undertake the task of defending Government measures from attacks by a numerous Opposition. The admirable way in which he rose to the occasion and dealt with all kinds of criticisms was generally com- mented upon. The society has done a valuable work in the past but if it is to maintain its position there must be no relax- ing of interest and enthusiasm on the part of any section of the House. We hope that the Liberal members will make it a point of honour to see that the Government has a strong backing in its endeavours to carry much-needed measures of reform. There is I sufficient controversial matter in the pro- gramme outlined by Councillor Hawthorn to provide some capital debates, and now is the time for budding orators to win their spurs.</p>
        <p>A LOCAL PARS. ",••,••%^/•'‘^Nr%'`•/°\AWNe•</p>
        <p>Personal. Friends of Mr. Harry New, the Liberal agent, will be pleased to learn that his wife presented him with a daughter yesterday morning, and that both mother and child are " doing well." Failure of Electric Supply. A sudden failure of the electricity supply, which plunged the centre of the town in darkness for about five minutes, cccurred at 9.25 last night. The performance at the Hippodrome was in full swing at -the time and there was some excitement when the lights went out, but fortunately the audience kept their seats and any semblance of panic was avoided.</p>
        <p>The Plcturedrome. The high standard of merit associated with the entertainmects at this popular house is well maintained and large audiences are the rule. On Monday, Tuesday and Wednesday next, there will be a splendid pictorial representation of " East Lynne " and on the remaining days of the week the chief picture will be "Dad's Boy." Musical Society's Concert. The second subscl intim concert of the season will be held in the Parr Hall on Wednesday, January 31st., when the al tistes will be : —Vocalist, Miss Marie H ughton •, solo violin, Miss Helena Kontorovitch. The plan of reserved seats opens to subscribers to-day (Saturday) and to the general public on Wednesday next at Mr. H. H. Pearson's, Bridge-street.</p>
        <p>Death of an ex-Padgate Choirmaster. The death occurred on Wednesday of Mr. W Johnston, Fearnhead, who some 18 months ago relinquished the post of choirmaster at Padgate Wesleyan Church owing to ill-health. Mr. Johnston i was everywhere held n great esteem. He was employed at Messrs. Garnett's, Warrington. The funeral will take place at Padgate to-day. Property Sale. That the property market is not dead was amply proved on Thursday afternoon, when Mr. Frank Hickman offered for sale on the premises the leasehold dwellinghouse and premises 151, Forster-street, War- rington. The biddinz commenced at £OO and after spirited competition the property was knocked down to Mrs. Jones, of Dudley-street, for £l4o.—a remark- able price for property in a neighbourhood where rentals of similar houses are 4s. 63. per week. Mr. Thomas S. Steel, of Mill-street Chambers, acted as solicitor for the vendors.</p>
      </div>
