      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>LATE FOOTBALL. -- Sensational News About Frawley.</head>
        <p>LATE FOOTBALL. -- Sensational News About Frawley.</p>
        <p>We learned yesterday that instead of re-signing for the Warrington Club now that the official Colonial timr is over, Frawley has told the Warrington officials that he is going back to Australia. This is a distinct breach of the agreement by which Frawley was allowed to play for the Colonial team, and tha I news has fallen like a bombshell on the Warrington camp, as the player was selected this week and expected to turn out for Warrington to-day. - •</p>
        <p>The question of clearing the ground for to-day's match was considered by the Committee last night and a final decision left' for this morning. A great effort will be made to bring the match off. Thomas and Shugars are playing for Wales to-day. To-day's match at Warrington was cancelled last night.</p>
        <p>The shareholders of Messrs. Brunner, Mond and Co. the great chemical m:nufacturers, are to meet in Liverpool on the 25th inst. to consider a motion which the directors will submit authorising an increase in the nominal capital of the company to £5,000,000 by the creatien of new shares. It is explained that the nee -ssity for increasing the capital arises from the recent acquisiti an by the company of controlling interets in the businesses of Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Limited, so apmakers, Warrington, and William Gossage and Soas, Limited, soapmakers, Widnes, the purchase consideration for which has to be satisfied by the is-ue to the shareholders of those companies of 39,000 fully paid preference shares of £lO each and 325,000 fully paid ordinary shares of £1 each in Brunner, Mond, and Co. The directors are taking the opportunity afforded by this necessity to increase the capital to an amount more than is requirel to provide for the purpose above mentioned so as to continue to have a reserve of unissued capital for future con- tinrzencies. Briefly stated (says the " Manchester Guardian ") this means that Brunner, Mood, and Co, are creating shares of the nominal value of £715,000 for the purpose of acquiring controlling interests in the two soap firms and taking the opportunity of• increasing their authorised capital by £2,000,000. The amount actually issu el at the present time is £2,789,650, of 'which £1,002,870 is in £1 ordinary shares and £826,780 in £lO preference shares, bearing 7 p?r cent. interest. The margin of £210,350 bets een the authorised and the issued capital added to the £2,000,000 extra which it is proposed to authorise gives 12,210,350, which will leave £1,495,350 for future contingencies after the soapworks purchase has been provided for. The shares for the balance of £1,495,350 will, of conrse, command a considerable' premium if they are ever issued, and it may be taken that if the neoessary resolution is passed the company will have at call well over £2,000,000 in the event of a profitable deal becoming possible. It does not follow that anything will be d ne in the immeliata future, but the financial world will at any rate be interested in watching for signs of possihilities. The £715.000 mentioned looks rather a low price for the property acquired, but the shares for that amount have a market vales of about £2,252,000, the 39,000 preference, at £l6 10s., being worth £643,500, and the 325,000 ordinary, at 99a., worth £1,608,750- The paid-up capital of Messrs. Crosfield is £615,508, and there is also debenture stock for £290,000. The company, however, is a private one, and it is possible that these figures do not bear a very close relation to the value of the concern. Messrs. Gossage and Sons publish no particulars whatever of their share capital arrangements, and it is only possible to form a very rough guess of the amount they are receiving for their business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>MAGISTRATES AND PUBLIC HOUSES. IMPORTANT MEETING. DRASTIC CHANGES OF REGULATIONS.</head>
        <p>MAGISTRATES AND PUBLIC HOUSES. IMPORTANT MEETING. DRASTIC CHANGES OF REGULATIONS.</p>
        <p>An important meeting of Warrington magistrates was held yesterday afternoon, when the following drastic changes in hours of closing, serving of women, Sunday hours, etc., were carried. The new regulations are based on the recommendations of the Liver- pool magistrates, following their experience in the railway strike :</p>
        <p>That the opening hour of licensed premises be altered from 6 a.m. to 8-30 a.m. That no woman, or person under 16, should be served with intoxicating liquor for consumption either " on " or " off " the premises, before 12 noon. That the closing hour of licensed premises on week days be altered from 11 p.m. to 10 p.m. That the hours of opening on Sundays be limited to three between noon and 10 p.m., but so that one of such hours be between 12 noon and 3 p.m. That clubs be put on the same footing as licensed premises as to the hours during which intoxicating liquor can be supplied with similar power to the Licensing Authority to'grant special exemptions. That the basis for the license duty be the quantity of intoxicating liquor sold.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>BRUNNER, MONO &amp; CO.</head>
        <p>BRUNNER, MONO &amp; CO.</p>
        <p>NEW CAPITAL FOR THE SOAP- WORKS PURCHASES.</p>
        <p>LARGE MARGIN FOR FUTURE DEALS.</p>
        <p>CROSS COUNTRY RUNNING.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON A.G. V. RUNCORN TALLY HO. This interclub race took place from the headquarters of the local club who mustered 17 against 19 from Rimoorn. Trail was laid by J Battersby and G W Hunt, over a two lap course which Nv4s intended to be nearly eight miles but a necessary detour, to avoid land not desirable, extended the distance to ahout nine miles of fair going. The locals arranged to decide the final stage of their senior points competition on the result of this run and a very bot pace was set from the start by H Holbrook followed by A Siddall, F Shadwell and J Parsonage. At the half distance Holbro air hal a substantial lead ' from F Antrobus as his nearest attendant with the others close up but Antrobus not being in "condition " dropped back in the final stages, leaving the West Lancashire Champion to completo the course about 100 yards in front of Siddall ; a similar distance off Shadwell had a like advantage over Parsonage, who beat W Bolton by about twenty yards for fourth place; the brothers Antrobus were followed by Thcrniley, Scott, and Turton, all of the home team, before J Claike, who was the first visitor to finish. _ _</p>
        <p>, WARRINGTON. RUNCORN. El Holbrook 1 J Clarke A Siddall ", T Jones l3 F Shadwell _. .. 17 _..</p>
        <p>J Parsonage 4 J W Moodlaid lB W Bolton 5 G Thomas l9 F Antrobus 98</p>
        <p>21 Runcorn were without several or their counters an, although the distauce was further than they are accustomed to: 77 points is a rather heavy reverse. C Voso hopes to resume training shortly shortly, but his enforced absence left the points competition more opea. Result :-1, J Parsonage, 41 points ; 2, F Shad well, 37 ; 3, A Siddall, 36.</p>
        <p>RYLANDS W.R.C. HARRIERS V WARRINGTON A.C.</p>
        <p>26 novices, 13 from each club, ran from the Recrea- tion ground, Gorsey-lane, over a six mile course, with the follow:ng result : WARRINGTON. RVLANTIS. Points. Pict. H Hough S Holbrook 2 C A Harding G Summerfleld G Wakefield 6 \V Skelborn S Siddall</p>
        <p>20 25 A very good race in which six yards covered the first three home. S Siddall won the Warriugton A.C. novice medal. Itylands had t.e eleventh position through J liollington,and A Summerfield of Warrington was twelfth. \Varrington A.C. hold an ordinary run from their headquarters starting at 3 p.m. to-day, and evening runs at 7.30 p.m. on Tuesday and Thursday next week. Efforts are being made by Warrington A.C. to clear enough ground of the snow, to make a track on one of the Wilderbpool fields, to hold to-day's run, so that preparations for the West Lancashire Championships, to be held a fortnight hence, will not be unduly interfered with. All members are specially requested to attend. Championships arrangements in connection with the Northern C.C.A. meeting at Lofthouse Park, Wakefield, on the 17th of next month, will be made by the com- mittee at a meeting to be held at the Hotel de Ville, King-street, Leeds, at 6 p.m. on Wednesday next.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <p>..</p>
        <p>r MR. LLOYD-GEORGE ,INTERVIEWED. THE OPPOSITION TO THE INSURANCE BILL.</p>
        <p>The " Review of Reviews" for January contains an interview with the Right Hon. D. Lloyd-lieorge, M.P., by Mr. W. T. Stead, on the subject of the Insurance</p>
        <p>"What of the opposition to the Bill?" asked Mr. Stead. "The. opposition to the scheme proceeds almost entirely from the articulate classes—that is to say, from the people whom you meet at dinner, the people who write in newspapers' the people who have seats in the hoes', and, in general, those who have to contribute but who do not draw any benefits. Hence a great outcry, engineered for party purposes or to make a newspaper sensation. But I was very much impres4ml by the experience of my colleague, Mr. Birrell, the other day. He went down to Manchester and met at dinner the usual people whom a Minister of the Crown would meet at dinner—well- to-do people in good circumstances who looked more or less askance at a measure which placed increased expenditure upon them without any corresponding direct benefit to anyone except their employees. He found them tepid in their enthusiasm, to say the least, and if they were not actually hostile they were critical and unsympathetic. After dinner he went down to a great meeting in the Free Trade Hall. The great building was crowdcd to the ceiling. Mr. Birrell was somewhat depressed and somewhat anxious as to the popularity of the bill, and with some fear and trembling he aproached the subject by saying: 'Now we come to Mr. Lloyd George's Insurance Bill ' He was never more impressed in his life than when he found the whole audience spring tn its feet and cheer and cheer again. He had never witnessed such a general and pro- longed outburst of enthusiasm for I.ny measure. I think you will find it so throughout the country among the people who will benefit. There will, of course, be a great deal of friction, but the people who benefit will soon realise the advantages which the scheme secures for them, and after that you will hear little of unpopularity.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>ME MISTRESSES' AGITATION.</head>
        <p>ME MISTRESSES' AGITATION.</p>
        <p>" What about the servant girls' agitation ?" " That was the holloxvest bubble that has ever been pricked. I sas immensely impressed by the deputation of mistresses and servants which waited upon moon the subject. I have never had a deputation in my life which impressed me more with its directnes., its sincerity and its admirable common sense. There was not a superficial word spoken by any woman there. One lady made a speech of two minutes, in which she went right to the heart of the thing in a manner which commanded mSr" admiration and respect. Ihe servant girls were extraordinarily intelligent, and although some of them came primed against the bill, I had no difficulty what- ever, by a course of question and answer, in convincing them that in producing this measure we were looking after their oan interests, and the interest of the servants was to support the bill."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>RECEPTION OF THE LAW.</head>
        <p>THE RECEPTION OF THE LAW.</p>
        <p>" Do you expect much hostility to the bill when comes into operation ? " "No," said he; "what is going to be done is this; all the friendly societies are going to conduct a canvass of the country for the purpose of securing the greatest number of members. Each canvasser will become is trained missionary, as it were who will expound the advantages obtained under the Act in order to secure the adhesion of the greatest possible number, for the societies have a natural ambition to do as much busi- ness as possible and secure as many members as possible. When the first payments begin it is possible there may be a certain amount of friction, but after the scheme once gets under operatios—theTsame thing will happen with this as with old-age pensions. Toe people who opposei it will try to hide their record by the vehemence of their support that thy would never, never, never, no never! interfere with the benefits secured for the working classes of this country by the insurance scheme. You see," said Mr. Lloyd George, "what Mr. Disrae'i said was very true : There are two nations in every country, the rich and the poor. The rich are the articulate class, they command the means of making their complainta audible throughout the world ; the poor are condemned in silence. Any measure like this that is introduced provokes the outcry of the articu- late. It will be some time before we can ascertain the real sentiment of the people towards the measure, but I have no doubt as to the ultimate result." SPILL THERE'S MORE TO FOLLOW.</p>
        <p>In conclusion, Mr. Lloyd George said : "We have at last made a beginning ; we have started a campaign against the worst evils to be encountered, and we are going on. The campaign against tuberculosis is only one of a series of campaigns which will be taken up in due succession for the purpose of carrying out a great deal for the amelioration of the condition of the people of thiscouutry."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>NEW TREATMENT OF DEAFNESS. -- DEMONSTRATION OF NEW METHOD IN LIVERPOOL.</head>
        <p>NEW TREATMENT OF DEAFNESS. -- DEMONSTRATION OF NEW METHOD IN LIVERPOOL.</p>
        <p>Few disabilities to which human beings are liable bring in their train more discomfort and inconvenience than deafness, and it is no wonder, therefore, that its treatment has continually engaged the attention of those engaged in medical research work. One of the commonest causes of deafness is a catarrhal affection of the throat and nostrils. and the explanation appears to be that the three small bones which lie behind the drum of the ear, and which in a healthy condition transmit sounds by a process of vibra- tion, become clogged with secretions due to the disease. In this condition the vibration is imperfect and it may cease altogether, with the result that various degrees of deafness are produced in different cases. The usual treatment for : deafness due to this cause is spraying or syringing with lotions, but an entirely new method is now being largely used by medical men. It consists in the employment of an electrical instrument, constructed on the principle of the telephone, and also equipped with a tiny steel hammer. which by an ingenious arrangement is worked by electricity. and is made to perform mechanical massage in the neigh- bourhood of the ear. -</p>
        <p>The instrument is the invention of Mr. Philip V. Summer. of Glasgow, and it is known as the Alcou-Vibra Massage" equipment. Mr. Summer was set to work on this instru- ment by the remark of a deaf friend that he could always hear a voice over the telephone better than in ordinary conversation, and that the improvement continued for some minutes after using a telephone. It occurred to Mr. Summer that the explanation might he found in the vigorous vibratory sound movements set up by the telephone receiver, and this is the underlying principle of.the vibration part of his invention. A graded ear- piece, resembling a telephone receiver. but capable of ad- justment to suit individual cases.is applied to the ear, and when the electrical current is turned on the patient hears a buzzing noise like that which sometimes produces vigorous language during a long-distance telephone call. The noise can be increased or reduced by the touch of a lever, and its effect, if it acts on the small bones referred to, is to set up a tickling sensation in the ear. The massage, which is effected by the gently-regulated beats of the.Oammer work- ing at a speed of about 5000 strokes a minute, takes place at the back of the ear and along the line of the Eustachian tube. It is claimed by Mr. Summer that the vibration loosens and causes to move away the catarrhal adhesions. and that the massage stimulates the circulation and opens up for a free air passage the tube from the throat to the ear. Many medical men have confirmed these claims. . . _ .</p>
        <p>Mr. Summer is giving full demonstrations to anyone suffering from deafness, at 36 The Arcade, Lord Street. Liverpool.</p>
        <p>The Salvation Army. The popular Monday night musical meeting vvas presided over by Mr. P. Hickman when a portion of the Bold-street Wesleyan Church Choir paid a return visit and a most enjoyable evening was spent. The quartette party rendered several selections, while Mr. McArthur sang two Scotch melodies in fine style. Mr. Hickman was to the fore as humorist causing roars of laughter. Mr. Gray sang " Thora," and great applause was given to Mr. Jennings for rendering "The Village Blacksmith," and the Misses Whitmore, Woods and Lswton also took part. The arrangements were carried out by the officer in charge, Adjutant J. Con tts.</p>
      </div>
