      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <p>igham, Jwn character, was charged before Mr S Wright (in the chair) and other magistrates on Monday, with having stolen 3s 9;d from the pockets of a fellow lodger, lames Colley. The Chief Constable said that Colley had been living at the Rose lodging-house, Dial-street. On Saturday night he had been drin.ing, and when he rose t 3 retire for the night the change from one atmosphere to another seemed to overpower him. He was taken upstairs by the prisoner. Later on Highasa went to the room and said Colley would be Ii led 61. or else put out if ha got into bed with his clothes on. Alter saying that he stripped Colley of his clothing. Cilley fell on to th 3 floor and then Iligham left the room as quickly as possible. Next morning Colley discovered that 3s 931 had been taken from his trousers. In the meantime the prisoner had cleared out. A man named Thomas Watson noticed that the prisoner had acted in a suspicious manner and communicated his doubts to Colley. In the afteraoon Iligham returned to the lodging house and was taxed with the theft which he denied and volunteered to go to the Police Station with Colley. The two set out together, but when in Battermarket-street Ingham ran away. Later in the day Detective Holland arrested the men who then had 6d. in his possession. Iligham denied the theft and said if it had not been for his previous character he would not have been charged. The Chief Constable said there were a large number of convictions against the prisoner. Higham was sent to gaol for a month's hard labour</p>
