      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>DISHONEST WORENIEN,</head>
        <p>DISHONEST WORENIEN,</p>
        <p>Arthur McVitty and Thomas McNamee, both labourers, of 16, Union-street, were charged at the Borough Court on Monday before the Mayor and other justices, with stealing between January 9 h and 12th, from some old property in Morris-court 801 b. of lead, value 95., the property of their employer. Bith men pleaded guilty. The Chief Constable explained that defendants were employed by a local builder, who had been at different times pulling down some houses in Morris-court. McVitty bad taken home qnantities of lead, the total weight of which was SOlb. On Friday night McNamee, who lodged with McVitty, obtained a handcart and they took the lead to another man, selling it for 3s. 6d. Their employer desired the Bench to take a lenient view of the case, the men having been employed by him for a number of years. Both defendants were fined 10s., including costs. Frederick Mullinder, a pedlar, of 42. Fennel-street, pleaded not guilty to receiving the lead on January 12th, knowing it to have been stolen. The Chief Constable said when the other defendants offered the lead for sale, they asked 4s. for it, and he offered them 3s. Gd., which they accepted. He never asked them any questions concerning the lead. When arrested be said, " I know too well what you want me for. I paid 7s. 6d. for it." He also said he did not know the men. McVitty, in evidence, said he lhad known Mullinder for some years as a rag gatherer. It was a lie for Mullinder to say he paid 7s. 61. for the lead. He never asked him the weickt or anything about it. Detective Metcalf spoke t 3 arresting the defendant, who, in answer to the charge, replied, " I did net know it was stolen. I gave 7s. 6d. for it." The defendant w ts fined 102, and costs. A LOQUACIOUS PRISONER.</p>
        <p>John Ferguson (28), of Warringtoo, was very loquacious when be appeared before Councillor Henry Roberts and other justices at the Warrington Police Court on Tuesday, charged on remand with having attempted to steal 4s. from a till in the shop of John Greenwood, 131, Sankey-street, on January Bth, and also with having stolen a piece of tripe from the shop of Frederick Greenwood, 12.5, Sankey-street. The prisoner pleaded guilty to the first charge and said he had no recollections of the second. The Chief Constable said prisoner went into Mr. Greenwood's shop and picket up a piece of tripe, value 11. The girl in charge saw Benson with the tripe in his hand and when she shouted to him he threw the tripe back int) the window. At 420 the same afternoon he went into the establishment of Mr. Greenwood, butcher, and attempted to rob the till. Mrs. Greenwood was in the kitchen. Hearing a noise as though so me- one was interfering with the till she hurried into the shop, where she saw Benson leaning over the counter. She taxed !aim with trying to steal money. This be denied and said he wanted to purchase some liver. Since the adjournment it was found that Benson had been in nearly every shop where there was a female in charge, cffaring to tell fortunes, either by cards or by the palm. Lucy Pickering, 138, Evelyn-street, who was in charge of the chip potato shop, said in her evidence that the prisoner was drunk. Prisoner : lam sorry lam placed in this position. It is the first time I have done such a thing, and I should never have dons it but for being under the influence of drink. My hands were as clean as driven snow until this occurred. lam 28 years of age and never knew what it watt to take anything before. I was under the influence of drink at the time. That is the only thing I have got to blame for it. The Chief Constable said the man's real name was Ferguson. Ile had teen convicted a drunkenness at Wigan and Preston. Ile once bad a good home, but he had been of such a character that his relatives had to turn him out. . _ . . .</p>
        <p>Benson further told the Bench that-he had been a timekeeper, an invoice clerk and a telephone clerk. He last worked with an uncle with whom he had a dispute, in consequence of which he left. Latterly he had been following faire. The Chairmen said the Bench would give the prisoner another opportunity. He would be committed to gsol for seam days, commencing from the tima of Lis arrest which meant thst he would be allowed to go on the rising of the Court. The convizition, however, would be recorded against him, and ho had better be careful in future. DID..IIE INTEND TO COMMIT SUICIDE.</p>
        <p>A peculiar case was beard at the Warrington Police Court on Monday, before the Mayor and other magis- trates, when Andrew Shimmin, watch repairer, 4,Church- street, was charged en remand with having attempted to commit suicide on December 6th. He was also charged with stealing 2s 51 from a slot gas meter and with havine. unlawfully pawned a watch. Ihe prisoner pleaded gnilty to all the charges. According to the Chief Constable's statement, Shimmin was in the Borough Ares public-house on December Ott). He got up with the remark that he w.. 8 going to poison himself. In his pocket he had a bottle, ‘vhich apparently contained poison. A witness informed the eolice with the result that an officer found the man in his house, frothing at the mouth. Shimmin admitted that he had taken nitric acid. He was conveyel to the infirmary, and was subsequently removed to the Work- house Hospital. Three weeks ago he was before the Court, and the miss was 7adjourned to give him 'tit opportunity of mending his ways. In the I Perim he anpeared to have been drinking. At 2.30 a.m. on January 2nd an officer was passing by 1, Caurch-street, when he heard someone shouting for help. When he gained admittance he sew Shimmin, who stated that he intended to "gas " himself. There was every indication that Shimmin had tried to suffocate himself with gas. He had cut the gas pipe, and by some means had robbed the meter of 80011. of gas. He had wrapped something round his head and had bad his mouth to the pipe. Whilst in gaol the doctor had had prisoner under observation and reported that he was of sound mind but was suffering from the effects of drink. During the time he had been on ramand efforts to assist him had been made, but the further inquiries were made the deeper Shimmin would appear to be in the mire. He had pawned watches which had been sent to him for repairs. Qaite a large number of charges could have been preferred against him. The watches had been pawned in different names. There was strong evidence to suggest that either his mind was unhinged or that he fully intended to commit suicide. In his accouy' book he had written some really fearful things and ' bade " Goodbye 'S to everybody a dozen times over. The Mayor said the very fact of Shirnmin calling attention to his attempts to suicide seemed to throw -doubt upon his intention to take his life. The Chief Constable said Mr. Firth, the police court missionary,bad promised to do his best for the prisoner. The Mayor in sending Shimmin to goal for three months in the second division said it seemed childish of him to pretend to attempt suicide over and over again. Borough Police Court. SATURDAY.—Before Messrs. A. J. Jolley (in the chair), T. Pemberton and J. Upson. MARKET TllEFT.—Hannah Davies, an elderly widow, of 18, Victoria-street, was charged with having stolen , from I a stall •in the market, a blouse, value 2i., the = property of Mary Connolly, 8, Ash-grove, Weaste, "Mar.chester. Two other charges of a similar nature were brought against the prisoner.—She was sentenced , to fourteen days imprisonment on each charge, the sentences to run concurrently. .TCESDAY.—Before Councillor Henry Roberts (in the chair), Mr. W. Richardson, Mr. J. R 3 mer Young and Councillor C. Jackson Holmes. SLOT GAS METER RonnEuv.—James McMahon, of Dakin's-yard, admitted having stolen 2s. sd. from a slot gas meter and also to having stolen a hammer, the property of Henry Fairclougb, from the works of Messrs. Crosfield and Sons. Ile pleaded not guilty to a third charge of assault and after hearing evidence this part of the count against him was withdrawn.—The Chief Constable said that prisoner had stolen the hammer from Messrs. Crosfleld's works some time during the last six months, and with this he bad broken open the slot gas meter in the house No. 15. Dakin's yard, doing damage to the extent of lOs 6s! and stealing 2e 5d in cash. The robbery was committed on January 2nd and</p>
        <p>accou* -4 11</p>
        <p>LINSEED COMPOUND' (Aniseed, Senegs, Squill, Tolu, to.) For Coughs and Colds, Influenza, Sul—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>_ , 7464 Z4-‘ .010` •</head>
        <p>_ , 7464 Z4-‘ .010` •</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, January 20, 1912.</p>
        <p>McMahon left the district, but surrendered to the police at Settle.—Prisoner said he had got into trouble all through having too much drink, lie was willing to refund the stolen money if he could get work.— McMahon was sent to gaol for a month's hard labour on each charge, the two charges to run concurrently. FRlDAY.—Before the Mayor (in the chair), Aldermen Smethurst, Burton and Pierpoint, Messrs S Wright, W Furness, G Pilling, T Locker, and H Thornton. VIOLENT PRlSONER.—Jeremiah Murphy, of no settled' abode, was sentenced ta fourteen days' imprisonment for having been drunk and disorderly in Horsernarket- street on Thurday night and with having wilfully broken three panes of glass in the cells at the Police Station, doing damage to the amount of is. 6d.—When prisoner was in the cells P.C. Plumb heard a smashing of glass and found that prisoner had smashed three panes with a boot which he had in his hand.</p>
        <p>CRUELTY TO A HORSE —Frank Acton was charged with cruelty to a mare by working it whilst in an unfit state on Janunry 9th. His • employer, Robert Turner, Moss House Farm, Grappenhall, was summoned for causing the animal to be v.orked.—The Inspector said the mare was very lame and suffering from ring bones on both fore feet. It was not fit to work on hard roads or setts.—Turner admitted having told Acton to take the animal out.—The case against Aoton was dismissed and Turner was fined 103.</p>
        <p>a party with a programme whlch could commend itself so much to the working class as did that of the Liberal party. (Applause.) Mrs. Crosfleld's letter was a splendid statement of what had been attempted and achieved to a certain!degree, by the party. In deciding which party one should follow, an incident in which be was concerned, weighed very profoundly with him. Nine years ago he prided himself on seeing much good in the Tory party as well as in the Liberal party. Since then, one trusted one had cut one's wisdom teeth. (Laughter and applause). One day he met</p>
        <p>A LANCASHIRE COLLIER whom he knew to be a Liberal. "I chaffed him " con- tinued Mr. Cooper, "by saying Now you'll vote Tory, Aye, Mr. Cooper ' he said 'I dar but.' That was his dialect for saying Yes, I dare but vote Tory.' I said 'Why.' He said At my employment we have had order that we must vote Tory.' I said Whatif you have ? You know the ballot is secret.' He said Yes but, Mr. Cooper, I can't read. ' I said And what if you cannot ?' —and do you know that very week I was contradicted when in public, I declared that the ballot was secret— in Lancashire too? But I said Do you know that when you go to vote, if you cannot read, the polling booth must be cleared and the clerk himself show you how to vote and if he dares to declare how you have voted, then he can be handled'? Yes' he said ' but I can't trust him 1 and it's as much as my livelihood is worth.' I made further inquiries, got to know how the ballot papers would be made out, and approached my friend a second time. I said Now look here, when you get your ballot paper, such a name comes first and such a name comes second. The first name is Liberal, the second is Tory. Now, lam a Librral but when you get in there, vote which way you like and snap your fingers at everybody.' (Applause.) In case anybody should ask for a specifi3 instance, there is one." (Applause.) Continuing, the rev, gentlemen said that for a long time this country had been dominated by the party of privilege and class. Their programme for generations had been to exploit the lower classes and they had succeeded so well that when at last they bad been largely brought to book, they bad no argument for their position except that which had obtained in the past. They had keenly resented being bi ought to book and had made a huge cry about it, pretending that they were the martyrs of their day. He was glad to know that the Liberal party had gained in strength and power so much that at last it bad been able to grapple with the whole situation. The veto of the House of Lords was practically</p>
        <p>RELEGATED TO A ROARY ANTIQUITY. (ApplesSe.j Now, instead of those people being able to dominate the country, it had become a case of each man having a right to live, to vote and to speak on the questions of the day. In spite of the dust-throwing to which they had been treated, the Insurance Bill bad become the law of the land. (Applause.) He noticed from that day's paper that the Boilermakers' Union had been recommended by their leaders to qualify themselves to meet the requirements of the Act and they contended that their society alone would profit to the value of £54,000 per annum. It was also a matter for rejoicing that about 000,000 people over seventy years of age were now receiving an old-age pension. (Applause.) They bad not to thank the Tory party for that. (A voice : "No fear.") That interjection was a sign that the Liberal women were just about ready for the vote. (Laughter and applause.) In Ireland there had been sad cases of misrule but at last that country was likely to come into its own. (Applause.) Mr. Cooper next paid e tribute to Mr. Crosfleld's labours in the progressive cause, pointing out that he was one of those men who, feeling convinced that the Liberal programme was calculated to lift the working-classes to a higher stage of civilization, educltion and social status, were pre- pared to forego their own specific interests and help on the work of Liberalism. Ile trusted that association would do everything p)saible to strengthen the hands of the leaders so that when the next election came, NVarrington would return a Liberal. (Applause.) Mr. HARRY New said a few months ago a prominent member of the Tory party said the Libarni women were copying the Tariff Reformers. (Laughter). The secretory of the Liberal Women's Association, however, tersely and pointedly replied that there was no occasion for them to try and emulate the deeds of the Women Tariff Reformers. (Applause). She must have had in mind a certain scene in Horsemarket-street at a rather late hour on a certain day. (Laughter).</p>
        <p>, IMITATION WAS THE SINCEREST FORM OF FLATTERY and, in a few days, they would find that instead of the Liberal women imitating the Tories, the Tories were going to try and imitate them. (Laughter.) They had taken the Parr Hall—they believed in doing things on a big scale—and they were going to have over 2,000 pork pies. (Laughter.) He could tell them a secret about those pies—(laughter)—and even Mr. Harold Smith could not make him apologise. (Laughter.) Mr. Smith seemed to have taken upon himself the position of public censor of Warrington. One felt it was a great privilege indeed to be allowed to breathe. (Laughter.) Some years ago when be (Mr. New) was serving in the navy he was told that when be intended to speak to his superior officer he was to take out his words, look at them and put them back again. (Laughter.) That was about the position of the average Liberal in Warrington. He was called upon for data hnd every possible thing just to please the whim of one individual whose one aim seemed to be to dash into the limelight and get a little cheap popularity—even at the expense of their friend Mr. Cooper. (Laughter.) However, Mr. Cooper was not alone in that kind of attack. Ile (Mr. New) had had a share of it. The Tories bad still got that £l.OO challenge out for him to accept. A more silly, nonsensical and ridiculous challenge was never issued, and no decent-minded body of people could issue such a thing, because they deliberately concocted things he did not say and deliberately left out things he did s .y. It seemed to him that their friends on the other side had to stoop much lower than the ordinary line of public morale to make their presence felt. Mr. New next passed strictures upon Mr. Smith's alliance with the Halsbury faction. He seemed to see the member for Warrington marching to the Hotel Cecil, swearing to die in the law ditch and throwing over his trusted leader, Mr. Arthur Balfour. Yet a few days after those proceedings he (Mr. New) heard Sir Gilbert Greenall say that Arthur Bilfour was the Conservative leader. HOW they could reconcile the two positions, he failed to see. Every Liberal woman, continued Mr. New, could sly to herself " I sun a Liberal, not because I worship a certain lady, not because I bow the knee to mammon, but because I am convinced from experience that the Liberals are the only party who are willing to give to the working classes that which should have been their's years ago." (Applause ) Mr. New then touched upon some of the chief measures for which the Government had been responsible, including the Children's Act, Old Age Pensions,and the Insurance Act. When the first Lloyd George Budget was to be intro- duced, there were only 700,000 men and women r ?ceiv- big old age pensions and 200,000 were debarred by the pauper disqualification. They were in the Workhouses, slums and back alleys. Mr. Lloyd George heard their cry and said " Give those 200,000 men and women the pension which it rightly theirs." Lord Lansdowne said No." lie threw the Bubget out from Lansdowne House and for twelve months more, at the bidding of the Tory party, 200,000 men and women had to wait in the Workhouses, infirmaries or somewhere elso, before they could get their pensions. The Tories</p>
        <p>RESISTED OLD AGE PENSIONS to the very last and he could imagine Lord Cromer, home from Egypt wilh £50,000 grant for services rendered and £SOO a year for life, saying that old age pensions would destroy the independence of the work- ing classes. (Laughter ) A few sears ago Mr. Lloyd- George crept into Birmingham like an outcast and the fury of the crowd was so great that he had to disguise himself as a policeman and get out by the back door. Since then commonsense had prevailed. Mr. Lloyd George recently went to Birmingham to proclaim the message of liberty to 12,000,000 people, telling them that he was going to give them an Insurance Bill which would once and for all remove from the homes of the poor that great load of trouble which accompanied visitations of sickness. The people of Birmingham received him in triumph like an Emperor. (Applause.) The Tory party, continued Mr. New, were alwass cackling about</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>~,.-,.,,,,,„•-.,.,-i CHILDREN'S CORNER, Conducted by UNCLE CALEB.</head>
        <p>~,.-,.,,,,,„•-.,.,-i CHILDREN'S CORNER, Conducted by UNCLE CALEB.</p>
        <p>"SING A SONG OF SIXPENCE."</p>
        <p>You all know the old nursery rhyme about the " four-and-t wenty blackbirds baked in a pie," but I wonder how many of you have ever heard the meaning of it. It is a very old song ; so old that the children probably sang it in ancient Egypt, though of course they would sing of some other kind of bird than a blackbird.</p>
        <p>This famous pie is supposed to be a picture of the whole day. The twenty-four black- birds are the twenty-four hours of the day. The bottom of the pie is supposed to be the world, and the top crust the sky. When the pie is opened day breaks, and the birds begin to sing. The Xing sitting in his counting- house is the sun, and the golden coins he is counting are golden sunbeams. The Queen in her parlour is the moon, and the honey is the moonlight. The busy maid in the garden is the peep of day ; the clothes which she is hanging out are clouds ; and the blackbird which snaps off her nose is the sunset. BRIDGET'S IDEA.</p>
        <p>"Did you post my letter Bridget?" asked her mistress. "It was an important one you know." "Yes, mum, indeed I did." "But why have you brought back the penny I gave you for a stamp? " "Share I didn't have to use it, mum," replied Bridget. " I slipped th' letter into th' box whin nobody was lukin."</p>
        <p>THE JUNG LE. A poem supposed to have been written by a boy who had been learning the names of herbs and plants. The meadow is an awful place For one so young as I; The dandelion you must face. The fiery dragon-fly, The snakeroot and the adder's-tongue; Terribilous to one so young ! Though gentle cowslips kindly yield Milkweed and buttercup, Beside you, hidden in the field, The bears's-paw reaches up To grab at you, behind, before; While tiger-lilies routine and roar. And my ! what brigand armies pass Oa horseflies or on foot, Their Turk's-beards waving in the grass; They're armed with arrowroot. And all the flowers have pistils, too; While sword-grass waits to cat you through. Now maybe Indian-pires don't mean A horrid Sc.olvix camp! And think! there's toadstools to b 3 seen,</p>
        <p>Tiorrifleently damp. tell you, I ain't going to go Where crawly, wawly creatures grow!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>HAVOC WROUGHT BY CHEAP HOME-MADE SALVES.</head>
        <p>HAVOC WROUGHT BY CHEAP HOME-MADE SALVES.</p>
        <p>Nine Years with Ulcerated Legs.—Had to Crawl About on Hands and Knees.</p>
        <p>MARVELLOUS CURE BY TIM GENUINE ZAM-BUN.</p>
        <p>When Mr. James Fulton begin to be troubled with skin disease, be adopted the penny-wise-pound-foolish policy of using cheap and unreliable ointments. Nine years' terrible torture was the penalty, with no pros- pect of relief till Zam-Buk worked a remarkable cure Mr. Fulton, who lives at 71, Buttermeie-road Abbeydale, Sheffield, is a well-known figure at the Sheffield Carter Knowlc Methodist Mission. To a reporter he said :—" About nine years ago scurf formed on my right kg. Scratching the leg caused matter to ooze out from under the scurf. I bought ointments as I did not think the trouble serious. But I've had to pay for it since. The many cheap and unreliable ointments I used only h,lped the disease to get auras hold, until my leg was a terrible sight. It was full of holes, which were constantly discharging matter. "Then my left leg started in the same way. I was almost distracted with pain. When I got out of bed in a morning blood and matter ran in streams from the sores. Often I had to crawl on my hands and , knees, scarcely able to bear the agony. " For months I was under the doctor, but my lops remained as bad as ever. I then went to a skin specialist, whose trea'ment proved just as useless as everything else. " But I decided to make just another attempt to heal my legs, and this time I tried Zam-Buk, I should mention that I was now bed-ridden. Zam-Buk eased the pain with the first applications, and after a little perseverance the improvement was quite marked. The discharge was less, the pain and irritation gradually died away, and I go more strength in my legs. I continued with Zam-Buk regularly until every hole on both legs was perfectly healed, and covered with new healthy skin I cannot feel grateful enough to Zam-Buk for this wonderful cure. Three years later Mr Fulton writes :—" My legs have kept perfectly well since the genuine Zam-Buk saved me pounds in doctors' bills, and enabled me to work regularly again. I hare finished for ccer with cheap and unreliable home-made ointments."</p>
      </div>
