      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>LIBERAL WOMEN.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL WOMEN.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER SUCCESSFUL WARD</p>
        <p>SOCIAL</p>
        <p>Letter from Mrs. A. H. Crosfield.</p>
        <p>REV. W. COOPER AND HIS CRITICS.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Women's Liberal Association has resumed its activities, and the splendid progress in numbers and enthusiasm which was such a striking feature of the work of last year is being steadily maintained. On Monday evening a most successful t peeting and concert in connection with Howlq Ward took place in the Cairo-street school- room. There was an attendance of about 200, and the proceedings were full of interest and encouragement. Admirable arrangements had been made by Mrs. Hill, ward clptain, assisted by members of the committee and Mrs. E. Osborne, hon secretary of the association. At the concert, the chair was taken by Miss Bessie Dutton who was supported on the platform by Mesdames Walker, Osborne,Dutton, Pemberton, Hill, Misses Walker and Booth, the Rev. W. Cooper, Mr. Barry New and Mr. M. Llewellyn. An excellent andw ell-varied programme was submitted and afforted great pleasure. Mr. Nathan Cook, jun., was heartily encored for his spirited rendering of "My cl shako" and responded with " Two eyes of grey." Later he sang "Ailsa mine" with equal success. Mr. T. S Hill recited " Trouble in Amen Corner " in a graphic style whic'a delighted the audience ; and he also created much merriment with the story of "The man with one hair." Miss Howard gave a graceful shadow dance and joined Miss Littlewood in en Irish jig, both being cleverly executed. Mr. Rowland played two violin volos with taste and skill. A scene from "As you like it " was creditably performed by the Misses Ridgway and Lomas. At intervals short speeches were delivered. The reading of the following letter addressed to Mrs. Hill by Mrs. A. H. Crosfield was greeted with enthusiastic applause : Rost' s Continental Hotel, Cannes A.M.</p>
        <p>Dear Mrs. Hill, I am writing to tell you how much I regret missing your Ward tea on Monday, and to wish you a pleasant and most successful gathering. Out here we have recently been entertaining the Chancellor of the Exchequer, the Chief Whip, and the Attorney General, and have had many long and deeply interesting political discussions with them, and one realises the more vividly the immense amount of labour undertaken by this powerful Ministry during these last eventful years. In the year that has just closed</p>
        <p>THE BURDEN ON IHE SHOULDERS OF MR. LLOYD GEORGE was of course specially heavy. In grappling with this vast problem cf insurance against the dark days of illness, infirmity or the most prevalent hind of unemployment; in taking steps to ward ofl sickness and abolish the slums which breed disease; in making provision against the scourge of consumption in carrying through that measure of national ble.ssing, the Old Ace Pension Act ; in fighting the vested interests which opposed the People's Budget —that democratic finance which has provided the moray for all this noble and splendid reform—Mr. Lloyd George has throughout been fighting the people's battle. Those of the well-to-do classes who are very selfish, and s:me vested interests may vie in the</p>
        <p>VIEELENCE AND VIOLENCE with which they have attacked him, but a man who is fighting with such courage and energy for the welfare of the people surely deserves the people's strong and irresistible support. In the long struggle we have successfully waged against taxes upon food and clothing, it has often been urged that vital as Free Trade is for us in England from a man's point of view, it is still more vitally a woman's question. And so of Social Reform ; let us all realise that this noble wok of sociil reform,which in the long run amounts to nothing short of national regeneration, will be for us women the greatest blessing. Again wishing you all a happy gathering and a very successful meeting, and feeling certain that the excellent progress of our Association and its work will be maintained, I remain sincerely 3 ours, ' DO MINI CROSFIELD. Miss DurroN expressed the opinion that the exien- sion of the franchise to women was drawing nearer—lf they would behave themselves and not go in for setting , any more post offices on fire or breaking windows. 1 I (Laughter). They must keep the question well before the public and they would triumph. (Applause). When I they had the power, they must see to it that they used it wisely and vell. Organisations like the Women's Liberal Association helped them to appreciate what was good and to know how to refuse the evil. Under present conditions, women could exercise an immense influence in political affairs. She had been told that women did not want the vote because they could so thoroughly intluen ce their men-folk. (Laughter.) They certainly had power in that respect and thefmust learn to use it in the right direction. Their enthusiasm must not expend itself in the mere wearing of ribbons or waving of flags. There must be real knowledge accompanied by hard work for the cause which they believed to be right. She had been a Liberal almost from her cradle, but it was not only because the principles of Free Trade and Liberalism had been inculcated by her parents, that she SV33 a supporter of that party to-day, She was convinced that the Liberal party was DOING SOMETHING FOR THOSE WHO MOST NEEDED</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>11..ELP.</head>
        <p>11..ELP.</p>
        <p>(Applause.) The real sacrifice, as Mr. Lloyd .George.— (applause)—had pointed out was made when those who were better circumstanced and informed were pre- pared to forego something for those who did not possess the same advantages. In this aud every country we should always be in the position that some would have far more chances and means than others, and they must practise self-sacrifice for tho sake of those who nceded their help. Teat spirit was gradually becoming nearer realization, although there was still a vast amount of grumbling on the part of some people if anything had to be done for anybody else. She was very sorry that there had been so much said about the Insurance Act ; because a great deal of the objection and of "taking the pledge"against that measure had been amongst those who could well afford to do their part. (Applause.) One never knew what misfortune might overtake a workingman's household, and the great thing was to guard against uncertainty and to provide for the evil day. They looked forward to the time when all the little difficulties and conflicts in regard to the work- ing of the Act would be smoothed over and everyone would realize what a</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>GREAT BLESSING</head>
        <p>GREAT BLESSING</p>
        <p>the national insurance scheme was. (Applause.) She wanted Liberal women to remember the great benefit they would get by clinging to Free Trade. (Applause.) It seemed as if that question had dropped somewhat into the background of political discussion of late, but there were still Tariff Reform Leagues and they were trying to increase their strength. She did not call tariff reform a reform at all,hut a step backward. (Hear,hear.) She therefore advised the workers to keep clear of that and to adhere to a policy which ensured a plentiful supply of cheap food. (Applause.) Pleading for incresseel energy and enthusiasm in the progressive interest, Miss Dutton said the Conservatives were anxious to conserve that which was to their own advantage but, until the screw was put on and they were forced, they did not conserve that which was of beseftt to those who were less able to help them- selves. It was strange that there should be such a person as a Cotservative workingman. Liberals wanted freedom end breadth in everything, and a feeling that should reach below the privileged classes to the workers. They must, concluded MiES Dutton, help each other and cling to everything that brought freedom and prosperity. (Applause.) The Rev. W. COOPER said he hoped to live to see the day when he c3uld address the wo-Jen as " fellow voters." Congratulating the Association on. that splendid gathering, he said that not only did those present seem capable of listening to the speeches, but he had not heard any councillor shout out " Stop the beer!" (Laughter and applause). Moreover, he had a suspicion that ir they were to form a procession with their esteemed president in the frcnt rank, they could walk steadily. (afore laughter and applause.) Although he was supposed to be a Christian minister in this town, he had been taken to task, even by some who thought they were his friends, for being interested in the Liberal programme. Well,he was not yet aware that being a minister of the Gospel debarred a man from being a citizen. (Hear, hear). Further, he could never forget that he had had to earn his daily bread by his hands and because of that he felt he must stand with the working classes of his day. (Applause.) In making his choice in the matter of politics, he thought out the question as intelligently as he could. Having been REARED 31,7 A TOE.Y it took one some time to have settled opinions about the matter. However, when a workingman began to think there was not much in the other party that would bear thinking about, so far as his interests were concerned, therefore be was driven over to the ranks of the Liberal Party. When his critics told him he had no right to take a prominent part in politics, he was afraid they forgot a few things. He was a Primitive Methodist minister in this town, not by the conferment of any lord of the manor or by the will of any brewer. 1 (Applause.) He was here by the free will of his own people and so long as it was mutually agreeable he should stay. He was very proud to be allied with</p>
        <p>ummouil</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>GERMANY.</head>
        <p>GERMANY.</p>
        <p>lie wondered whether they were going to advise us to look at Germany now. (Laughter.) Germany, they had hitherto told us, was the great enlightened country where the men were united and tariff reform reigned supreme. But there had just been an election in Germany and the country had gone rel. (Applause.) The people had awakened and 4,000,000 of them bad recorded an emphatic protest again the warlike tendency of those men who desired to eee two great nations at each other's throats. Oae reason why he rejoiced in the EU nO9S of the Social almocrits was because there would be no war between England and Germany while such a feeling prevailed. They should pray for peace, rememberiag that directly peace came on the scene, social reform made- advances. We were spending £16,000,000 a year in building immense battleships. He contended that a large portion of the money which was at present expended on armaments would he better devoted to alleviating the sufferings of the toilers and ma'ling their lives brighter and more useful. (Applause). Mr. M. LLEVICLLYN also addressed the meeting. He said he was living in hopes that before long the Government would cry out " Stop the beer " en election da3B. (Applause.) Liberals had tremendous forcel arrayed against them and it was remark- able that the advsrse influence generally flowed from the public houses. With regard to other reforms which demanded attention, he was looking forward to the time when a man's political power would be based on his manhood and not on the wealth he possessed. He predicted that before the Insurance Act had been in force twelve months, those who were now criticising would be applauding it. The measure would be the salvation of many homes. (Applause.) It was announced that a prize given by Mr. G. Howard for the best )2lb loaf had been won by Mrs. Hemingway.</p>
        <p>The total value of the bulldog-I exhibited at the Leeds and County Bulldog Clubs show on Saturday, was estimated by an expert to be something over £15,000, the value of individual exhibits in severil oases rnnning into four [lvrea.</p>
