      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <p>WARR INGTOti</p>
        <p>e-SOC/AL</p>
        <p>profession end eminent men in other walks of I:fe. He also referred to the great and prolonged suffering to animals which had been practised by vivisectionists. The whole system had the tendency to harden the natural feelings of medical students. The discoveries of vivisection were all challenged by the leading authorities of the day and the pain infl.cted upon the animals, for whatever object, was cruel arid impious. Mr. kfchlachren read the lesson. Tee Secretary led the prayer. Mr Payton presided at the organ. Bold-street Church.—Men's Bible Class. The heavy snowstorm did not prevent the Bold-street Men's Class from holding their lecture on Thursday evening, when Mr. (LA. Dunlop spoke on "The science of the Sea." The lecturer, who illustrated his remarks with about 200 tine lantern slides, dealt with the subject under six heads, viz. :—The development of sea science, The sea bed, the sea, work at a marine station, a dredging cruise, and some marine biology. The last' three heads were exceedingly interesting, as Mr Dunlop gave his exreriences on one of the special vessels which are chartered to make scientific researches round the coasts The various forms of life which abound in the British Seas, from tiny specks to the great " six footers," had been photoraphed by Mr. Dunlop, and the slides were thrown upon the screen. The lecture throughout was fascinating and educational ; and it is no flattery to saY that Mr. Dunlop is an authority on this as well BS other branches of natural science. The CHAIRMAN (Mr. J. C. Holland) voiced the feel- ings of the audience when he thanked the lecturer ; and on the proposition of Mr. STANLEY C. JONES, seconded by Mr. PETER WOODS, a hearty vote of thanks wait accorded to Mr. Dunlop for his kindness, and to Mr. B. H. Sutton, who skilfully manipulated the lantern.</p>
