      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>Sudden Death of the Rector of Lowton.</head>
        <p>Sudden Death of the Rector of Lowton.</p>
        <p>The death took place suddenly on Saturday night, as the result of a stroke, of the Rev. Webster Hall, rector of Lowton, a large village near Newton-le-Willows. The rev, gentleman, who was well known and generally respected in his large parish, was about fifty-five years of age. Educated at St. John's College, Cambridge, where he graduatad B.A. in 1878 and M.A. in 1889, he was ordained by the Bishop of Chester in 1878 (the diocese being then undivided) to the curacy of St. Saviour's, Huskisson-street, Liverpool, under the late Rev. J. Waring Bardsley, afterwards Bishop of Carlisle. After an interval of live years (1880-1885) spent as rector of a little country parish in Yorkshire,he returned to Liver- pool as vicar of St. Alban's, Limekiln-lane, in the heart of the city slums, where he laboured devoutly till 1891. In that year he became vicar of St. Cleopas', Toxteth Park, and remained there till the spring of 1809. The heavy labours of carrying on two churches in that parish with wholly insufficient local resources sapped his strength, and be was glad to receive from the Earl 'of Derby the offer of the rectory of Lowton. In this comparative retirement his strength seemed to be gradually returning, and he was in Liverpool in his usual health as recently as Wednesday of last week. An impressive demonstration of sympathy marked the funeral which took place at Lowton Parish Church on Tuesday. Some hundreds lined the road to the church, and bore the service was conducted by the Bishop of Liverpool, Archdeacon Madden and Arcladeacln Spooner. The deceased s favourite hymn, "Hock of Ages," and the " Nun° Dimittis " were sung; and Dr. Chavasse preached a short sermon. The Bishop said it was nearly three years to a week when ha came among them to lay in his last earthly resting-place the late rector, Mr. Richard Smith, and again he had to perform the same solemn office to the late Mr. Webster Hall. Mr. Smith had worked there for thirty-three years, and was regarded as the father of the parish, Mr. Hall was only with them for two years and tbree-quarters, and It was on May Day, 1009, that he was instituted and inducted. He was in the prime of life, after having spent twenty years, the best years of his life, in some of the poorest parts of Liverpool. The call had come to him, and he bad gone. The Bishop urged those who knew him to remember him, his work, and his noble life.</p>
        <p>Amongst the general mourners were the Revs. J. Davies (Warrington), James Ryder, F. F. Komlosy. and B. E. Taylor (Newton), J. T, ocren (representing Canon Pentbyn,Winwick), Francis Smith (St. Mary's Lowton), E. W. Kay (Newchurch), T. W. Richardson (Golhorne)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>Christ Church, Latchford. Dedication of the New Porch.</head>
        <p>Christ Church, Latchford. Dedication of the New Porch.</p>
        <p>As a memorial of the jubilee of Christ Church, Latchford, a new porch has been built at the door or the north aisle. The cost is estimated at about £l5O which has been raised by subscription. Archi- tecturally the new structure is in harmony with the rest of the building. Running round the inside is the inscription :—" Ann° Quioquagesimo Ilujus Ecclesiae Consecrate° Han Porticus Aed &amp;cats Est A.D. MCM XI. Sit Pass Deo."—" In the fiftieth year of the consecra- tion o f this Church this porch was built A,D. 1911. To God be the praise." The work wis commenced in the autumn, the architects being Messrs. William El d Seger Owen, and the builder, Mr. J. F. 113oth, of Forrest- street. Another memorial of the jubilee of the Church is to take the form of a peal of tubular bells, which will be erected in the tower from time to time as funds will allow.</p>
        <p>The new porch wa3 dedicated by the Vicar (Rev. G. C. Briggs) prior to Matting on Sunday. The choir entered the church by the North door singing Psalm cxxii., and the dedicatory prayer was read by the Vicar. In the course of his subsequent sermon the Vicar, who took for his text the wards, " They presented unto Him gifts;' St. Matthew ii., ii., said that in that chapter of the gospel the Evangelist was alluding to the visit of the wise even to the infant Redeemer at Bethlehem. They that day were also presenting Him with a gift in the shape of an addition and adornment to His house, which was dedicated to Hod in the name of His Son.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>Free Church Council.</head>
        <p>Free Church Council.</p>
        <p>The annual exchange of pulpits arranged by the War- rington Free Church Council took place on Sunday. In the evening about 250 members of the various churches attended the annual united Communion service of the Free Churches, which was held in Wycliffe Con- gregational Church. The Pastor (the R3v. J. Yonge) preilded anl the Rev. E. )ran, of Padget de'i ve e an address. Prayers were of/.3red by tin Revs. W. Rid and W. Cooper.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>St. John's Presbyterian Church.</head>
        <p>St. John's Presbyterian Church.</p>
        <p>On Thursday evcaing last the annual congregational business meeting was held at St- John's, the Rev. Wm Reid presiding, On account of the very inclement state of the weather the attendance was much less than would otherwise have been the case. The reports of the Kirk Sessicn and the Board of Managers were presented along with the annual financial statement, and reports from the various societies and organisations of the church. The Session report showed the member- ship to be slightly over 230, as against little more than 50 when Mr. Reid took over the pastorate, late in 1909. The financial statement indicated a substantial increase in all the funds, a pleasing and healthy feature being that not only was the strictly congregational finance well maintained,but sums amounting in all to nearly 20 bad been raised and handed over either to mission and other schemes of the Presbyterian Church, or to loml philanthopic objects.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society. There was a fair attendance at tha Wycliffe Cungt e gational P.S.A. mooting, over which air. iiimswarth presided. Mr. R. E. O'Callagan, of thr Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Animals by Vivisection, gave an address entitled " Csn the end justify the means ?" This claim, the speaker said, was responsible for much oppression and cruelty to men and women in the p st• As instances, he referred to the Spanish Inquisition, the torture of prisoners ; the treatment of supposed witches; child lab ; negro slavery and cruel sports., and in our da3 to sweating and the specious excuses for forms of immorality. To uphold that the end justified the means was an immoral and dangerous doctrine. It implied that we might do things that we knew to be intrinsically wrong. In the past such teaching hal been tested and tried and universally condemned. In regard, however, to the infliction of pain on animals by vivisection, the belief that the end justified the means was still prevalent and was sheltered by law. The speaker contended that the vivisection of animals was both inhuman and immoral. Referring to an influential meeting of the Research Defence Society recently held at Oxford it was pointed out that stripped of verbiage and sophistry—the addresses then given showed that in that great seat of learning grid in the presence of a Bishop of the E,tablished Church, it was openly claimed that the end justified the means so long as the end was the alleviation of human ills and the means the torture of animals. The spikaker traversed the various statement made at the Oxford meeting, quoting from prominent members of the medical</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>WHEN DOCTORS ARE DEAF.</head>
        <p>WHEN DOCTORS ARE DEAF.</p>
        <p>WHEN A DOCTOR IS AILING</p>
        <p>he has the opportunity of visiting his pro- fessional brethren quite free of any fee. It follows, therefore, that a Doctor suffering from any disease or affliction would only go to the recognised authorities on the particular form of trouble he has.</p>
        <p>The affliction of deafness to anyone in the medical profession is terrible to contemplate. It means a life's study and labour lost. No one wants a deaf Doctor, as he is at such a great disadvantage. This also applies to all other professional men, business men and women, and general workers,no matter what their calling may be. The race for life's success and the keen competition calls for the highest efficiency of every faculty. Dr. M. L. H , L.R.C., L.M., suffered from catarrhal deafness, accompanied with head noises, and had visited every well- known Specialist all over the world, and was in despair of any benefit and with the dread of being obliged to give up his practice. His own profession having failed to give him relief, he turned his attention to electricity and scientific instruments, and ultimately investigated the merits of Mr. Philip V. Summer's Akou-Vibra Invention. THE DOCTOR INVESTIGATES. The Doctor wrote for particulars and sent for a machine on trial and hire. His awn words written out of a spirit of gratitude best express his verdict THE DOCTOR'S OPINION. "My ear trouble is of 11 or 12 years' duration, and has gradually become worse. My left 'ear is practically useless, and the hearing in my right ear is only about half as acute as that in a normal ear. My case is a pure one of middle ear catarrh with gradually increasing deafness, accompanied by constant noises (tinnitus aurium). For such cases your Akou-Vibra instrument seems to have been specially designed, and as far as my judgment goes, I think from both an anatomical and physiological point of view the treatment is scientific, rational and safe—scientifical and rational because the electrical massage and vibrations are designed to act upon the drum and through it on ossicles (or bones) of the middle ear, all of which structures are affected in this form of deafness; and safe because of an ingenious device, there is no , danger of electrical shock in using it. "In conclusion, I am glad to be able to saY that the noises in both ears aro less trouble- some, and my hearing is already more improved by it than any other treatment I have ever had previously." THE INVENTOR IN LIVERPOOL. Further comment is superfluous. His et ter can be seen by anyone, together with hundreds of others (many by doctors), and from the general public.</p>
        <p>The Inventor has received a great man3r- letters from residents in Liverpool and dis- tricts asking him to give them an oppor- tunity of seeing and trying the Akou-Vibra.' ,He has already visited the principal towns in, Scotland and the North of England, and ha 9 received remarkable proofs of successful cases. Mr. Summer has local testimonr' proving that the Akou-Vibra will alleviate and cure the most obstinate cases of deafness 4 and head noises. FREE TESTS DAILY. Damonstrations are now being given for-- a short time at 36, THE ARCADE, LORP ST., LIVERPOOL. Hours 10 to 12.30, 2.30 to 6. Saturdays to 2.</p>
        <p>SIMPLE TO USE. Anyone can use an Akou-Vibra, and 0 those living too far away to attend t.lle rooms, an instrument may be had on trial and hire.</p>
        <p>Sankey Bridges Adult School. Last Sunday morning Mr. B. S. Townroe delivered an address on National Service. An interesting an somewhat animated discussion followed, the feeling 0. the s3hool beingogin the whole, against any form compulsory service.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
