      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>FATHER'S FAREWELL WISH.</head>
        <p>FATHER'S FAREWELL WISH.</p>
        <p>"Keep Willie to his Sunday School."</p>
        <p>The circumstances attending the death cf Thomas Green (50), a boiler-maker, of 56, Winwick-street, who committed suicide by cutting his throat with a bread- knife were inquired into by Mr. S. Brighouse, county coroner, on Saturday. The evidence showed that about 5.15 on Wednesday morning Green took his wife a cup of tea acd some toast upstairs. About 6.23 she thought she heard him moaning, and OD going into the back kitchen she fonmi him lying face downwards with a wound in his throat. She gave an alarm, but on the arrival of the police ber husband was dead. Lately deceased had been depressed, and had been in ill-health. He complained on Tuesday that he conld not go to work. The deceased formerly served in the King's Liverpool Regiment (Bth), and had been in India eight years. A notebook was found on the window ledge, in which was written :—"Dear wife, God bless you and the children. I can hardly write for the voices calling me. Keep Willie to his Sunday school. Good-bye." The jury returned a verdict of " Suicide whilst of unsound mind."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>ANTIQUARIAN CHAPTERS</head>
        <p>ANTIQUARIAN CHAPTERS</p>
        <p>CHRISTENING CURIOSITIES.</p>
        <p>QUAINT SCRAPS FROM CHURCH REGISTERS.</p>
        <p>There is history and interest in everything if, in Shakespeare's words, we will but patiently distil it out. We once in a country thurch saw the godmother at a baptism stand real y with a cambric handkerchief which was placed over the infant's face immediately the water was sprinkled. Possibly not one of that company of country- -sred folks had the remotest idea of what the custom meant, and possibly they all, as good Protestants, would have been shocked to learn that it was a survival of very remote lays when children were marked with holy Al and protected by a Chrisom cloth. We will leave out from our present dis- cussion any reference to the origin of what is called infant baptism, and deal only with such interesting scraps as can be gathered from church registers. In medieval days in England children were brought to church, not to be sprinkled with water, but to be anointed with the Chrism or holy oil. The priest took the tiny child and upon its bare breast and back made, in oil, the form of a cPoss. The infant was then wrapped around in a white cloth, the idea being to preserve the marks from hasty removal and give the child a suitable spotless wrapping. The holy oil was called the chrism, the cloth was called the chrisom and was worn as a vesture by the child for seven full days, or until the mother was able</p>
        <p>ISED SLAB AT CROXALL, DERBY&amp; SHOWING CHRISOM CLOTH.</p>
        <p>to be " churched "—as the common phrase still goes. The cloth belonged to the church and at this service the mother gave it back to the priest. CHRYSOMER CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>If, however, the child died before the mother could come to church the chrisom became its shroud. White bands of linen were tied around the body and it was thus laid to rest. Such little ones were called " Chrisom Children."</p>
        <p>Sometimes, in cases where the child has been of noble family, old brass funeral tablets record the burial, and here and there an engraving shows the chrism and bands. There is one in the church at Croxall in Derbyshire which always attracts the eye of the visitor.</p>
        <p>The anointing of children was not given up at the Reformation, as may be gathered from the Prayer Book of 1549. Here it is decreed: The godfathers and godmothers shall take and lay their hand 4 up in the child, and the minister shall put upon h;m his %%hit° vesture, commonly called a otitis:Ll, and say 'Take this whi e vesture f r a token of innocency which God's grace in this holy Sacrament of Bapti,m has given unto thee; and for a sign whereby thou at adword,hed, so long as.thou.livest, to give thyself to inneceney</p>
        <p>of livig, that after this transitoi'y life thou may's-t be a partaker of the life ererlasling. This disappeared from the Prayer Book of 1552, but though no longer enjoyed by the Church the use of the chrism lingered, and the placing of a handkerchief by the god- mother is the last trace of it.</p>
        <p>Even after the use of the cloth and oil had gone the term "chrism child" remained in common use. Shakespeare uged it to make Dame Quickly say that Falstaff died like "any christom child," and the parish registers use it all through the seventeenth century in such forms as these: 1588, November 18th. A Crysomer of Thomas Gooses buried. (Broughton Register). 1687. The Princess Aim's Child a Chrissome</p>
        <p>ibu'irti.ge vault. Oct. 22. Westminster Abbey. - The persistency of a custom is splendidly illustrated in the Chrysom. Young babies were so called until the eighteenth century, and women continued to take a cambric handkerchief to give to the minister at their churching, though scarce one of them is likely to have had any glimmer of knowledge as to its meaning.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>CHRISTENING AND BAPTISING</head>
        <p>CHRISTENING AND BAPTISING</p>
        <p>Akin --Co the word Chrysom is Christening, or dedication to Christ. In the old days the word was used correctly, and it is necessary to point out to this generation that there is a decided difference between a Christenirg and. a Baptism, . .</p>
        <p>The Christening was the public receiving of a child before the congregation as a mem- ber of the church; the baptism was the immersion or naming. We see the distinction which our fathers preserved when we read the church registers: 1795 Rob., son of Rob. and Mary Baker priv. bapt. June l4ih publicly christened June 21st. 1701. Thos of Tho and Margaret Burett was bapt and christened in the Church 6th December. A Variation in phraseology appears in the entry 1598. Thomas the sonne of Stephen Smith was incorporated into the Church° of God February 25th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>PARISH RECORDS OF BAPTISMS.</head>
        <p>PARISH RECORDS OF BAPTISMS.</p>
        <p>Enough has been written to reveal the interest of the Parish Register even in its usually monotonous "Baptism " pages. True, the pages are wearisome enough, as any searcher will testify, and many of them are next to valueless. As, for instance : 1556 Jan. 27. A child of Robert Welelle Bapt. 1537 Oct. 10. ./Jhn Kilriche Bap. The second at least give the sex and &gt;name o f the child, the first gives neither. Of the usu al entry of the sixteenth century no better ex ample can be given than the famous one of Stratford-on-Avon. - -</p>
        <p>1564 April 26th. Guliemus fihiu Johannis Sh akspere bapt.</p>
        <p>In this way the infant arrivals on the world's great stage were recorded and the pages are bald enough, but here and there the eye lights upon a word that provokes enquiry. Such is the term "Half-Baptised."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>HALF BAPTIZED.</head>
        <p>HALF BAPTIZED.</p>
        <p>This was the word chosen of our ancestors to describe those who had been privately sprinkled with water when it seemed that they were in danger of death. Such private administration of baptism is, of course, allowed by the Church of England, and when puzzled to find a word to describe it the clergy fixed upon this. There is a good illustration in the register of Westminster Abbey. 1778 June 274 Rebecca, daughter of Anselm and Rebecca Bayley, barn Juue 23rd and half- baptised June 27th. Fully baptised April 290, 1779.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>NAMES.</head>
        <p>NAMES.</p>
        <p>Then there is the subject of Christian names, the curiosities of which will always repay the searcher, and of which perhaps enough has been written. In the middle ages the people gave them- selves no worry about the choice of a name. The Roman Calendar was simply full of Saints days, and they took the name of the Saint on whose day the child was born, Martin Luther born on St. Martins eve is an example. But this led to delightful complications. Nearly fifty days in the calendar(49 probably is the correct number) were associated with some saint or martyr named John. This gave a remarkably good crop of Johns. And even when twins were born they were both, if they were boys, called by the same name. Here is an entry from Newenden. 1561, November 10th, John and John, ye two wanes of Thomas Savage.</p>
        <p>As though there w-eren't complications enough with twins without calling them by the same name.</p>
        <p>ANCIENT SPELLING, The delights of ancient spelling also await the register searcher. Our forbears were anything but fastidious in this matter. To mention classic names, John Bunyan is described in a single document (the one by which he was given his release from prison) as Bunion, Bunyon, Bunnion, Bunnyan and Bunyan, and the name Shakespeare is spelt in one register in fifteen different ways, the variations being from Shaxpere to Shakespeare. Even the familiar Smith which everybody is supposed to know how to spell, varies from Smyth to Smyghth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>DOUBLE NAMES.</head>
        <p>DOUBLE NAMES.</p>
        <p>The habit of giving a aOlii;ie name origin- ated upon the Continent and was adoptedibut very slowly in this country. Even monarchs used to be content with a single name. And if one may dare to level a criticism towards such lofty spheres, would it not be better to restore the old habit than inflict that lumbering many-barrelled compliment to nations and ancestors which begins George, Andrew, David, Patrick, Albert, Victor, etc., and which does not always fight its way to something distinctive at the finish? A.M.</p>
        <p>(To be continued).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>PARK'S BANK, LIMITED. .---- Report and Balance Sheet.</head>
        <p>PARK'S BANK, LIMITED. .---- Report and Balance Sheet.</p>
        <p>The 17th annual ordinary general meeting of this Company will be held at the Cannon-street Hotel, London, E.C, on Thursday, January 25th, for the pnrpose of declaring a dividend to be payable on 3rd February, electing directors and anditors, and conduct- ing the ordinary business of the company. The report of the directors is as follows : The directors, in submitting to the shareholders the balance sheet of the bank at 31st December, 1911, certified by the auditors, hive to report that the pJaition and prospects of the business continue to be in every way satibfactory. Including £159,030 Os. 61 brought forward at 31st December, 1910, the net balance of profits for the year after writing down investments to below market value, providing for all bad and doubtful debts, and rebating the current bills at 5 per cent. is £664,881 12s. id. Out of this an Interim Dividend at the rate of 20 per cent, per annum (less income tax) for the half year ended 30th Jule, 1911, was paid on 3rd August £207,616 15s. Bd., total £457,267 13s. sd. From this sum the Directors recommend :—The payment of a half- year's dividend at the rate of 20 per cent, per annum, less income tax, payable on 3rd February, £207,616 153. Si.; a bonus of 1 per cent., lets income tax pay- able on 3rd February, £20,761 139. 7d, ; the transfer to bank premises account, £30,000; the transfer to officer's pension and provident fund, £25,000; total £23,378 93. 31. ; leaving a balance to be carried to next account £173,889 7s. 2d. .</p>
        <p>in' accordance with the arrangement publicly announced in August hat, the hank, in conjunction with the Bank of England and oth3r banks, has bPcome a guarantor of the Yorkshire penny bank, limited. Offices of the bank have been opened at Rh )s-on-Ses, Denbighshire, and ‘vinscombe Somersetshire. The lion Hugo Baring awl Mr It W Whalley have been elected to s,at4 on the Board, and the shareholders will be asked to confirm these appointments. The directors who retire at this time by rotation are :—Mr N (Tarington Eckersley, Mr John It t Goodden. ,Mr Henry J Badcock, Mr II Cary G Batten, Mr Hugh B Muir, and the Hon Sir Charles VV Fremantle, K.O B all of whom are eligible for re-election. Mr Robert Neill retired from the Board in February hat. The Auditors, Messrs Turquand, Youngs and Co., London, Messrs Stead, Taylor and Stead, Liverpool, and Messrs Price, Waterhouse and Co., London, retire ano offer themselves for re-election.</p>
        <p>CECIL F. PARR, Chairman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>CHEAP FODDER CROPS.</head>
        <p>CHEAP FODDER CROPS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ernest Parke has issu.d his tenth annual report on field experiments on oats and marigolds carried out on his farm at Kinetou (Warwickshire) in conjunction with Dr. Dyer. Although the season over most of the country. and even locally, has been a bad one for roots, owing to the exceptionally dry weather. Mr. Parka was fortunate enough, on his best plot, to grow over 41 tons of marigolds per acre. This yield was obtained with a dressing of 4cwt. of superphosphate and 6cwt. of nitrate of soda. A comparison plot left unmanured gave 17 tons. Superphosphate alone gave not quite 27 tons. The addition of 2cwt, of nitrate of soda raised the yield to nearly 32 tons, and that of 4cwt. of nitrate to 37 tons, while the increase of the top dressing to 6owt. gave as already stated, over 41 tons. The figures are in close accord with the annual average over the whole period , of experiment in different fields, and •on the heaviest plots the average increase of mangolds due to manuring has been over 26 tons per acre of roots, at an average cost of about 78s. for fertilisers, or about 3s. per ton. The unmanureJ oat crop gave 51 bushels of oats and 25cwt. of straw per acre. A dressing of 3owt. of super- phosphate followed by a top dressing of lcwt. of nitrate of soda gave 63 bushels of oats and 30 cwt. of straw, and when 2cwt. of nitrate was used as top dressing the crop was 791 bushels of oats and 40owt of straw. On this plot the extra yield above that of the unmanured plot amounted therefore to 3i quarters of oats and 15owt. of straw, in return for an expenditure of about 309. on fertilisers.</p>
        <p>BILLPOSTING MANAGER'S DEATH AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. E. Broom, who has for many years been manager for the St. Helens Billposting Company, died on Monday night. He was about 41 years of age, and had been in delicate health for some years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>vEmrs</head>
        <p>vEmrs</p>
