        <p>COUGH CURE 1 ArsmirSarlrn:Esq..M.S.ll.Sc.. the celebrated London analyst, says :—Vyrro's Doirrsixo Comm Cues is composed of pure ingredients, and Is a most excellent remedy for bronchltls, asthma. and all lung affections.'</p>
        <p>The Dureat and surest remedy obtainable for COUGHS and COLDS INFLUENZA COLDS lIILDREWS COUGH: BAD BREATHING BRONCHITIS BLOOD SPITTING CHRONIC ASTHMA WEAK LUNGS Price Om, Ifl and 2/9, of all Chemtatq.</p>
        <p>By Philip Snowden, M.P. THE WORLD'S GREATEST WORKER'S PARTY.</p>
        <p>The expected but still very wonderful victories which were won in the German Elections last Friday by the Social Democrats have set all the world talking about this great and growing working class party, which has proved by its electoral strength to be by far the largest political party in the German Empire. It occurs to me that it might be of interest to the readers of this column to give a few facts about the history and the organisation of this extraordinary proletarian party. The figures which are already to hand show that the Social Democrats will have polled probably over 3,500,000 votes at the elections held last week. No other party will have polled much more than half that number of votes, but owing to the unequal distribution of seats to population the Social Democrats have a smaller number of mem- bers than some other parties. There has been no redistribution of seats since 1869, and in the meantime the urban population has vastly increased. The SoCial Democrats have captured five out of the six seats in Berlin nn the first ballots by enormous majorities, and they go to a Second Ballot for the sixth seat. THE FIRST BEGINNING.</p>
        <p>The German Social Democratic Party may be said to date its first beginnings from a meeting which was addressed by Lassalle, a great popular leader, at a working men's club in Berlin, on the 12th April, 1862, although it was nine or ten years later before the movement definitely took its present form. At this meeting Lassalle advocated the formation of a definite working class political party to achieve the industrial freedom of the workers. For this he was arrested and imprisoned, but on his release he published his famous. Open Letter to the German Workmen, lin which he outlined the policy of a workers' political party. This led to the formation 'of the Working Men's Association which a few years later joined with an association which had been founded by Marx and Engels, and by this alliance the modern German Social Democratic Party formed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>PROGRESSIVE ADVANCE.</head>
        <p>PROGRESSIVE ADVANCE.</p>
        <p>It would be a long story to go through the record of the persecution and imprisonment which the leaders of the movement suffered in building up this great party, which is now admitted by everybody to have the future destinies of Germany in its hands. Since 1871 when the party first began to take part in the Reichstag elections, its electoral progress has been practically an unbroken record of advance. In 1874 the party returned ten members to the Reichstag, polling 450,000 votes. At the last elections in 1907 the Socialist vote had risen to 3,251,000. The organisation of the German Social Democratic Party is marvellous. It is an ever-present reproach to the British Socialist movement. The German seems to take more naturally to discipline and organisation. No doubt the military training of the males has something to do with this. The organisation of the party is so complete and perfect that in Berlin, with its population of two millions, a circular can be put into every house in the city at any time within six hours of the decision to do so being arrived at, and all by voluntary labour.</p>
        <p>THE TRADE UNION MOVEMENT. In addition to the representation of Social Democracy in the Reichstag, the party has 186 members in the State Diets and Lantage (Parliaments.) There are 4789 representa- tives on 1779 rural councils, or 6157 repre- sentatives in all. German Trade Unionism is mainly a development of the Social Democratic movement. In the last ten years the German trade unions have forged ahead in a most extraordinary manner. There are three principal groups of trade unions—the Social Democratic, the "Christian," and the " Hersch-Duncker." The membership of each group was as follows at the end of 1900 and of 1910 :</p>
        <p>Social B i rEch- Year. Democratic Christian Duncker 1900 ... 677,510 ... _84,667 ... 96,765</p>
        <p>1910 ... 2,077,298 ... 295:129 ... 122,571 It will be seen that the membership of the Social Democratic trade unions has risen by 200 per cent, in ten years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>SOCIALIST MEMBERSHIP.</head>
        <p>THE SOCIALIST MEMBERSHIP.</p>
        <p>Turning back to the political Social Democratic Party some facts may be given about the widespread activities and the remarkable organisation of the movement. The party has an organised membership of 836,532 men and women—the women number- ing 107,693. The minimum subscription for members is 4d. per month for men and 2d. for women. There are 381 branches of the party in the Empire, and these branches are sub-divided into 3,300 groups. The party has 120 paid secretaries devoting all their time to the work of the movement. THE PARTY PRESS.</p>
        <p>This week the British Labour Party has issued the prospectus of a company to be formed to establish and publish a daily news- paper. The German Social Democrats run 82 daily papers! Some of these are not very imposing productions, it is true, but a num- ber of the Sacialist papers are quite up to the highest standard of German journalism. The leading Berlin Socialist daily, " Vorwarts," is a journal of very high standing, and is extremely well informed on political and international questions. It has a circulation of 150,000 copies a day. The party owns no less than 57 printing offices. Its publications are not confined to serious journals. It issues an illustrated weekly which has a circula- tion of about half a million copies, and also a comic journal of which over 250,000 copies are sold. In addition to these, it publishes a weekly scientific journal and a woman's paper. The publication department provides an income of many thousand pounds a year for the propaganda and organising work of the Party. • A COMPACT PARTY.</p>
        <p>The German Social Democratic Party has managed to keep fairly free from the internal disruptions which have rent the Socialist movement in other continental countries and in Great Britain. The German Socialist movement has had the great advantage of possessing men of outstanding ability and capacity, who have kept the movement together by their intellectual power. The' absence of any real Parliamentary Govern- ment in Germany, and the persecution to which the Socialists have been subjected, have also helped greatly to keep the move- ment intact and solid. But in recent years there has developed a good deal ot intellectual dissension in the party on questions of dogma and policy. The new movement is a revolt against a rigid application of Marxian principles, or of a construction which has been put upon Marxian teaching. The</p>
        <p>" Revisionist " movement, as the new activity is called, is more opportunist in its policy, and is willing to use existing means to bring about partial reforms,rather than to wait until the forces of Socialism are so strong that they can overthrow the present ruling classes and assume control of govern- ment. The latter policy is strong where there is no real legislative power in the Parliament as in Prussia, but in the South States, where there is a freer Parliamentary system, the Revisionist policy is making great headway. Where there is any chance to influence legislation it is being taken advantage of by the Socialist deputies. There is not likely to be any serious trouble in the party from these differences, which will settle themselves as matters of government develop. The German Socialist Party is quite free from the revolutionary sentiment which has done so much to injure the Socialist movement in France and Italy. It is a constitutional party, seeking the con- quest of political power by the proletariat by political education and organisation. This German Social Democratic Party is the most impressive thing in world politics. It is probably the most important thing in Europe to-day, because Germany dominates the European situation, and Social Democracy bids fair to dominate Germany in the not- distant future. As a branch of the Inter- national Socialist movement it stands before all else for international peace.</p>
        <p>GOODALL St. STOCKTAKING SALE NOW PROCEEDING AT KING STREET MANCHESTER..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>IN WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>IN WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Five Years Ago and To-day</p>
        <p>A Warrington woman, whose personal excerience of years ago was rep oled in the local tress, now has something of great interest to add. Mrs. E. Howard, who lives at 3, O'Leary Street, Orford-lane, tw o doors from the Orford Barracks, and opposite the Bearont Schools, Warringt3n, says : "It was a cold that brought on my kidney trouble. It settled in the kidneys, and I began to suffer with cruel pains in the small of my back. After stooping it was a great struggle for me to get upright again. In bed I cou'd hardly turn over. Often I cried out with the pain. "I heard that Doan's backache pills were a good kidney medicine, so I obtained some and started to use them. It was not long before my back felt easier, and a course of .he pills soon put me right again. "I am quite free from the pains now, at.d have lost that tired, drowsy feeling I used to have all the time. I shall always speak well of Doan's pills (Signed) (Mrs.) E. Howard." Orer fire years later, Mrs. Howard said:—" I derived lasting benefit from Doan's pills. I have often told my friends how much Food they did me. I have occasionally used the pills since I gave my testimonial, when I have bad a touch of backache, and they have always given me relief." 2/9 a box, 6 boxes 13/9; of all Dealers. Ask for DOAN'S Backache Kidney Pills, like Mrs. Howard had</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>CORPORAL PUNISHMENT OF SCHOOL CHILDREN.</head>
        <p>CORPORAL PUNISHMENT OF SCHOOL CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>Cheshire Educationists' Varied View.</p>
        <p>A MINIhIUM AGE.</p>
        <p>- --- Corporal punishment was discussed by the Cheshire Education Committee at Crewe on Monday. Professor Gonner brought forward several amendments recom• mended by the Elementary Schools Sub-committee. These proposed that corporal punishment should be administered by the head teacher only subject to the following :—" The cummittee strongly deprecate the punishment of girls, but where such punishment is necessary in schools where there is no head mistress it shall be administered by the senior assistant mistress. That the attention of teachers be called to the fact that all punishments, however slight, must be entered in the punishment book ; that corporal punishment shall not be inflicted on children under eight; that all blows by the band or ruler, boxing the ears, striking on any part of the head, and other irregular modes of inflicting corporal punishment are forbidden." Dr. Wilson said he b-lieved the regulations were had for the children, the parents, and the teacher. He pro- p, sed they be referred hack. The Rev. W. Bidlake seconded, and said that if they showed mistaken kindness to children they might spoil them. Colonel Thorpe thought that any restriction of corporal punishment might lead to lack of discipline. "WEALS ON LITTLE CHILDREN." The Chairman (Dr. Hodgson) protested against the description applied to some of the little children—that they were little savages—and said they must be careful to treat children not according to a savage system, but according to civilised methods. They were told the great curse of the present day was lack of discipline, but there WAS A great deal of sentimentality heard on the question. They had abolished flogging in the Army and Navy, and largely also, in their gaols. He had seen the weals on the hands and backs of little children. There was too much of a tendency among males to knock women about, and the committee were seeking to Introduce better methods.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Ilinchcliffeiraested against the proposals as an interference with the freedom of the teacher in the conduct of schools, and an insult to them. The teachers should be allowed discretion, and not have their office degraded in the eyes of parents and children. The authorities had tried this method in Birmingham and Liverpool, and bad gone back to the old regulations. The Rev. J. Grant Bird said there should be as little corporal punishment as possible in their schools and no punishment should be administered at the moment of the offence, or by the person against whom the offence was committed.</p>
        <p>Professor Gooner said Sunday schools had done a good work without any methods of corporal punishment. He did not think the cane was a desirable instrument. Nature had provided the means for corporal punishment. On a vote, it was decided by 15 to it to send the pro- posals back to the committee for further consideration.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN'S NEW VICAR WELCOMED. The new vicar of Runcorn (the Rev. H. N. Perrin) was formally welcomed on Monday night at a largely- attended reception. Speeches were made by the churchwardens and other officials and music was furnished by Nfr. Tonic's string band.</p>
      </div>
