      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>SYMINGTO</head>
        <p>SYMINGTO</p>
        <p>)N'S/ ,sence. N d.g-ton co., London. IL</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, January, 20, 1912.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, January, 20, 1912.</p>
        <p>177frest aatlng tram years and yeals "6"3-7 and he might be making the journey only to have all hid worst fears realised. But there was the Bostock business. The man had proved himself a liar. He had been in Manchester, that was certain ; and Mrs. Caulfield had been anxious that he should return there at once. Yet to convict her of all • the falseness and treachery in- vdived appeared almost cowardly, consider- ing the flimsy nature of the present evidence. and her former earnest help in the search for Helen.</p>
        <p>He was utterly baffled, and by the time he reached the great cotton capital his mind was in a whirl of conflicting thoughts. When the cab set him down at 533, Ex- change-street, he found that there were several sets of offices in the building, and he was eaually at a loss to know which was the one ha sought, and for whom to ask. His only course was to go to them all in turn and ask for Helen, as Miss Paxton or Miss Powell. It</p>
        <p>Mr. Stevenson's office wa6 on the first floor ; but the door Was locked. Mr. Steven- son had gone to Liverpool, and had sent Ilelen off to Backpool and given- the clerk a holiday. Bub-tier, after knocking once or twice, was turning away when Arnold Stevenson, who had been very diligent in his efforts to make his peace with -his uncle, and had to some extent succeeded, came up the stc.'reaee. _</p>
        <p>"Do you want Mr. Stevenson?" he asked. "I am bids nephew." "I wish to see Miss Paxton or Mirs Powell,' said I3ulmer. "Can you tell me if I shall find here here?"</p>
        <p>"1 think I can help you," said Arnold readily. "As the office i 6 shut, will you come to -mine? It is close by." Bulmer was only too glad to agree, and they went out together, Arnold being full of curiosity as to what a man of Bulmer's evi- dent respectability could want with his uncle's typist. "You asked for Miss Paxton or Mb-a Powell," he said, when they were in his office.</p>
        <p>"I should tell you who I am first," said Bulmer. "My name is Buttner Trevellock, of Trevellock Chase. It is Miss Paxton I wish to find—to see, I mean." He made the correction quickly, but his companion noticed it, of course. "She is here as MisS Powell, but I happen to know that in London she was Miss Paxton. I know that, because I have a friend in London, Mr. Garwood, a solicitor, and Ghe was in his emplovtneot no a typist." "It was in L'Ondon I knew her; but I forget Mr. Garwood's address." Bulmer had not failed to see that underneath Arnold Stevenson's courtesy to himself there was . a suggestion of hostility toward Helen. Arnold gave the address, and added: "Do you know the circumstances under which she left my friend's office?" "No. I was in America at the time, and I have not seen her since. She has been here in Manchester since then, I presume?" "I believe so. She has been with my uncle for some months now." "I am very much obliged to you," said Bulmer, rising. "Perhaps you can tell. me where she lives here?"</p>
        <p>"Yea, I think I can. I'll look it up in a moment if you will wait," said Arnold, who was desperately anxious to find out what Bulmer wanted with Helen, and whether the thing could not he turned to account in his own interest. "We all take a great intemst in Miss Powell ; and if there is any other in- formation I can give you, I shall be de- Babied." "Beyond her private address there is nothing, thank you," Was the reply, and, having obtained it, he left. "I don't like that fellow any more than he likes Helen," was Bulmer's comment as he walked away;. "but he's given me the Gar- wood connection, and I'll follow it up at once."</p>
        <p>,He went to the telephone exchange, found Garwood's number in London, and rang him up. But Mr. Garwood was very cautious and uncommunicative. He admitted that Miss Helen Paxton had been in his employ- ment, but would not say why she had loft. Bulmer explained who he was, and that be was a great friend of Helen's; but Gar- wood's reply was that, although be would be glad of a personal interview, he could not speak freely over the wire. He added, how- ever, in reply to further questions, that in London Miss Paxton had lived in West Ken- sington, that be thought highly of her, and did not know her reasons for baying gone to Manchester in the name of Powell. With this Bulmer was more than eon- t‘mted. It was clearly Helen herself, and also clear that she was not married.</p>
        <p>Nothing else mattered, and as he drove to the address which Arnold Stevenson had given him, all the thoughts of the cunning complications and intrigue by which they had been kept apart, were lost in the one supreme fact—he had found her again, and she was free to be his wife.</p>
        <p>At the house there was a disappointment. Her landlady said that Helen was away, and v...)uld not be back until an uncertain time in the evening. She had gone to Blackpool. She had been looking worried and troubled for a few days' and was sadly in need of a breath of fresh air. Did she know where he would be likely to find her at Blackpool? Scarcely; but probably on the front some- where. She had no friends there to whose house she was likely to go. By the next train Bulmer was off to Black- pool, and during the journey he had time to think of that strange and circumstantial story of the marriage with a Californian millionaire. How could it have ever appeared in that Pocatello paper? Who could have sent it? Whose interest was it to coin that elaborate lie? Who had the knowledge of the facts with which it had been so cunningly garnished? He knitted his brows as he reflected that there was but one source from which it could have emanated. The Chase. And who at the Chase? In heaven's name not his own father! The thought revolted him. It was impos- sible. He could not—he would not believe it. . .</p>
        <p>Yet if not Sir Gregory, then surely there was only one other person possible. The woman he still was pledged to marry. That thought was scarcely less revolting. A few hours ago he would have scouted it as a shame to her, and have struck down the man who had suggested it. But there was Bostock, and the mysterious connection between the two. Bostock could have found the means to get the lie printed; money would probably do all that was needed, and his ally could provide both the information and the money. _</p>
        <p>He reviewed all the events of the time when he had been searching for Helen; Mrs. Caulfield's sympathy and help; her eagerness in the work—the proof of her disinterested friendship, as he had then deemed it; the various efforts she had made; and it did not seem within the bounds of possibility that it could all have been no more than one long, elaborate; consistently acted lie. And that was the woman who would have been his wife within a few days but for the accidental sight of the letter in his father's study that morning! He was still pledged to her, indeed. His last words to her before leaving the Chase had been that if he had wronged her in his suspicions he would make her ample amends. What if he were ,wrong? If she was inno- cent of all this, and ho had to blame Sir Gregory? He would then have to keep his word. The only amends he could make were</p>
        <p>to marry her. To marry her, dieir intigh he had found Helen. The thought of that was distracting,' and then another followed. Why had Helen her- self fled from him? He remembered her strangely cold letter when she had pleaded that business would prevent her meeting him on the eve of his departure for the States. They had parted after that day on the river on the usual terms, and he could not recall a single syllable or act of his or hers to account for any estrangement. Yet she was not a girl to act in such a way without a powerful reason? Or at any rate what stood to her for one, and what that was he would know before many hours were over their. heads. He made his way straight from the station to the magnificent parade—one of the finest sea walks in the world—and was disconcerted by its great length and the number of people upon it. A search offered him little hope of success.</p>
        <p>But fortune befriended him. With the object of walking the entire length, he went urn to the north end, and there, just past the end of the hulking he saw her, sitting alone on a tiny plateau of the cliff, looking thoughtfully out over the sea. With a quickened pulse and brightening eye he made his way to the scot. She sprang to her feet the instant she saw him, the colour flooded her face, and then as suddenly fled, leaving her whit; . agitated, and shaken.</p>
        <p>"Butiner !" "I have found you," he said, stretching out both his hands.</p>
        <p>But she drew back, and instead of pl-acin,% her own in his, she clenched them and pressed them to her bosom, gazing at him in silence, her featurcs strained and her cycti troubled and _emo.tio_nryl._</p>
        <p>He let his hands fall ;lowly. "You will not speak to me. You shrink from me?" She leant back against the cliff, her face now hidden in her hands, while she fought for self-control. The sight of him and the sound of his voice had roused once again all the old love which she had been struggling to subdue in all the months of their separation. She realised that he knew nothing of the fearsome barrier which stood between them, and that he had sought her in all good faith and ignorance. The thought of the blow she had to infliet, the cruelty of which her own sufferings made her understand, rent her until she shook and quivered under the shock of it like one in a palsy. "Helen, he appealed again. She could not calm herself to reply. Sl_e could not trust herself even to look at him again, until she had fought down this mad- denin,7 sinful desire to throw her.elf into his arms and find there the comfort for which her heart had been hungering during all the weary months of separation. Ile would read in her eyes and hear in her voice the secret which must now be kept from him —from him more closely • than horn all the world. "But what have I done to deserve this?" he asked. Then a thought of the possible truth flashed upon. him. "Heaven help me, can it be true that after all you are another man's wife? Helen !" All the passion of his strong heart was in the cry. She was still silent; and for an instant grasped at his words as offering a way of escape. Should she let him think her false? But the next instant she saw the futility of such an attempt. He would find out the truth easily; and she would be but postpon- ing the blow she had to deal. Her courage rose in rebellion against such a course Then in a voice of relief he said: "No, I Fee- that it is not it. You wear no ring. Thank God. Not even an engagement ring. Thank God, I say, for that. There is no bar between us. But—"_</p>
        <p>With a supreme effort she rallied her strength to stop him from any further plea. She took her hands from her face, now white like death, and despite her quivering lips mastered her voice keeping it steady and firm, almost cold. "Stay," she said, raising her trembling hand in protest. "Xou should not have sought me. Oh, why have you not been told? Oh, how cruel!" Lost in amazement., he listened, and when she paused, broke in. "Been told? Been told what, Helen? .Why did you fly from me and hide yourself?" Again she raised her hand in protest. "You said there was no bar between us. That proved Sir Gregory had said nothing to you." "My father. Do you mean that he has parted us?" She paused and passed her hand across her brow. 'You must have courage, Bulmer; great courage." His eyes lighted at the use of his name; and seeing it, she shook her head. "You do not understand. I may call you Bulmer but—but it is because I am—your sister."</p>
        <p>"Helen!" he cried starting back. "It is true, Dulmer; too true. Sir Gregory, is my father just ads he is yours." - For an instant he was silent, unable to bc•- Here it, every sense and hope and instinct in hot rebellion. _</p>
        <p>"It cannot be true. My God, it cannot!" he eriod:•" "You must be brave, as I said, Bulrner. Sir Gregory himself told me after you had gone away. I feared it from something you said when we met last. That was why I could not see you again before you went, and why I fled from you afterwards, beg- ging you never to seek me. It is only too true, too horribly true." The blow had struck him with all the deadly cruelty she had feared, and he reeled before the shock of it, speechless, horrified, and broken-hearted.</p>
        <p>(To be continued.)</p>
        <p>IV-•••^0,-‘,j</p>
