      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>OUR SERIAL STORY.</head>
        <p>OUR SERIAL STORY.</p>
        <p>CALL RIGHTS RESERVED.] (Copyrighted in the bnited Slates of America by Arthur W. Marchmont.). SIR GREGORY'S SILENCE</p>
        <p>BY ARTHUR W. MARCHMONt, Aithor of "The Man Who Was llead," "By Right of Sword," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XXIII.</p>
        <p>ON THE TRACK OF THI TRUTH.</p>
        <p>"Are you better, Eft?" asked &amp;diner, breaking the silence. "Yes, a little. I have had one or two at- tacks of this. Get me a little brandy, my boy." As Bulmer stepped back Sir Gregory stretched out his hand eagerly to recover possession of the letter; and noticing the ges- ture, Bulmer picked it up and carried it away with him. "Is that—my letter, or—one of yours, Be!- seer?"</p>
        <p>Bulmer pretended not to hear the question, and getting the brandy poured out half a wineglassful. "Isn't that my letter, my boy?" repeated his father.</p>
        <p>"Yes, sir. Will you drink this? I wish to speak to you::</p>
        <p>-Sir Grekory's fingers shook as he rai,ed the glatis to his lips; and as he set it down he looked up nervously at his son.</p>
        <p>"As I 'came across to you just now, my eyes fell on the first sentence of this letter, sir, and I could not help reading it. It is about Helen. I shall have to ask you to allow me to read it all through." - "I have not read it myself yet, Bulmcr. Give it me."</p>
        <p>"As it is about Helen, sir, you will see that it is a letter I ought to read, I am sure." "Give it to me, if you please," replied Sir Gregory, with some severity. "My dear father, never in all our lives have we had a serious disagreement. And I think I can say that .I have always en- deavoured to show you the obedience and consideration a son •should show to his father. But you must not ask me not to read this."</p>
        <p>"You have no right to look at my letters, Bulmer. Give it to me, I say." "Will you return it to me when you have read it?'</p>
        <p>"Certainly I will make no such conditions. It is a dishonourable thing you arc doing. Give it to me at once, I repeat." Bnlmer considered for a moment in great distress of mind; and then handed the letter to Sir Gregory, who immediately placed it in a drawer and turned the key. "You have angered me, Bulmer. I did not think my son would have acted so." Bulmer sat down and passed his hand across his forehead in thought. "You decline to let me see it?" he asked very quietly. "You have both surprised and angered me. I will not discuss the matter with you any further. I wish to be alone."</p>
        <p>"You are keeping soirething from me, sir. What is it? And why do you do it? I must know it, please. I have a right. The letter spoke of Helen as Miss Paxton, or Powell. Does that mean that she is not married? You know what this means to me. More than my life. You must tell me."</p>
        <p>"Yen are forgetting yourself, Dulmer, to speak to me like that." "I hope not, sir. I am trying to be abso- lately calm. I ask you to Uri me all there is to know of Helen, instead of leaving me to find it out."</p>
        <p>......_ ...., ........ "I will not discuss it with you while you are in this mood. Leave me. You can see I am not in a fit state to be worried now."</p>
        <p>After a pause Bulmer rose with a sigh and left the room. Not since he had been a boy had he and his father had any quarrel; and he would not make one now, if it could be avoided. But despite all his outward calm- n?ss and self-control, his blood was boiling at the thought that his father should be in possession of information about Helen, should know, as it seemed, that she was not mar- ried, and yet should keep him in ignorance. There must indeed be something behind; and what it was he would find out. As he crossed the hall he heard Bostock's voice. "I will write you from Manchester," and the next moment Mrs. Caulfield came running down the stairs.</p>
        <p>The leading of the letter had been an ad- ess in Manchester, "533, Exchange-street," and in a flash the chain of suspicions was welded together in his mind. He had in- tended to go to his mother to get her to ob- tain the letter from Sir Gregory; but at this he changed his plan. "I want to speak to you," he said to Mrs. Caulfield as she came smiling towards him with outstretched hands.</p>
        <p>"Not more than I to speak to you always, dear," she replied, gaily; but the look on his face showed her that something was very much amiss.</p>
        <p>"Come in here a moment, please," he said gravely, opening the door of a small room next the drawing-room, and closing it care- fully after him. "Is anything the matter, dearest?" she asked nervously. "I want you to tell me how long you have known that Miss Paxton was not married and that she was living in Manchester?" "Buimer! What on earth do you mean?" His expression had warned her, and the long question gave her time to affect intense as- tonishment. "Tell me, please," he said almost sternly. "I don't know anything of the sort. And I don't understand you in the least. I know no more about Miss Paxton's. whereabouts than you do, nor about her marriage." "You pledge me your word of honour on that?"</p>
        <p>"You are very strange; but of course I</p>
        <p>do." "There is one other matter. Mr. Garwood, the London solicitor, what do you know of .him?" "The New York lawyer, you mean. I told -you recently that he has some affairs of mine in hand. Why are you asking me all this - and in such a tone? As if I were a criminal, Balmer." "If my manner is strange, you must for- sive inc. I have had some very disturbing sews this morning." "I don't think that is any excuse for .talking to me like this. I have never been ''.spoken to in such a way before in all my life. It—it breaks my—my heart—from you of all :'men," she faltered, on the verge of giving • way. "I would not willingly do you an injus- tice." But you have done it. You have accused me in your thoughts or—or you couldn't have treated roe so." "I believe, I, have wronged you. I was hasty. Forgive me." Ile was impressed by -her emphatic and ready denial and by her 'evident agitation. • She threw herself into his arms. "I would , rather die than have you angry with me like this, dearest. I love you so. It kills me to foel you think such things of me. I love you •so. I love you so"; and she drew his head down and kissed him passionately. But as soon as he was alone, he remem- bered he had not questioned her about the •remark he had overheard from Bostoek—that he would write to her from Manchester; and t• an his doubts sprung to life again. If he was right, and Bostock had been in any way con- cerned with her, the denials she had made so readily and passionately rendered the case •, infinitely blacker. . He went straight from Mrs. Caulfield •to search for Bostock, and found him packing his bag. "You are leaving us, Mr. . said as he entered. Bostock turned in surprise.</p>
        <p>Bostock?" he</p>
        <p>"Yes. I'm</p>
        <p>'LlazsEitn Comror ND for Coughs end Colds. A proven einem; 9jd. &amp; Is. Id. Of Chemistsonly.-Advt</p>
        <p>o 5 again. Had a letter this morning calling me to town."</p>
        <p>"That's singular," replied Bulmer quietly. "I was with my father when the letter-bag was opened and there waa nothing for you." "Did I say this morning? Stupid of mc. It came last night, of course." "3carcely that either. The last post here is in the afternoon, and at dinner you wer3 planning a ritle to Overcross Abbey," - "Quite correct; sir. So I was. But Pie been thinking things over, and find that I must get away at once." "We may travel together, perhaps. I am thinking of going to Manchester." "The devil you are," exclaimed Bostock, in some dismay; but he recovered himself instantly and laughed. "I should be de- lighted, Mr. Trevellock, but I am going to London."</p>
        <p>"Oh. I thought you were going to Man- chester, becalm.- I heard you tell Mrs. Caul- field. you would write her from there."</p>
        <p>"You'll excuse me, but you must liav‘ made a mistake. There's a lot of this little country of yours that I like. but not Man- chester. No, sir," and he, shuddered as if the thought of the place even were un- pleasant. "Well, when I come to think of it, Lon- don will suit me almost as well. I want you to do me a little favour and introduce me to your friend Mr. Garwood, the solicitor." _ .</p>
        <p>"Garwood? Garwood? A solicitor?" re- peated Bostock, as if not understanding. "I never heard the name before in my life. Gar- wood?"</p>
        <p>"I think you are a little overdoing it, Mr. Bostock. Think again." "My dear sir, I assure you I never heard the name before." Bostock was a very sturdy liar, and spoke with unblushing cool- ness.</p>
        <p>"Again I ask you to think. I know that you know him." "There may be some fellow of that name at my club—there are three or four thousand of us; and I may have heard of him and for- gotten." "Well, I invite you to remember him and tell me what your connection with him has been—in regard particularly to Miss Helen Paxton."</p>
        <p>Bostock laughed lightly, and threw up his hands. "You have me. I don't know what the devil you're shooting at. But you're a hundred miles from any bull's eye. Anyway, you must excuse me now. I have to catch this train," and he stooped down over his bag again. • Bulmer's eyes followed the movement, Lad saw on it the label of the Regina Hotel, Manchester.</p>
        <p>"Do you always stop at the Regina in Manchester?" he asked sharply. "Oh, that label. This is a friend's bag, Mr. Trevellock," was the ready excuse. "I find myself in a some what awkward poeition, Mr. Bostock. You art a gueg hero and courtesy stands in the way of free speech. But my father told me you had had some dealings with a London solicitor named Garwood; and Mrs. Caulfield said that some of her matters were conducted through you with a Mr. Garwood. You now say that you never heard the name. Can you explain that?"</p>
        <p>"I don't intend to try, Mr. Trevellock. If your father or Mrs. Caulfield wishes to talk to you about Mr. Garwood, they can do so; but Jasper Bostock isn't in the habit of talk- ing to order about anyone or to anyone." "Do you still deny you have ever heard Mr. Garwood's name?" • Buhl-lees tone was very calm, but his eyes were signalling danger, as Bostock perceived. "I must decline to discuss the matter with you at all, Mr. Trevellock." "You will see that your denial imputes falsehood both to Mrs. Caulfield and to my father," continued Bulmer in the same level tone. "That, of course, I cannot take from you or anyone. I no longer ask you to ex- plain, but I require it, sir.'' Bostock straightened himself and met Bul- mer's look steadily. "Well, I guess you can go on requiring it all you please," ho said in his most deliberate drawl.</p>
        <p>The two men stood staring at one another during a pause of some moments; and in the silence someone knocked at the door. Bul- mer opened it and found Mrs. Caulfield's maid with a message that her mistress wished .to see Bostock at once.</p>
        <p>"Just say I'm busy, Mr. Trevellock, will you?" said Bostock. Bulmer understood the message—that it was intended to warn Mrs. Caulfield and put her on her guard. He was more than ever persuaded that the two were in collusion, and without saying a word more to Bostock he left the room. Hd called the maid back and said he would take the message and went to , Mrs. Caul- field's sitting-room. She was pacing up and down the room in great agitation, and her back was to the door as he entered. Think. big that it was Bostock she exclaimed, "You must return to Manchester at once and " Then she turned and saw that it was Bulmer.</p>
        <p>He stood and looked at her steadily. "I know all that is behind that sentence. I have just been closeted with your—your friend. I am going to Manchester, and can take any message you wish to Miss Paxton." "Bulmer !" she cried, going towards him. He drew back. "Not until this is all cleared up, if you please," he said coldly. "Bulmer, I swear to you you are wronging me," she declared passionately. "In that case I will make you ample amends afterwards ;" and he turned and left the room. Just outside he met Bostock. "Mrs. Caulfield's orders for you were given inadvertently to me, sir. You are to return —to return—to Manchester at once. We may meet there, but as you will not then be a guest, you may find it more prudent to avoid me."</p>
        <p>Bostock stared after him as he ran down- stairs, then whistled, shrugged his shoulders, smiled—and swore.</p>
        <p>Bulmer went to his father's study. "I think it right to let you know, sir, that I saw the address of that lettet which you re- fused to show me, and that I am going there at once. I find that there has been some very underhand work going on in regard to Miss Paxton, and fear that Mrs. Caulfield has been concerned in it. I know that that fellow Bostock has, and when I return I shall have the proofs." "I know nothing of it, Bulmer, if that is what you mean to imply." "I have not suggested it. I pray to heaven you have not. It would part us, father, and break my mother's heart.' With a suppressed groan Sir Gregory I buried his face in his hands. "You had better know all I have to tell you, Bulmer," he said at length, in a low, tense tone. Get- ting no reply, he looked round to find that Bulmer had left the room. He sent for him at once; but the servant returned to say that Bulmer had had his horse saddled, and had galloped off to the station.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XXIV. "I AM TOUR SISTER." Dulmer reached Manchester in the after- noon, and during the hours. of the tedious cross-country journey he had harassed and worried himself into a condition of feverish ucitement. The reference to Helen in the letter and his father's strange conduct on receipt of it; his suspicions of underhand Work which it had engendered; his belief, at first negatived and then confirmed, that Mrs. Caulfield was in some way connected with it; the uncertainty as to what he was going to iiscover when he reached his destination • all these recurred to him and moved him alter- aatively to the extremes of anger and alarm, hope and despair. He had rushed away' on the journey in obedience to an irresistible impulse, but ho had scarcely taken his seat in the train be- !ore he realised how little the one sentence )f the letter which he had read had told him. tic did not even know that Helen was at Hanchester,,sor whether she was married or single. The writer of the letter, whose name he had not seen, had expressed himself as being interested in "Miss Helen Paxton or That was all. It „might be au</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>HOCKEY.</head>
        <p>HOCKEY.</p>
        <p>LATC II FO R D.</p>
        <p>The First Eleven won 3-0 against Clayton last week In a game that was devoid of anything very noteworthy except some unnecessarily heavy hitting by the visitors backs at times. Following a speedy run on the right wing by Dale, an accurate cootie was cleverly con- verted by Travis and before half time Pearson increased the lead with a neat ohot. Parker scored the third goal in the second half. Allanson and Cline played very well at full-back for the home team and gave the Clayton forwards little chance of getting through Several movements by the visitors were spoiled through Indiscreet hitting by the backs. The Second Eleven, thanks to the terrific speed of the trains between Arpley and Stockport arrived at the Stockport ground an hour late after an exasperating journey. Stockport had a very strong eleven out and gct a lead of 2 goals before half time. In the second half the play Nvas very close and iklinards reduced the lead but Stockport scored again and won 3-1 after a keenly contested game. The Stockport forwards were very smart and indulged in some good combined movement that required some stopping their left wing in parti- cular being most prominent. Bradshaw and Wright played soundly for the visitors in defence but the attack for once was weal, . _</p>
        <p>The First Eleven v. West Kirby away this afternoon is :—T Dixon, goal ; Allanson, W H Pickles, backs ; W Fletcher, J B Wilkinson, A. Knight, halves ; It A Dale, J Pearson, D Travie,.ll Parker, E Banks, forwards. Train from Central Station 1.9 p.m. The Second Eleven v Ashley at Latchford is :—II Wright, goal ; R Walsh, R Parkinson, backs ; H Eccles, A E Bradshaw, W Isherwood, halve 11 It Colton, II Fletcher, W II Minards,T Banks, A Starkey, forwards. Bu Ily-cfl 2.15 p.m.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>EX-FOOTBALLER'S FATE.</head>
        <p>EX-FOOTBALLER'S FATE.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn on Tuesday, Mr. Ridgway held an irquvst on the body of George Wood, aged thirty-six, en ex-member of the Runcorn Rugby Club, whose body was found on the railway outside Runcorn station early on Sunday morning. Mrs. Wood stated that her husband was the licensee of the Union Vaults, Union- street, and he also worked as a weighman. On Saturday afternoon she had a few sharp words with him, and he left the house in a temper. He did not return. A guard named Robert Joseph Roberts deposed to finding the body near the goods yard. The head was severed from the body, and was lying in the four-foot. The body was perfectly straight, and there were no signs of a struggle. A single ticket from Liverpool to Runcorn was found in hie possession. John Grounds, stationmaster, said every inquiry had been made, but they bad been unable to find out by which train the deceased returned to Runcorn. The jury returned a verdict of "Suioide whilst Of unsound mind."</p>
      </div>
