      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>Clergy and tratrimony.—Clergyiren's children are, according 'to an old proverb, no better than those of other people, if as good, and there is another e 53 ing that it is the "fool of the family" who goes into the Church. Possibly it is these old and silly traditions that have moved Mrs Ruth Jackson to advocate celibacy for the Anglican clergy. It is, at any rate, not easy to understind why anyone of the nature of a self-constituted authority, should want to impose the cheerless conditions of bachelorism upon the clergy of the Protestant Churches. The doctrine that family ties are a hindrance, is accepted readily enough nowadays, without encouraging it still further with examples of this sort. Moreover, the vicar's wife, as a rule, does an immense amount of good in parish work. There may be here and there an exception, but it is not true, as Mrs. Jackson says in an article in one of the January magazines, that she is more often than not a trouble- maker. There is this also to be said, that the world would I ave been very much poorer in great men and women if the clergy had never married, for the names of their sons and daughters may be found by the score, amongst the greatest in art, literature, science and in every calling of the Church end State. House-Warming.—Ever sirce coal was first dug out of the earth, purple have found fault with our system of open fireplaces, which warm only one corner of the room, and one part of the human body at a time. There is, however, no (petition that our open grates are more cheerful to look at, and more healthy than the central system of house-heating, despite what the ch ctors -ray about winter colds being attributable to our cold houses. We may sometimes catch cold going from over.heatod rooms into cold passages and bed- rooms, but nowhere are the more serious respiratory diseases—pneumonia and the rest of them—so prevalent rs they are in New York,Vienna, or Dresden, whore the houses ire mostly heated by a basement furnace Ventilation is even more import rat than warmth, but we certainly do want a system which will combine the two, and be less expensive than having open coal fires all over the house.</p>
        <p>The Tobacco Habit.—Some men, it is said, are giving up smoking because it is becoming to effeminate, and the proposal to permit smoking in tho auditorium of theatres, seems likely to further encourage the tobacco habit among women. The fashionable side of New York has only lately permitted women to smoke in public, but the habit has become so general in this country, that there is a demand that the railway com- panies should provide special ace° mmodAti on for women smokers. Nor does such a demand sound far fetched when we commonly have the wives of Cabinet ministers, lighting cigarettes at a public) dinner. Men travel:ing there is no place wh3re a woman can smoke, unless after meals in a dining car, and few are bold enough to enter an ordinary smoking compartment for this purpese. In Salelani.—Needless to say, a good remnant should be secured—at a reduced rate of course—at the sales, Materials are combined and mixed in a multitude of ways, a state of affairs that promises to continue throughout the coming spring, and is most favourable to short lengths of fabric. Short pieces of velvet, sateen, moire, end brocade, can all be utilised by the home milliner, whilst a charming afternoon frock can he evolved from two pieces of satin, silk or cloth, in different shades of the same colour. Blouses, again,can be made from all sorts of oddments by the girl or woman who knows how to combine them and is skilful in the use of her needle and sewing machine. Evening cloaks are also lending themselves to mixtures of plain and broche velvet, plain silk, brocade, silk lace and chiffon, cloth and velvet brocade, or any two materials that blend well. A narrow band of fur finishes tho neck of any of these most becomingly. When buying fur sets at sales, care should he taken in the style of stole and muff, for sinco these ought to last several seasons, they should be in the latest fashion. Also, the sets should not be built of many little pieces, because in such an event it is impossible to alter them satisfactorily. Household g00d,,, such as kitchen utensils, linen and crockery, the housekeeper may buy with profit, for genuine reductions are made in these lines always. The Russian Influsnce.—Rnssian fashions are again itfluencing our own and Russian tunics, blouses and coats, not to mention embroideries, and trimmings, and evening dresses, but whilst the style may be Russian, the materials are European. In Paris, the Russian toque is worn far down on the head, but we prefer the more becoming and picturesque mode of turning it into a kind of bonnet. While the smaller bats and the toqne generally are not greatly needed, the result is that they are not as much in evidence as was the case a year or two ago. There is therefore veyy little in the wt‘y of novelties to record, those veils donned at present, being of flue clear net, frequently edged with lace. 'The rims of many hats are so bent and twisted, that to r,tiess what they were originally would seem impossible. Natural ostrich feathers are quite out of date. The fronds of feathers are knotted to give the desired length, and are more like artificial feathers than any- thing else. Groups of three feathers have freqnently but one stem,the tips only falling in different directions. The Small, Economical Hat.—Perhaps the fact that the small hat is more economical than the large one, accounts for its increasing popularity, despite the prophets who have predicted its banishment for at least three seasons ; thus proving that women individu. ally influence fashions and are not to-day the blind followers of designers and milliners that they were in the past. The small hat admits of infinite variety, for a plain straw, or felt, may have half-a-dezen mounts, each of which makes a different hat of it. Per instance, for morning wear, with a tweed coat and skirt, it may have a m mot of toque feathers, and be in keeping with the suit. Should a change be desired, a wide-flat piece of feather-work in dark green, blue, black will transform it into a feather toque. Bows of ribbon to</p>
        <p>match or contrast with an afternoon tailor-made dress, can be ensily fixed on the same hat in Orme of t feathers. Other mounts to suit various occasions ra consist of velvet flowers, fancy feathers or a pair. wings twisted to form an Alsatian how all of wilt admit of variety in mounts, for the small economic hat.</p>
        <p>The " Rest Clown."— Tea, and "rest gowns are most, elaborate, and perfect in cut. A favourite colour an° material is gold criipe de Chene, but tulle and the Nelli's lace for full dress occasion, over rose-pink is exceed' ingly lovely. White tulle in olmbiaation with blaoli lace, is also most effective, and with these •Abwilo girdles of black velvet make a very delightful finish.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>DOCTORS' AGITATION.</head>
        <p>THE DOCTORS' AGITATION.</p>
        <p>Knutsford Liberal Candidate and the Rate of Pay. Mr. P. M. Oliver, the prospective Liberal candid' for the Knutsford division, spoke at Higher Peover SAturday night. He dealt mainly with the Insure! Act. He said thst it would bring an enormous arnol of good to the country. Its imperfections would hi to be found out and altered; but they would never these out unless they tried it and worked it. Ti 2 was one difficulty before them ; he referred to I doctors The doctors would get 4s. Bd. per head I year, but that was only an average, and the matter v left for agreement betvreen the doctors and the Tart( insurance committees, and if ths dortors refused work the committee would_ have to mite other ter! . .</p>
        <p>He believed that it would be imposstble to fix ace sum, because in Cheshire doctors had to spend a I deal more money than the town doctors ; in toxvns or the slums the doctors had all</p>
        <p>patients round Chem, but it .yr- e;;;;;;--dthe-r-ent" country. The doctors deserved careful consid</p>
        <p>because it took many years to become • doctor, meant not much less than £l,OOO for educstion,and the there was buying a practice. If they could not vrn! for the 4s. 61. he would be in favour of increasing money, lut he should not meet that by incressl contribution* he would do it in other ways. • difficulty with the doctors was one which would b solved simply because it most be solved. Mr. Llof George had got over many hedges, and they belie'' that he would get over this. (Applause.) They orrec too much to the doctors to treat them badly, and bi believed that Mr. Lloyd George and the Liberal 9511 were the first people to recognize that. Proceeding, Mr. Oliver said the measure would affsei the Cheshire farmers. They had to pty the 3d., must be remembered they bed other things besined pockets and clothes; they had got hearts and heads,lo3, that was the way in which farmers ought to regard thi. Act. It was not putting anything directly Into t!).! I pockets, but after all it might mean something. "' measure ought to mean a reduction of the poir (Hear, hear.) Ile was not there to make any ro' promises ; he would leave that to the Tariff Reforniel lie would say that the poor rate ought to come doff but he could not promise that it would; he did not 00,11 trol the rates. Farmers were specially responsible I° their workmen, and in the country men got rheumatiO they got damp, and bad to bend down while working, and in many cases their sickness was caused by 00 employment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>AN ACTUARY ON THE INMAN</head>
        <p>AN ACTUARY ON THE INMAN</p>
        <p>ACT.</p>
        <p>An interesting analysis of the Insurance Act Wst°, given at the Accountants' Hall, Manchester, at a Mee, ing of the Insurance Institute (with Mr. Hugh C. president in the chair), by Mr. W. Worthington, of t_A Royal Insurance Company, Liverpool. Having pointy; out that the Act was complementary to the Old Agf Pensions' Act of 1908, be pointed out that the tateq. contribution had been scientifically constructed by two eminent actuaries employed by the tioverntnee, Mr. H. F. Hardy and Mr. F. B. Wyatt, who ue.,ed their basis the aickness experience of the 7°,7; cheater Unity of Oddfellows, with a memrer"o, exceeding 0110 million, during a series of 3,e,ts combined with the rates of mortality prevair,„, amongst the population of the United Kinga°74 gathered from the Census returns and the register, deaths. These payments would be initially inadeque, to an amount they estimated at a capital sum "R £60,000,000, were it not for the amount to be deduetri from the eekly contributions and invested at compclo interest as a sinking fund to provide in due course lid nt cessary reserves. This the actuaries calculated 10114 be accomplished in about 18 years, when the whole,), the contributions would be available for benefits, wl3'°,4 shonld then be materially increased in such a roarTj as Parliament might determine. He combs the statement of Sir James Barr (president-elect of V's British Medical Association) that the Act wee qr actuarially sound as a charge which could not be eadt,, stnntiated, explaining that all who were medically and and 33 were unable to gain admittance to the " approC_A societies," would be dealt with as deposit contributor;, and as such would not in any way underallee to. actuarial foundations of the national scheme. He CA° eluded with a tribute to the Chancellor of</p>
        <p>Exchequer s genius and pertinacity in steering suncell:: fully to the Statute Book such an elaborate and 0 reaching piece of legislation. A discussion followed, and Mr. Worthington heartily thanked for his address.</p>
