        <p>( .' ' ' . .. - " .., t . . . _ . . ~ . . . ~</p>
        <p>Hatton. EDDING CELEBRATION.—On Tuesday afternoon the arish Room was the scene of another tea. It is not often that weddin,gs are celebrated kefore the event actually takes place, Seeing that the room was dm- /Untied, the billiard table being taken down for the Children's party, Mrs. Kirkham, widow of the lata Mr. ot• Kirkham, Britton Arms, who is shortly to be married ° Mr. J, Leyland, of Stockton Heath, asked for the Ilse of the spacious room, and all the mothers that cat red to coma were invited. A goodly number rolled up full justice was done to an excellent tea. sBtZsZDAY SCHOOL TREAT.—On Monday afternoon the Ltticia9 school scholars and teachers belonging to St. tru'is'a mission, Hatton, were entertained to tea by 11'21. J. Whitley of Ilatton Cottage. The youngsters ?u a right royal time of it. After tea presents were :4113.tributed to the children, and to wind up a conjurer "llghted the youngsters with a sample of his skill. Atkinson's Cafe catered.</p>
        <p>Cuerdley. FARM FIRE.—The Warrington Fire Brigade was .alled out at 8 o'clock on Monday evening to an ent- reat( at Cuerdley Hall farm in the occupation of Mr. Rftroa Harrison. Under the direction of Deputy "uPerintendent Turner,the brigade turned out promptly, 1121 when they arrived at the scene of the conflagration h• eY fonnd the shippon, stables and barn in flames. The zuildings were destroyed, the damage, which is covered " insurance' being estimated at several hundred —„?ande. Fortunately the animals had been safely "moved. The brigade are to be complimented upon ',l:n3fining the fire to the buildings in which it broke out. ratal it spread to the seven bays of hay and corn the j„e,ge would have been most serious. The outbreak ra- a,"eged to have been the work of an incendiary as a was seen running away fram the farm when the "re Was first discovered. Fearnhead. RISLEY INDEPENDENT METHODIST CHIHWIL—The ;untie' prize distritution took place on Saturday night Vi_st in the Sunday school Mr. Thomas Bold occupied tr_ chair, and in his address spoke of the usefulness of Sabbath school and commented ou the perseveranca 'lit the children who, were the successful recipients of v.Prize. Mrs. J. Dilliway and Miss B. Bate kindly gave tuial Prizes to the children representing the old school s t the new school. Twenty-five first prizes were _ ributed, which in itself speaks for the good attend- :title !Ilea of the scholars as 100 marks have to be made to et a child to a first prize, thres second prizes were ,aerited and 4 thirds. After the distribution Miss B. te responded very nicely to a vote of thanks, then all the Sunday scholars partook of refreshments which bad ellen provided by the young people, who catered very vaillentlY. Songs and recitations were given by qi_cule children and adults a very profitable ani enjoy- ' evening being spent.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath. orHILL CLIFFS AND STOCKTON HEATH UNITED BAND 11°PE.—This society held its fourth entertainment the session in the Wesleyan Schoolroom on Monday ser,"ePing• The R.v, George Curtis, president of the ad-c,'etY, occupied the chair and delivered a short The programme was given entirely by the 112:tubers and friends of Hill Cliffs Sunday School and 3 greatly zaaster enjoyed. Pianoforte solos were given by Cryil Pemberton and Mr. C. Bostock and latcitations by Agnes Philps, Alice Mone, Violet Ashton, M&amp;sie Lc:9yd, Elizabeth Lloyd and Mr. George Ault.jhe iadys Pemberton sang very sweet „ sweetly " t,r4b .‘lareus home, " and Mr. ft Pemberton con- -o.ioted two humorous songs, to the delight tho young folks. Mr. Caleb Whitley was r zie-erved Y encored for his well-executed banjo pla"""s• The pianoforte accompaniments were bY Mr. C. Bostock, Miss Ads Grounds contri- Tezr",e couple of songs which were well received, as i!eiso the humorous reading given by Mr. J. Whitley. wa:°p eccg and drill by girls from Hill Cliffe school T heßiven with good effect and was deservedly encored. ,61,..event of the evening was the humorous dialogue of ilium's Matrimonial Mart," which provoked roars Areseughter, the charscters being well represented by arid 7,8.• John Roughsedge, G. Ault, G. Moss, P. Moss, es A. Grounds and Eley. A vote of thanks to th ^oo had taken part in the meeting was accorded, on e Proposition of Mr. G. Whitfield, secretary,</p>
        <p>GARDEN SOCIETY. tuSir Gilbert Greenell, Bute"' presided at the annual v-,,-.`etejog of the Stockton Heath Garden Society which oeld in the Hall Cafe on Monday evening. Amongst rei,ers Present were :—The Rev J Collins, Mr Roger Mr H Woodcock (chairman of the corn- Ir D E Watson (hon. treasurer), Mr le G delhite hoe. secretary), and Mr .1 H Clarke. h, Hon, Secretary in his report referral to the 1.-Blon to defer for the present the holding of the rilkg Bh°wa, It has been felt for some time that the eo° of the Society was somewhat crippled by the tberlieluance of the adverse financial conditions and, trl.„.,etore, it was decided for at least a season or two to ae"turle the efforts of the members to the summer and exetkon shows. The bulb .how a1w555 presented an seeeedinglY interesting display, and it is uoped that as rit is found possible this section of the work resumedeeeThe summer show which, as usual, Wse d attend- in August was in every respect, except the the e of visitors, a great success. Considering Nem weather (weeks of rainless skies) the exhibition the °f a very high character, and speaks volumes for Of te-P°Bsibilities of the district. If only the majority weer members would early in the year decide to grow tieu,Le of show quality, there is no dctibt the August -13'aY could be made far more attractive. The attend- lute, ‘i to " somewhat disappointing, largely owing to Factions, an IL 19 • otter att d • • hoped that members will in fe. eet ture make attendance at the shows a special duty, en °IIIY as an education to themselves, but as means of TheeQura2ine exhibitors. (Hear, hear, and applause.) the„,autumn show was again mainly devoted to chrysan- leXilcas and hardy fLiwers The show was an exceed- -3' good one, end merited a much larger attendance the- was present. (Applause.) The committee feel that act if th,, erk of the society would be very much ren_ dealt 's roestion of attendance could be satisftbe toty. till with. The junior section under the able leader- -5 hedP of M. G. A. Dunlop and other friends, has again to 4 fairly successful year. Tuis section of the work is an exceedingly important one, and the .°• 11Yeittee urge upon all our members to encourage rielleeir children to join and become active workers. The alletirl.P;s of the society are being gradual'y brought into cons:,B aet'my condition. The debit balance has LO9O Wry thaCilerablY reduced, and taking into account the fact e society possesses properties exceeding in value ftillPrnumt owing to the bank, a su3cessful year and a rlBt of members will put the finances on a sound basis 4, estilt which will be welcomed by all who have taken Tilelaterest in the society and its work. (Applause.) eeeie°,lroolittee who have ever the best interests of the reeerZY in view are recommending the members to ehip, to the origin d minimum subscription of one incrfing. and it is hoped that with this alteration a large tieeTtse of membership will ensue. Considerable atten- inlpo ala been given to the schedule for this year. Some Lewd bret;vrietrel changes have been made in its eerraogement new classes have been added. It should e take_ " exceedingly popular as there has been great care The_ to provide for all sections. GerteenrePort was adopted, on the proposition of Mr. The II With „ °n. Treasurer reported that the year began rede -11 adverse balance of £25 6d., which had been e fe37,etd to £lO 193. 10d. The total subscriptions showed !ad k Nee," of £ll 16s. 6d. as compared eith last year, Ante ,"3* 61. as compared with 1909. The loss on the el'ocv this year was £39 19s. 10d., compared with 2,,ct_e-si;eB; 311. in 1910, and £57 38. 7d. in 1909. On the 7 aeoW this rear the loss was £1 Is. against in 1910. a*nd £l.l in 1909. ‘)sr Uhe Proposition of Mr. W. H. Woodcock, seconded teesle Ereenell Oilibeqod.president of the society. Jr? respondieg, Sir L Fletcher, Sir Gilbert Gwas proud_ sr9d he felt proud of the position, but.ne w the w er still of the skilful and earnest manner 111. which Past cirk of the society had been carried on during the and ‘,. The debt had been very considerably reduced tine, brl°Pa economies carried out; but at the same the ahe did not think they had reduced the standing of denatic'w• (APplause.) He would be pleased to give a oth „.°TI of £sss. to show them how satisfied he was ott. 7e way the work of the society had been carried ‘vbeel'APPlallse.) They had put their shoulders to the b .rlcl he hoped in time they would have a substan- fitratV'es on the right side. (Loud applause). atson was re-elected hon. treasurer and Mr filh,'" hon. secretary. The following were elected to ecancies on the committee : Messrs J McDermid, At,leWoodcock, D S Birrell, E Vernon, E F Baker, A vid It thbo De F Brook F Tim erle W Yarwood, and Wright. P</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <p>Sole Agent:</p>
        <p>J. S. GTARLAND, 51, 53 &amp; 55 BRIDGE ST., WARRINGTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <p>A number of alterations in the rules and schedule were approved, and the Secretary announced that Mr Armitage had contributed .£5 towards the prize list for outdoor gardens. (Applause). It was decided to hold the summer show on August Bank Holiday, and the Chrysanthemum Show on September 28th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>Croit,</head>
        <p>Croit,</p>
        <p>THE PARISH CHCHICH.—At the Liverpool Consistory Court on Wednesday. a faculty was decreed giving authority to the Rev. Henry Isaac Wadeson, rector, and Messrs. Joshua Ball and Peter Ingham, wardens, to erect in the chancel of the church of Croft- with- Southworth a reredos the width of the sill of the east 1 window, to panel the wall on either side thereof, and the north and south walls of the chancel, with oak ; to substitute new brass communion rails for the existing rails ; to remove the brickwork now blocking up the two lancet windows in the north and south walls of the chancel ; also to remove the plain glass from the two windows, and to insert stained glass bearing figures of St. James and St. John the Evangelist, as a memorial of the late James and Mary Hardiker' the cost being defrayed by their daughter, Mrs. P. Ingham. The reredos is intended as a memorial of Mary Wadeson, the late wife of the rector, by whom its cost, and also the cost of the pannelling of the east wall, will be defrayed. The expense of the remainder of the work will be met by voluntary contributions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>Frodsham. CREAMERY SMOKE.-AMUSING CASE.</head>
        <p>Frodsham. CREAMERY SMOKE.-AMUSING CASE.</p>
        <p>At Frodsham, on Wednesday, the Runcorn Rural Council had eight E. lIIDMODSOS against the He'shy Creamery Company for having emitted black smoke from the chimney of their premises in Helsby main road Mr. Linclon Riley, for the Council, said the creamery I was not much larger than an ordinary dwelling-house, but an iron chimney bad been added to it, and this stood a little higher than an adjoining building, The creamery was at the foot of Helsby-hill, upon which a number of high-class residential houses bad been erected and the smoke nuisance affected the tenants. Mrs. Dutton, " Brackenhurst," Debby, said that on June 29th last her house was never free from smoke for three hours. She produced photographs she had taken of the chimney emitting black stroke. Unfortunately, the photographs could not reproduce the smell for the benefit of the Court. (Laughter.) The smoke had made her father ill and be tad had to go to Llandudno. John M`Cauley, smoke inspector to the Liverpool Corporat,on, said he made observations on October 2nd last. In the first hour black smoke was emitted for fifteen minutes; and in the next hour for nineteen minutes, which constituted a serious nuisance. He again made obsevations on October 9th ; but a man, whom be had since ascertained to be the manager of the creamery, went to him and said he knew him, and there would be no more smoke that day. (Laughters) . James White, Helsby, said the soot from the chimney seriously affected fruit-growing in his garden. Mr. b'. Smith, Liverpool, for the defendants, com- plained that the prosecution had called an expert to give evidence against them, whereas, if he had given them advice on his visit, the nuisance might have ceased. The company bad made changes in their fuel and had done everything in their power to prevent a nuisance. _</p>
        <p>Witnesses having been called to say no nuisance existed, the bench made an order for the abatement of the nuisance, and ordered the defendants to pay £7 is costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>Winwick. IMPORTANT APPEAL SETTLED.</head>
        <p>Winwick. IMPORTANT APPEAL SETTLED.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday, in the Court of Appeal, the Lord Chief Justice and Lords Justices Vaughan Williams and Farwell had in the list for hearing the case of Longton v. Winwick Asylum, which was an appeal of defendants from a judgment of the late Mr. Justice Grantham, sitting with a jury, at Manchester. The notion was brought by Mr. Peter Longton, of Alder Root Farm, Winwick, near Warrington, Lancaster, against the Com- mittee of Visitors of the Winwick Asylum, near Warrington, claiming damages sustained by him as occupier of his farm, which had been flooded and his crops injured in consequence of the defendants' alleged acts in regard to a brook and certain drainage. Defendants denied liability, and Mr. Justice Grantham entered judgment for the plainti When the appeal was called. Mr. Sutton (with him Mr. Whitworth), for the appellants, said he had seen Mr. Younger, K.C., and Mr. Morris, who appeared for the respondent, and they had agreed that the judgment wbich was entered by the late Mr. Justice Grantham at the hearing of the case should be discharged, that the action should be dismissed and the appeal to this court allowed with ru) costs either here or below, and that the defendants should be allowed to take out of court the damages plid in in pursuance of Mr.Justioe Grantham's orders. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>Mr. Younger assented to the terms. The court made the order as asked by counsel.</head>
        <p>Mr. Younger assented to the terms. The court made the order as asked by counsel.</p>
        <p>CONCERT.—A very sulse39ful consort was given in Winwick Schoolroom the other night, by members of the Arcadian Concert Party, and the Winwick Orpheus Glee Society. The proceeds ware in aid of A G Norman, who met with an accident a short time ago, while following 'his employment as a painter at the County Asylum. There was a very good attendance when the concert started at 8 o'clock. Miss D Lawton was in good voice and song " Swallows " and " Far from the land." Mr GI Wheelhouse was prevented from attending owing to a bereavement, and at the last minute Miss L Ormiston took his place, and sang " The Deathless Army. " Mr 3 Gibson was well received with " Jack's the boy." The A B C duet, by Miss Lawton and Mr Gibson was loudly applauded. Mr F Richmond, A.L.C.M. rendered two violin solos in masterly style. A popular item was a song " I remember" by Miss Rene Connington. Mr E Smethurst pleased the audience with his song "By the waters." Mr G Richards was heard to advantage in " Thom." Mr G Gibson was up-to-date with his comic song " Paper bag cookery." The Glee Society con- tributed two glees "Comrades in arms," and "The soldier's farewell." Johnnie Woods created much laughter with his two songs" Shirts "and " Setting the village on fire," The duties of accompanist were well carried out by Messrs It Wilson and Taylor. Mr Ormiston presided.</p>
        <p>Padgate. COMING OF AGH.—On Monday last Miss Elsie Lunt eldest daughter of Mr. and Mrs. Elijah Lunt, Spring Villa, attained her majority. Festivities to celebrate the happy event were held in the King's Cafe, Warrington. About 100 guests were present, several coming from a distance, and a large number of valuable presents testify to the esteem with which the recipient is regarded. Toasts were enthusiastically received and several were accompanied with musical honours. Songs were contributed by Mrs. Short and Mr. Levi Richard- son. A short whist drive was held, the successful competitors being Mrs. Matthews, Miss M. Hill and Miss Ida Muskett. while the victorious gentlemen were Messrs, J, C. Harrington, R. Denham and IL B. Nicholson. Dancing was indulged in to the able accompaniment of Messrs. Bootbroyd and T. Taylor. The M.C.'s were Messrs. Short and Cooper.</p>
        <p>-- --- FUNERAL OF TIIE LATE MR. ALBERT DUNIBELL.— Amid general manifestations of sorrow, the remains of the late Mr. Albert Dumbell, of Woolston, were laid to rest with those of his father, who predeceased him seven months ago, in the churchyard on Sunday after- noon. The church was crowded, many of the elder scholars, choir members and officials of the Wesleyan Church being present. The Risley Independent Metho- dist Church was, also well represented, the afternoon service having been somewhat curtailed to allow the members to attend. Rarely, if ever, has the sacred edifice witnessed so large an attendance of Non- conformists. The Vicar (Rev. G. W. Dickenson) officiated. The hymns " On the Resurrection Morn" and. "Days and moments quickly flying " were sung with much expression by the choir inside the church, and at the graveside they sang " Now the labourer's task is o'er." Mr. F. Blackwell presided at the organ and played the Dead March in Saul." Many choice floral tributes bore testimony to the respect in which the deceased was held. Among these there was one from the past and present members of the Padgate Wesleyan Church Choir, and another from the staff and scholars of the Warrington Secondary School. Much surprise was evinced among the Nonconformists of the village when it became known that the original arrangement to hold the funeral service at the Wesleyan Church had been abandoned. At the morning service this alteration was male known and it was announced that a special memorial service conducted by the Rev. E. Morgan would be held on Sunday evening the 21st inst. at El p.m. At the conclusion of the service the deceased (Mr. Hugh Mellor) played the Dead March.</p>
        <p>WESLEt GT:MD.—At the weekly meeting of the Guild held on Monday evening, Mr. Mark Howarth gave a lecture (with limelight illustrations), on ants. The lecturer dealt in a most interesting manner with this attractive subject, mentioning the habits, peculiarities and antipathies of the different kinds. Extensive reference was made to the wild ants and the formidability of the driver ante. Mr. F. Appleton manipulated the lantern with his usual skill, and the remarkable beauty of the coloured slides exhibited, evoked the admiration of those present. A vote of thanks to the lecturer was unanimously given on the proposition of Messrs. Pearce and Plinston. Moore. LANTERN LLCTURE.—On Wednesday evening last, i connection with the Wesley Guild, Mr. G. A. Dunlop, of Warrington, gave an exceedingly interesting lecture on " British Beetles and their story" illustrated with some very fine lantern slides, there was a very poor attendance, doubtless due to the heavy fall of snow during the day, but those who did venture through the snow were amply rewarded, for Mr. Dunlop is a lecturer who never fails to interest. At the outset Mr. Dunlop said that the average person thinks of beetles only with a shudder which he thought was very often due to confusing them with the creature they very much resemble,namely the cockroach in whose favour he admitted not much could be said. He then described and illustrated the different species of British beetles with their habits and anatomy. Mr. Lawton, who presided, proposed a very warm vote of thanks to the lecturer and expressed regret that there were not more present, this was seconded Ly Mr. J. H. Cartwright. Mr. F. Wilkinson very ably manipulated the lantern.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>enca iner, Saturday, January, 20, 1912.</head>
        <p>The enca iner, Saturday, January, 20, 1912.</p>
        <p>Daresbury.</p>
        <p>HALF CENTURY AS A BELL RINGER.-Mr. Peter Hamblett, who has retired after having been a ringer at Daresbury Parish Church for nearly 56 years, was on Saturday presented with a framed illuminated address and a purse containing £26 14s. A large number of parishioners were entertained to tea by the Vicar in the Sessions Rom. Subsequently the Vicar referred in eulogistic terms to thh regular and consistent manner In which Mr. Hamblett had performed his duties for over half a century, not only calling parishioners to church, hut regularly remaining for service himself. (Applause.)—ln the absence of Miss Milner, the oldest parishioner, Mrs. Wynne Jones, made the presentation. The address is artistically painted in the outline of a bell. The text reads :—•• Presented with a purse of gold, to Mr. Peter Hamblett, by parishioners of Dares- bury and other friends on his retirement from the posi- tion of bellringer at Daresbury Parish Church, as a token of their esteem, and in appreciation of his faithful services for over 50 years. Signed on behalf of the subscribers. T. T. Lee- lones, vicar ; W. S Adams, assistant curate ; C. H. Tinsley and Thomas Warburton, churchwardens. January 13th, 1912."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>Penketti.</head>
        <p>Penketti.</p>
        <p>LABOUR PARTY SoCIAL.—This was held on Saturday evening last in the Infants' Schoolroom, Penketh. The Entertaining Committee of the Building Fund were responsible for the arrangements with the assistance of the Women's Labour League, who were in ebarge of the refreshments. There was a remarkably good attend- ance, especially of young people. Games and dancing were indulged in and much enjoyed by all. Refresh- ments were handed round at the interval. About 100 were present and the party left for home about 11 o'clock.</p>
        <p>- DINNItaeT PENKETH INsTITUrE.—On Thnrsday night the members of the Penketh Institute held one of their annual meetings whtn a splendid dinner was provided. This over the President (Mr H Wilkinson) presented the prizes in \ connection with the Christmas Billiard Handicap. In tbe course of his remarks the President said he regretted tbe resignation of Mr F Ward from the post of Secretaryship and whilst publicly thanking him for his past services said he was pleased to be able to announce that he bad been fortunate enough in securing a worthy successor in the person of Mr T Jackson. Mr F Ward in expressing his regret at having to resign through private reasons thanked the members for working so cordially with him during his term of office and on behalf of the members, he wished to thank the donors of the prizes, viz. : Messrs Greenall, Whitley and Co., Dr Murray and Messrs H Wilkinson, R Jones, E Foster and [ Ashcroft. The following were the winners :—Messrs J Hampson, J Knowles, It Jones, H Wilkinson, W Knowles and A Goulden. Mr Woodward proposed a vote of thanks to the hostess(Nlrs Wilkinson)and Mr T Jackson seconded. Despite the unfavourable state of the weather 73 members were present and after a most enjoyable dinner the company spent the rest of the evening in different games.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>PENKETH PARISH COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>PENKETH PARISH COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening at the Council Schools, Stocks- lane, a statutory meeting of the above Council was held, there were present :—Dr Murray (chairman), Mr 3 0 Withinsbaw, Miss Tinsley, Messrs J Ditchfleld, W Davies, R Routledge, J Woodward and E Foster (clerk to the Council). CLOSING OF rOOTPAIII AT FIDDLER'S FERRY. . .</p>
        <p>The Clerk said he had not receivPd a reply from Mr Bates (agent for Captain Michael Hughes) to the letter sent from the Council respecting the closing of the footpath. Mr. Routledge enquired if it was advisable to ask Mr Bates to reply to the letter. Mr. Ditchfleld said that a gate had been erected at the Ferry Inn end of the footpath. Mr. Davies : That seems like a reply to the Council's letter. It was agreed that the Clerk write to Mr Bates for a reply. DISCUSSION ON VINE HOUSE FOOTPATH._.</p>
        <p>Mr. iiit-1;i-n-shi-w- pointed out the state of th 3 Vine House footpath which, he said, was disgraceful. He remarked that there were pools of water on it quite three inches deep, and it was not fit for ladies to pass along. It bad been repaired somewhat, but opt satis- factorily. In his opinion it was used as much as any in Pecketh. Ile suggested that it should be fenced off and raised six inches. . .</p>
        <p>The Chairman said it certainly was an important footpath and ought to be repaired once a year. Ele informed the Council that the District Council repaired it once without prejudice. Mr. Withicshaw thought that the Parish Council ought to take it over and make a proper job of it. The Chairman suggested that the best thing to do was to ask the District Council to let their men do it again. Mr. Withinshaw proposed that the following be appointed a sub-committee: Messrs It Routledge, W Davies and J Woodward to consider the question of repairs to the said path with power to carry out any improvements. The Chairman seconded and this was agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>LIGHTING OF DOE GREEN CORNER.</head>
        <p>LIGHTING OF DOE GREEN CORNER.</p>
        <p>Mr. Davies presented a petition from the residents of Doe Gretn, requesting the Council to put a lamp at the coroer of Doe Green. _ _</p>
        <p>After some discussion Mr. Davies proposed a lamp should be put there as soon as possible. Al r. Withinshaw seconded and the proposition was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>STATE OF TILE lIIGII ROADS AND_FOOTPATHS„</head>
        <p>THE STATE OF TILE lIIGII ROADS AND_FOOTPATHS„</p>
        <p>Mr. Woodward proposed tbat the Clerk write to The County Council to draw their attention to the wretched state of the highroad and footpaths. He said they were in a fearful state.</p>
        <p>Miss Tinsley said the roads were not fit to walk across never mind driving. ' ' •</p>
        <p>After further discussion Mr. Woodward proposed that the Clerk write to the County Council complaining of the disgraceful state of the mile and q uarter of high road and footpaths through the 'village and asking the authority if they were too busy would they empower some local authority to repair the footpaths.—Mr. Routledge seconded and the matter was agreed to. CORONATION ACCOUNTS'.</p>
        <p>The Clerk presented the statement of the coronation accounts showing a balance of 2s sd. in the bank. It was agreed to have the statement affixed on the walls at the Poet Mice in order to give the public an opportunity of seeing it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>DANGER OF BRONCHIAL COUGHS.</head>
        <p>DANGER OF BRONCHIAL COUGHS.</p>
        <p>Peps End the Trouble Once and for All.</p>
        <p>Bronchial troubles are dangerous because they settle so near the lungs. Generally it is the common bronchial cold with its nasty cough and " made-up " feeling that, neglected for days, lays one up at last with pleurisy or pneumonia. The introduction of the Peps treatment has rendered obsolete the ever-unsatisfactory method of trying to cure throat and lung troubles by means of liquid medicine. Not only do old-fashioned cough mixtures frequently contain dangerous drugs like opium, , morphia, chloral, but, being liquid, they are merely ' swallowed into the stomach and cannot, therefore, even touch the lungs where the trouble lies. Herein are the two distinguishing points about Peps: First, they do not contain any trace of opium, laudanum, or any other dangerous drugs occurring in old- fashioned cough medicines; second, they are a breatheable rcmedy. Tlat is, as a Peps tablet, removed from its preserving silver jacket is made to dissolve on the tongue, certain valuable fumes are given off which, mixing with the air we breathe, are at once taken down the windpipe and passed straight into the innermost receases of the lungs and chest. The soothing effect of these Peps fumes on the throat and bronchi is marvellous and brings not merely strength to the chest, but by repairing the delicate membrane torn by constant coughing, fortifies the windpipe against " cold-germs " and against further attacks of disease.</p>
        <p>Peps can be freely used by young and old ; and because cf their uniqueness, convenience and unequalled efficiency, these wonderful breatleable tablets should be kept always handy to ward off coughs, cold, bronchitis, sore thr oat, hcarseness wheeziness, &amp;c. Of all chemists at Is. 11d. or 2s. Ocl. a box. Avoid imititions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>SIR JOHN BRUNNER. ANOTHER GIFT TO NORTHWICEI.</p>
        <p>Sir John Brunner on Wednesday announced his in- tention to present the town of Northwich with a guildhall for the use of trades, friendly, and other societies, for which he has acquired a site from the Northwich Compensation Board. The new hall is intended to be a meeting-place for every society in the district. Similar gifts to Runcorn and Winsford have proved most valuable. Sir John's previous gifts to Northwich include a free library and a grammer school building.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>SAVED BY A BUTTON.</head>
        <p>SAVED BY A BUTTON.</p>
        <p>A boy's remarkable escape from being killed by a revolver shot is reported from Widnes. A boy named Thomas Oldtleld, aged 15 years, h3d by some means obtained possession of a five-chambered revolver and a number of bullets. With two other boys—John Prescott and Thos. Leyshon, of Tirnperley-street—he set up a target to practice shooting on some waste land. Leyshon appears to have been utanding behind the target when Oldfield fired: The bullet struck him on the breast, but fortunately caught a bone button and glanced off at a tangent. The only injury was a slight graze on the skin an inch or two from the heart.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN lIERO. FORMER FOOTBALLER REWARDED.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn Police Court on Monday, Superintendent Ennion a,ked the chairman of the bench to hand a cheque for £5, received from the trustees of the Carnegie Hero Fund, to Thomas George Washburn, a Well-known trainer of fire brigades, and a former Runcorn footballer. Superintendent Enaion said that in March last a fire broke out at 31, Granville-street, Runcorn. The only occupant of the home was a nine-months-old baby, asleep upstairs. Several people attempted a rescue, but were driven back by the flames. Washburn arrived on the scene, and at great personal risk effected a rescue. He had already received a certificate and silver medal from the Liverpool Shipwreck and Humane Society. The Bench warmly congratulated Washburn on being the first local recipient of a grant from the Carnegie Fund. Washburn said it was very encouraging to find what he had done recognised. He would try to do his best always.</p>
        <p>INSURANCE ACT. GOOD NEWS FOR RUNCORN MOTORS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Walter Millington, of Runcorn, a P.G.M. of the Grand United Order of liddtellows, speaking in support of Mr. J. T. L. Brunner, M.P., at Runcorn on Tuesday night, dealt with the local effects of the Insurance Bill. In Runcorn, he said, 6,000 out of its 18,000 inhabitants. would have to insure under the Act, and on the Os, basis the local doctors would certainly get £3OO each. and would have about 1,000 patents to attend' As a friendly society official of thirty years standing, he finew that nut 25 per cent would require medical treatment. He estimated that the doctors would have to pay four visits a day during the year, and receive £3OO tor doing that. In addition, the doctors would have two-thirds of the community for their general practice, and if they reckoned these at the rate of 6s. Ahich the medical men claimed was too little, it meant £9OO a year. There was not, a friendly society in the world that paid its doctors Os. per head. He admitted that the payment 2s. 6d. to 4s. was "sweated " pay. but, if a club vacancy occurred, all the doctors competed for it. Mr. Lloyd-George bad boldly _tackled a problem that friendly societies had failed to solve after 100 years of effort.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>RUNCORN MYSTERY. SUICIDE OR NATURAL DEATH?</head>
        <p>A RUNCORN MYSTERY. SUICIDE OR NATURAL DEATH?</p>
        <p>Coroner Ridgeway held an inquest at Runcorn, on Tuesday, on the body of John Joseph Matthews, a , former well-known athlete, whose body was found in bed at his home in Leicester-street on Sunday morning. Evidence showed that the young fellow had not been well for some time, and had complained of pains in his head. On Saturday night be retired to rest as usual. He slept with his little son, and on Sunday morning his wife heard the boy calling him. Finally the child came to her and said he "could not wake decide." Deceased's mother-in-law went to the room and found him to be dead. Dr. Murphy said he had attended Matthews for slight congestion of the brain, the result of sunstroke. He bad made a complete recovery and resumed work. When witness was called to the house on Sunday he thought deceased had been dead about eight hours. He found some brownish liquid in a tumbler under the bed, and also some powders, but, on analysis, he found that neither contained powerful poison; and, judging from the position of the deceased and his peaceful appear- ance, it was possible that the end had come naturally. The Coroner said a letter had been lelt by tho deceased and he had once attempted suicide. The position was puzzling, and he would adjourn the case for a post- mortem examination.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS. On Tucsday, Mr. J. Spruce presiding. YACCII,TATION FEES.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from the Secretary to the Anti- Vaccination League requesting that when next filling the vacancy for Workhouse Medical Officer that be shall be paid a salary which should include th 3 performance of any vaccination at the Workhouse or Infirmary. Mr. Morris said the letter came from a lot of cranks. He did cot believe in them and moved that the letter lie on the table.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. Linaker : Would you call Mr. Snowden a crank? Mr. Morris' resolution was agreed to.</p>
        <p>TILE PROPOSED CHILDREN'S HOME.</p>
        <p>The Boarding-out Commissioners reported having met that morning %hen the architect submitted plans for the proposed alteration to Dutton Lodge, the estimate for which amonnted to £4OO to £5OO. The committee agreed that subject to certain arra, gements with owners, the plans to be sent to the Local Government Board for adoption. The minutes of the committee were parsed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>DOUSE COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>DOUSE COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The committee reported an increase in the number of vagrants and inn ates. A letter was read from Rev. A. L. Temple regretting that the Board were unable to consider his application for an increase of salary as Workhouse Chaplain and under the circumstances he would be glad to be relieved of his duties at Easter. It was resolved that the committee could not see their way to grant the increase. The minutes were confirmed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL COUNCIL. At Dutton on Tuesday, Mr. T. Wright, presidiog. THE LATE MR. DIG G LE. _</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL COUNCIL. At Dutton on Tuesday, Mr. T. Wright, presidiog. THE LATE MR. DIG G LE. _</p>
        <p>Before commencing the business of the Council, the chairman feelingly referred to the death of Mr. Diggle. The deceased gsntleman, he said, had been surveyor to the Coubcil in Frodsham District for about lb years. At the last meeting the Clerk was able to tell them he was a little better but since then an operation had heed performed from which Mr. Diggle never recovered. The fuheral took place on Saturday week and the Council were represented. Although he had not been able to see eye to eye with every member of the Council he had been very attentive to his duties and they all regretted his untimely death. He moved that their appreciation be recorded on the minutes and a vDte condolence be sent to the widow and family in their sad loss. _ _ .</p>
        <p>Mr. Linaker, sen., seconded the resolution and said Mr. Diggle had one good quality, and that was, no matter how roughly he was haudled he always came in the council with a smiling face and a shake of the hand showing there was no resentment. He was a familiar figure in the district and always tried to do his duty to the best of his ability. The resolution was passed by the members standing</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>SUCCF SSOR.</head>
        <p>A SUCCF SSOR.</p>
        <p>The Council resolved that ca-is---iemporary expedient Mr. J. Swinton, the Budworth Surveyor, have under his supervision the Council's Highwass, and that Messrs. Wright and Handlyn supervise lilt building operations for the present if their terms were favour- able. A special committee N 9,19 appointed to consider the question of the vacancy created by Mr. Diggle deceased.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>lIOITSING AND TOWN PLANNING.</head>
        <p>lIOITSING AND TOWN PLANNING.</p>
        <p>The housing and Town Planning Committee reported having obtained tenders for sanitary improvement at Woods'-row, Halton, and submitted that of 111e9srs Davies and Co. of Frodsham amounting to £.165 16s. It was resolved that subject to the approval of the owners of the property that Messrs. Davies' tender be accepted: -</p>
        <p>The minutes were confirmed. THE SRODSII.II\I SMELL.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from Dr. Selby, of Frodsham, stating that bones were being discharged here again that day, and an adjoining truck of food stuff was being loaded. There was no leas sentiment about this than there was when he stated they were being loaded near milk cans. What were the public officials for but to see into the matter properly and go for it. The Medical Officer said his. difficulty in answering the question was extreme. The state of affairs he bad seen was what should not be allowed by any Railway Company. It was implied that the Railway Company should only accept bones in a condition uhich Was not offensive. The traffic was a legitimate one, but under the circumstances under which the Inspector and him- self had seen they were discharged, the practice was detrimental to health and ought to be suppressed. It was resolved to write to Dr. Selby,asking whether be contended that the bones were offensive upon the particular date mentioned.</p>
        <p>THE OFFICE BOY AND HIS DINNERS. At the yearly meeting of the Winnington Recreation Club, which has a membership of over 1,000. Sir John Brunner (the chairman of Messrs. Brunner, Mond, and Co.) said on Tuesday night, that his colleagues and himself had welcomed the suggestion from Mr. C. Jarmay, one of the directors, that they should offer to the boys in their employ, from 11 to 17 years of age, a daily dinner. Ile bad been told that the proposal would not be universally acceptable. The proposal was made not only with a kind heart, but a good deal of consideration for their own interests. They looked forward to a result from the giving of the dinners, coupled, as it was to be, with the requirement that the boys should attend the gymnasium, to seeing the boys grow up sturdy and self-respecting. (Applause.) As a result of the practice in the gymnasium, Brunner, Mond and Co. expected to be better served if the men in their service were strong and healthy. He was told this might be looked upon as a charity He asked them not to believe anything of the sort (Applause.) He remembered very well indeed when he used, as a boy, to go to an office in Liverpool and his father gave him his weekly dinner money how that money went—(laughter)—a penny roll, a glass of milk, and two cigars. (Laughter.) Sometimes it was a couple of oranges and two cigars. (Laughter.) He wanted to pin the lads dowo, to get them to take a good lunch. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS HOUSE FAMINE. TENANTS' REPLY TO CORPORATION PROSECUTIONS.</p>
        <p>Twelve householders were prosecuted by the St. Helens Corporation under the Housing Town-planning Act on Monday for keeping their dwelling-houses in such a state as to be dangerous or injurious to the health of the inmates. _ . _</p>
        <p>r P. A. 'laden, deputy town clerk, prosecuted, and said that seven of tte defendants lived in Ellison- svare, three in Scholes-lane, nett() Heath, and two in Critchley-street, Parr. During the inspection of the d ellhags of the borough, under the Housing Act, the houses occupied by tbeso defendants were found to be in such a state that the medical officer reported them to the Corporation, who made an order, on October 4th, that the defendants should vacate the houses until they were rendered fit for habitation. The necessary work had not been done, and the proper notices to quit had been served, but the defendants had taken no notice. Defendants told the Bench that they found it impossible to get other houses. Orders to quit were made by the Bench, which if disoLeyed will be followed by a penalty of £1 a day. Robert byes, of 38, Ashcroft-street, Parr, was summoned for overcrowding his house, Mr. Turton said the proceedings were taken under Section 91 of the Public Health Act. That was a very bad case of overcrowding. There wi-re only two bed- rooms, and the back bedroom was occupied by six boys and girls, aged nineteen, seventeen, fifteen, thirteen eleven, and ten sears. It bed no fireplace, and the small window would not open. There was only 565 cubic feet in the room, an average of le, s than 100 cubic feet per person, whereas in a common lodging-house the standard was 400 feet. Evidence was given that notices had been given, but not obeyed. The Bench made an order for the nuisance to be abated in seven days, falling which there would be a penalty of £s*</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>CHILDREN'S BALL AT ST. lIELENS. MAYORAL TREATS.</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S BALL AT ST. lIELENS. MAYORAL TREATS.</p>
        <p>The Mayor at;d-Mayoreas of SL lielens (Alderman Sir Joseph and Miss Beecham)- gave a children's party on Tuesday night at the Town Hall, when 570 juveniles between the ages of ten and sixteen enjoyed a series of of entertainments, danced to lively music, and after- wards eat down to a substantial supper. The guests were received by the Mayor and Mayoress, and after- wards assembled in the large ball, where the grand pro- cession and dance followed. The parents, who were all greatly interested ia the event, were admitted to the balcony to view the proceedings. Mr Collins's band provided the music ; Carl Vernon gave ventriloquial entertainments and Professor ih'ishart of London, also amused the children by clever juggling, dcc. Dr F J Knowles and Mr C Rowed were MX:S and the stewards included Major Duke Woolley, Mr W H Andrew (town clerk), Mrs Boston, Dr and Mrs Jackson, Mrs Knowles, Mr and Mrs E J Simpson, Miss Simpson, Mrs end the Mrs Rowed, Mr and Mrs W Oppenheim, Miss Oppenheim, Mr and Mrs E M Hollingsworth, Miss Bellamy, Mrs Martin, Mrs Malkin Mr and Mrs T A Turton, Dr Martha Wilson, Messrs II Beecham, E Bamford, 2,7, Glover. A Scott, W Moss, J Hammill, jun.. J Dennett, A C Willcocks, J Sephton, Smethurst, C H Rowed, Mr and Mrs Scrymgeour, and Mis A Oakes. •</p>
