      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <p>RRESPONDENT: AJMER'S reply to" Leber Orcnie Vincit " will next week.—ED. " Ex."</p>
        <p>Religion and Tory Tyranny. Sir,—What the Rev. W. Cooper criginally said is in dispute between the " Examiner " and himself. He now says that within five miles of Warrington there are people who dare not attend the Church of their choice because of Tory tyranny. He definitely refuses to attempt to prove it, although hessys the facts came under his personal observation, and have been fully corroborated. Who and where are these Tory tyrants? Can they be the persons Mr. New referred to in connection with the boxes for the Tariff Reform Children,s Treat? In each case the charge rests on one man's assertion. In each case the " Examiner" report is disputed. In each case evidence is called for but none ia forthcoming. They either fear their case too much, Or else their proof is small, Who dare not put it to the tcuch, And test it once for all, They don't allow for common-sense, And over-rate their credit, Who only give as evidence, That they themselves have said it. Yours etc E. AUBREY WHITE.</p>
        <p>12 e 4 11 , • , • 1111111 \ A IStthe ' 11•-77-"7-4-i., 1 ‘tt EDITOR L.') \ il ,--__ .- , ( .........", '_ NOTICE TO Cw Mr. PA appear -- 1.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>17th January 1012.</head>
        <p>17th January 1012.</p>
        <p>Mr. Uarold Smith M.P. and Trade Unionism.</p>
        <p>Sir,—The letter in your last Saturday's issue is evidently from a member of the A.S.R.S. whose political bias is such that he prizes lawlessness and anarchy before law and order. Ere is apparently a Radical first and Trade Unionist afterwards, hence his suggestion that if Mr. Smith is a conscientious Trade Unionist he shou!cl go over to the Liberal side. Nov I should like to know why Conservatives cannot be true Trade Unionists. Be will be the first to admit they are, many of whom have been persuaded to become Conservatives from the greater knowledge they have of the intentions cf Trade Unionism than the writer of the letter signed A.S.R.S. Is lie aware that the policy cif his party, that of Free Trade is the greatest curse Trade Unionism has had to meet? Does be forget the greatest enemy Trade Unionists ever had? If so, I will remind him. Cobden—the Radical Free Trader proclaimed that free imports would be the death blow to Trade Unionism. If he will revert to what is now history he will find that Cobden's hopes lay in this direction and further if he will review the position of Trade Unions to-day, and go back for a pericd of 50 years, he will not fail to realize that the position then so far as its members were concerned was better and more helpful in every way. So long as Free Trade is allowed to continue the nett value of Trade Unionism to its members will he a negative quantity. I am rather wandering from the point I intended to follow, which is, can a man be a consistent Trade Unionist and oppose coercion, intimidation, &amp;c.? I say yes. Picketing can only be of benefit to Trade Unionism when conducted in a manner supported by its true leaders. Threats and intimidation and damage to property do not assist but rather hinder the object in view. The writer of the letter " A.S.R.S." is quite correct when suggesting" Workingmen of Warrington are taking note of Mr. Smith's doings " ; they are more than satitfied with their representative. It is not necessary for the "Member 'to walk over to the Liberal side to prove himself honest and earnest to the workingmen. of Warrington. They have eyes to see and ears to hear, so, be not afraid, be will not walk over !—Yours, H. MADDOCK.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>"The Quaker Cid."</p>
        <p>Nothiug appeals to the taste of a Warrington audience more than an up-to-date musical comedy, and as a result Ms. Loader's enterprise in bringing " The Quaker Girl" this week has been rewarded by Zscord bookings. The company is one of the most tslented which has ever visited the Rylands-street house, and the production generally is on the moit lavish scale. The dresses alone are a feast for the eye, and may be considered le dernicr cri in fashion, while some of the stage pictures presented are surpass- ingly gay and beautiful. Not a little of the interest in the production is due to the fact that several artists are recognised as favourites who have gained distinction with "Our llias Gibbs" and "Miss Hook of Holland" on previous isits to Warrington. "The Quaker Girl" is rapidly, and deservedly, rivalling the popularity Co f "The Merry Widow," and though this is the first visit to Warrington it can be said at once that to the majority of people the many delightful songs and musical numbers were perfectly ' familiar. In the title role, local theatre-goers were , fortunate to find so charming and accomplished a young actress as Miss Dora Laugher's. Whether in Quaker bonnet and gown, or in the latest Paris modes at the liaison Blum or the Prince's Ball, she was the essence of daintiness, whle her singing and dancing were a continued delight The companion-part of Tony Chute was capitally pour- trayed by Mr. John T. McCallum, though we thought, at times, be was over-boisterous and laid ton much emphasis on the comic side of things. He infused any amount of " go " into anything he did, and both the vocal and dancing duets between himself and Prudence invariaMy brought down the house. One of the prettiest features of the whole comedy is the by-play around the exiled Bonapartist Princess Mathilde, ard her young English husband, Captain Charteris. The wedding celebration is a juyful incident in Act 1., and thereafter we are con- cerned with the adventures and fortunes of the young couple in Paris under the nose of the indefatigable Chief of Police. Miss Josset Ellis makes a fascinat- ing Princess, and sings and dances with true artistic elect throughout the piece. Equal praise is due to Mr. Frank H. Crimp as the gallant captain, his fine baritone voice being heard to great advantage. A big 'Proportion of the humour is in the bands of the droll Jeremiah, Madame Blum, and Pheebe, the saucy maid. Mr. J. H. Hooper carved out a distinct and happy line for himself as Jeremiah,and Miss Olga Morra made one of the most finished Madame Blume we have seen in the role. Miss Maud Denny made a great hit with nor clever song and dance "Or There- abouts" in Act 2. Quite an original and laughable study of Larose, Chief of Police, is presented by Mr. Frank Attree, and everywhere " the man with the limp" is greeted with roars of laughter. The Prince was played with due empresse- mciit by Mr. Cyril Dane, though he was a little bit too French for our liking. Miss Elsie Wallace, as Diane, a French actress, was excellent and made the most of her one effective seene.The orchestral music was admir- ably played under the direction of Mr. A. Garfield Barratt, and contributed very largely to the thorough njoyirent of "The Quaker Girl." NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME. Plays dealing with life amongst the Redskins are always popular, and, with characteristic enterprise, the management of the Royal Court Theatre have arranged for a visit next week of what is admittedly one of the very best of such pieces now being toured —the beautiful Red Indian romance, "His Indian Wife," by Miss Emma Litchfield. The play will be produced by Mr. Arthur Hinton and Miss Leila Zillwood's talented company, with practicslly the same caste as at the Elephant and Castle Theatre, London, where it had a long run and achieved a really genuine success. In order that the drama might be mounted and played in as realistic a manner as possible, Mr. Hinton, Miss Zilltatood, and several of tho principal members of the company paid an extended visit to Pine Ridge Reservattou, U.S A., where they had an excellent opportunity of studying the lives and customs of the Redskins.</p>
        <p>A very good illustrated book almanac for 1912 is that published by Thomas Holloway, proprietor of Holloway's pills and ointment. It is crowded with usefcl items and the illustrations are excellent. It also contains a railway accident insurance coupon for £250. A free copy may be obtained from any chemist, or one will be sent by the Publisher, 113. Southwark-street, London, S.E. on receipt of half-penny stamp for</p>
        <p>postage:</p>
      </div>
