      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>WORK FOR WARRINGTON'S UNEMPLOYED.</head>
        <p>WORK FOR WARRINGTON'S UNEMPLOYED.</p>
        <p>Warrington has experienced its full share of the seasonable visitation of Arctic weather which has placed all England under a snowy mantle. The storm of Wednesday and Thursday meant a stroke of luck to many of the unemployed for, although snow is pleasant to look upon, it has its drawbacks to vehicular and pedestrian traffic, and the task of clearing the streets necessitated the engagement by the Borough Surveyor's department of many extra hands. On Thursday 226 of the unemployed—many of whom were engaged</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>TOBOGGANING AT HILL CLIFFE.</head>
        <p>TOBOGGANING AT HILL CLIFFE.</p>
        <p>Photos, W. Burrell, The snow has been a source of joy to scores of people who have for some days past tobogganed down the steep bill alongside the Baptist Graveyard at Hill Cliffe. Our snapshots depict a spill at the bottom, and a sledge in full flight down the hill.</p>
        <p>company were guilty of a breach of duty surely ti e present revondents were not the people to suffer. He submitted that the pain the man had, was due to t.a'ural causes, very probably lumbago. Longmare whilst at work was suddenly seized with pain and it could nnt he said definitely that he had in fact had a strain. The present incapacity was dila to naruiral causes and his present condition to nrentature oil age. His work was of such a kind as to m k • him prematunly old. _ _ .</p>
        <p>Dr. Naden who asked the judge whether ha was obliged to give evidence and received a reply in the affirmative, said he saw the applicant on Vie day of the accident. There were no external signs that he had met with an injury to his back. Witness knew he suffered from lumbago and he cotasidered his condition was due to lumbago. In reply to Mr. Goldie witness said he had nothing to do with the firm of Monks Hall and Co. He was the works club doctor. He admitted writing the following letter dated December 16th to Mr. Steel :—" I have no record whatever of club patients and therefore camaJt tell you when the illness was. My impression was that Lougmore was suffering from lumbago. I have nothing whatever to do with certificates for accidents."</p>
        <p>Dr. Naden added that he knew nothing about the case until he was rung up on the teleph( ne by Dr. Fox jun., sometime last year. He was then asked what he knew about the accident. Witness told him as far as he conld remember that Longmore said he had kenched " his back.</p>
        <p>In reply to his Honour Dr. Naden said the appear- ance of Longmore to-day was very similar to thitof four years ago. Dr. Teller, of Manchester, who examined Longruore on Tuesday last, said he noticed there was some limita- tion of n overlent in all directions, but not more than could be reasonably expected in a man of his years. Ile could and no evidence of injury. His general state of health was very poor, due to an almost entirely marked and general arterial disease, attributable to a life of hard work. An accident would certainly not accelerate it. Lonp,more, in his opinion, was not capable of doing any manual work nhatever. In reply to his Honour witness said the present con- dition of incapacity was entirely due to a process of natural causs and not to a strain.</p>
        <p>Dr. Fox, jun., said he had examined the applicant on two eccasions, in April, 1911, and on Tuesday. He lound no evidence of soy injury to the spine. in his opinion Longmore suffered from premature old age. Mr. Goldie addressing his Honour said there had been no evidence calico' to rebut the story of the applicant that. be had met with an accident.</p>
        <p>His Honour who %as assisted in arriving at a decision by Dr. Bowden, acting as medical referee, paid he was not qatisfled that the present condition of the applicant end the condition at any time since November 2lth, 1911, was due or contributed to, by the accident. He therefore gave an award for the respondents without costs.</p>
