      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>ACCIDENT OR LUMBAGO?</head>
        <p>ACCIDENT OR LUMBAGO?</p>
        <p>A PUDDLER'S CLAIM.</p>
        <p>At. the Warrington County Court on Thursday, his Honour Judge Stanger, KAI, heard an application for an award under the Workmen's Compensation Act, by Edward Longmore, of 170, Evelyn-street, in respect of an accident Which he alleged he sustained at the works of Messrs. :gooks nail and Co., Ltd., on July 24th, 1908. Mr. N. Goldie, barrister (instructed by Mr. I. S. Steel) appeared for th A applicant, and Mr. Lisbona, barrister (instructed by Mr. R. Freldhouse, solicitor, (Manchester) for the respondents. Mr. Goldie said the applicant was a paddler, and his wages averaged about Zi 93. weekly. Compensation had been paid at the rate of us. 6d. from the time of the accident until November, 1911. Longmore had worked for the respondents for something like thirty years, and he Was now sixty-four years of age. On the day of the accident he was engaged lifting a piece of pig iron weighing about lcavt., when he slipped and I.is back seemed to crack. A workman named Dykes assisted him to the works lodge where he was given some brandy. Afterwards he was taken home and put to bed, remain- ing there for nine wreks. Ever since he had been incapacitated and even now it was impossible for him to walk, with the aid of a stick, more than our hundred yards withont having a rest. Long.nore was absolutely permanently iocapacitated.An extracrdinary feature of the case was that after the accident he wife received the compensation money regularly and systematically right down to November 24th, 1911—no less than three years. During the time the compensa- tion had been paid the applicant had been summoned to Manchester and elsewhere by the respondents to be examined by their own doctors. The applicant Edward Longmore said that whil.st he was lifting the piece of pig iron he slipped and his back " snapped " and he was unable to move, until Dykes came to his assistance. When in bed he suffered great pain from his back. William Dyke, 4 Dale-street, deposed that he sacs Longmore in a stooping position with a pieste of pig iron in his hand. He seemed to be in difficulties and witness took it from him. Longmore was very white in the lace and perspiring freely. Edward ‘N hittaker, 62 Baxter-street, said Longrnore had almost to be carried home. lie seemed to be in great pain. The wife of the applicant, Mary Ann Longmore,spoke to receiving the compensation money. On Each occasion she had to sign a receipt. James Lomax, clerk in the employ of Mr. Steel, said that on March 15th, 1910, his employer received a letter from the solicitors of an Insurance Company in which they said that further examination of Longroore, who was receiving compensation under the Workman's Compensation Act, was desired and that they would like the man to appear at the ofnces of Messrs Monks, Hall and Co , to be examined by Drs Smith and Sankey, of Manchester. In accordance with that letter he attended with Longmore and the examination was made in the presence of the Secretary of the company. Dr. G. W. Joseph said he examined the applicant and found that there was tenderness on pressure all over the muscles of the back and over the tips of the spine. In his opinion the man was totally incapacitate.) from any work. His present condition was consistent with the fact that he had met with an accident snch as bad been described In crosr-examination by Mr. Lisbona witness said he would not expect the condition of the man's lack to be due to senile decay, nor would an attack of lumbago last over such a long period. In reply to his Honour, Dr Joseph said in his judgment the present condition of the man was due to the accident. For the respondents, Mr. Lisbona said it was quite true that the man had received compensation pay up to November 1911, but there was a very good explanation for that course. The respondents were insured with the Law Car and General Insurance Company which went into liquidation in December 1910 and the respondents continued the payments which the company commenced. At that stage the Law Car and General Company were in hopelessly involvent condition and it seemed very unlikely when in such a position that they would ' run the expense of fighting the action. In November 1911 the respondents had the man examined and as a result of that examination they refused to make any further payments. He submitted that the money paid to the applicant WBS paid under a mistake of facts. The , man had never met with the accident. If the insurance</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>GOLF.</head>
