      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>Judging by the attendances at the Hippodrome, local people are exceptionally fond of the condensed pantomime. This week large audiences have witnessed Mr. Harty Burns production of "Robinson Crusoe," which is under the personal supervision of Mr. Fred Terris. It is a welcome feature of panto- mimes that rollicking fun should be to the fore and this weeks show is certainly no exception. All the essentials of pantomime are made evident and there is a clever blendirg of fun, music and ' fable. An entrancing array of colour is shown in the scene which represents Crusoe in "the home of Fairy Pearl 'neath the sea," when the stage is thronged with groups of charmingly dressed damsels who disport themselves in the fairy retreat. The songs are all the latest favourites and the catchy choruses are taken up and sung heartily by the audience. Miss Lily Edward's is a vivaciovs principal boy and her songs are intetpr2ted in a satisfactory manner She is well supported by Miss Blanch Marion who as Polly Perkins the charming sweetheart of Crusoe, scores a decided success. Laughter hearty and con- tinuous is that which follows the exploits of Mr. Arthur Aiston as Will Atkins. His humour is fresh, original and infectious, and keeps the audience on the go all the time. It is d'fficult to discriminate between the many funny people, Miss Victoria Cross (Mattha Ann), Mr. W. Lindsay (Billy Crusoe), Mr. G. Collins (the Dane), Messrs Cox, Hughes and Wilson (the pirates), and Messrs. Stewart and Morris (Captain Cuttle and his mate) working well together in the brighter vein. The chorus does its work capab'y and the show is fall of colour and animation. NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>A notable engagement next week is that of Miss Mary Mayfren's Company in "Yellow Fang," a dramatic episode of an opium hell in Chinatown, San Francisco. Schaal, described as the world's most versatile musician, will also appear in "The Music Store" an original idea. The rest of the bill contains comic, terpsichorean, and musical items in nice variety.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>SOLICITOR AND LIMITED COMPANIES. A Warrington 6cTuify Court Protest.</head>
        <p>SOLICITOR AND LIMITED COMPANIES. A Warrington 6cTuify Court Protest.</p>
        <p>At Warrington County Court, on Thursday, before his Honour Judge Stanger, K.C., Mr. A. Browne, solicitor, Warrington, raised an unusual protest on behalf of the local branch of the Law Society. A case was bong heard by his Honour in which a limited company was suing for the recovery of a debt. A representative of the firm was in the witness-box giving evidence in support of the action when Mr. Browne proteated against limited companies not being legally represented. His Honour said his attention had been cal'ed to a similar protest in a county court in Yorkshire. He had not carefully considered the matter, and his mind was quite open. Supposing the employers of the witness were not a limited company, would there be any objection? Mr. Browne—Not the slightest. His Honour—Do you say that a servant of the company has no right to go into the witness-box to prove a small debt of 2s. or 35.? I have had a long experience of county court work, and if such protests were upheld it would be a startling revolution. Mr. Browne—l should like to call your attention to the action c,f the late Mr. Stewart, the Liverpool stipendiary, who declined to allow the secretary of a company to appear in a smoke case in Liverpool. His Honour said he thought he could dispose of the present case, and then hear the arguments afterwards. As far as the present case was concerned he would allow the evidence to be given.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>LYMM.</head>
        <p>LYMM.</p>
        <p>LYMM PETTY SESSIONS. WEDNESDAY.—Before Captain' Groves, Messrs. Alfred Watkin,• William Callender, Samuel Davies, John Coxon and Thomas Ridgway. MISCELLANEOCS.—Emma Lawson, of Rushgreen, Lymm, was fined ss. for being drunk and incapable on the 13th November last. James Higgins was summoned for not showing a red light at the rear of his lurry. The case was adjourned to the next court. TAXI-DRIVER CAUTIONED.—Richard DOggiaS a taxi- driver of Warrington wassnmreoned for leaving his car unattended while the engine was in motion. He was let off with a caution, this being his first offence. OCCASIONAL LICENCES.—Thomas Charks Heaven made two applications ; one for an extension of time from 11 p.m. to 12 p.m. on the 9:..h of February next, the occasion being a dance held at the Railway Hotel, Ileatley for the Oughtrington Football Club. The other ma for an occasional Repose for the ILymm Assembly Ball to be held on February 19th, 7.30 p,m. to 3 a.m, on the 20th. Both were granted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>LIBERAL MEETING AT LYMM.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL MEETING AT LYMM.</p>
        <p>MR. EDGAR JONES, M.P., ON THE INSURANCE ACT.</p>
        <p>One of the series cf meetings arranged by the Altrincham Division Liberal Association with the object of explaining the Insurance Act was held in the Drill Hall, Lymm, on Thursday evening. Notwith- standing the Arctic weather, there was a large attend- ance, over which Mr. G. L. Welford presided. The principal speaker was Mr. Edgar Jones, senior member for Merthyr Tydvil. Others present on the platform were the Rev RW B Whiteway, Mr J D B Smith and Mr J H Mather.</p>
        <p>The Chairman stated that the other day he was speak- ing to a high officer of the Druids' Society who, after having made a special study of the Insurance Act, told him he was convinced that it was calculated to be of great benefit to the workers, and when it bad been fully and fairly tried, it would be found to realize all that was expected of it.(Applause.) Further,he was of opinion that practical experience of the working of the measure would not suggest the necessity of much alteration or amendment. As the different Friendly Societies held their conferences, they were all coming round to the view that the Act was full of good things for friendly societies, trade,lunions and the working classes generally. It meant, In fact, asocial revolution (Applause). When old age pensions were before the House of Commons, there was a great desire on the part of the Labour mom bars to reduce the age limit to 65 or 60, but the atte rept failed because the national finances were not ripe for such an enormous addition to the cost. Under the Insurance Act, however, any insured person between the ages of 16 and 70 would, on becoming permanently disabled, be entited to ss. per week for the remainder of his life. That was an immense benefit to people who would other- wise hey° nothing at all, and for whom the only refuge would be the Workhouse. The Chair man showed how, wi h the broadening of the franchise, social questions affecting the material welfare of the workers had increasingly forced themselves upon the attention of the legislature. Ile did not know that we need be afraid of that tendency. He did not quarrel with the prevalent unrest and dissatisfaction so tong as it limited itself to vocal expression and did not resoet to violence. It was a hopeful sign wkon the lowest strata of our near:teal society began to be dissatisfied with its conditions. So long as agitation was conducted on constitutional lines, it was to be welcomed. Most men wore reasonable at the bottom. It was only now and again that a wild fellow kicked over the traces and could not be controlled. If we could make everybody contented, give everybody some cause to feel that life was worth living, and train the workers more efficiently, there need be no anxiety about England's ability to hold her eels amongst the growing nations of the earth. The Insurance Act would, he believed, tend to leak° England a better, happier and more prosperous country. (Applause). Mr. Edgar Jones, M.P., who had a hearty reception, said that for many years statesmen had looked, from a standpoint more or less outside, upon what was known as the social prob'ent, and they had said : " There is a drag upon the wheels, a clog upon the progress of our people.' Many reformers: Parliamentsry Com- missioners reporting in voluminous blue books, investi- gators like Charles Booth, Rowntree and others, had teen pointing out many of the detailed causes why the people lagged behind and we had a submerged tenth, poverty and misery, in spite of the fact that the national income had been steadily increasing every year by leaps and bounds. Statesmen simply took the broad view and asked what was the chief obstacle in the way. The answer given in every report was " Sickness." Before setting about any real reform of the Poor Law or any sub- stantial and complete scheme for deeding with unemployment, statesmen had therefore to tackle the question of sickness which caused more unemployment than fluctuations of trade, and more poverty than drunkenness or any other thing. Once they had dealt with sickness they could devote attention to reforming the Poor Law and the Workhouse that Dickens did so much to transform but failed to obliterate, would soon disappear from the life and history of Britain. (Applause.) We had already provided for the older people, so that those who reached the see of 70 might choose to remain outside the Workhouse. We had been dealing for some years with the children and in a quiet, but steady and persistent way, the Local Government Board had been adopting a system of putting children into homes, under foster parents, and giving them a fair chance in the battle of life instead of herding them together and stigmatising deem as paupers. There still remained to be dealt with large numbers of honest men and women who went into the Workhouse because of sick- ness and during periods of illness. The Insurance Bill would enable us to deal much more effectively with the masses of the workers because it would give us for the first time reliable and complete information. The whole adult population, the great masses of the sick and infirm who now constituted our Workhouse and pauper population, would be provided for by methods much less expensive and much more CONSISTENT WITH THE CHARITY OF A CHRISTIAN NATION. (Applause.) When we had thus completed our great social plan there would only be one other class now frequenting the Workhouse to that with—those people who were equally afraid of work and soap. (Laughter.) Everybody in the House of Commons was agreed on the necessity for dealing with sickness and invalidity. If M,e Lloyd George had had regard only to personal popularity with the masses he could have adopted a very short cot by dealing with sickness on the same line as old age pensions. Ile could have voted a sum of money from tte Treasury and said that the Government would pay out to any main or woman who became sick or who wanted to become sick—(laughter) —los. a week. To raise the funds, he would only have had to clap a little extra on the super-tax or the higher income tas, the few eho would have had to pay could have grumbled as they had done before, and Mr. Lloyd George would not have lost much politically by it. Everybody a ould do the Chancellor this credit : that in resisting that temption he was at least</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>lIONF:ST AND COURAGEOUS</head>
        <p>lIONF:ST AND COURAGEOUS</p>
        <p>(Hear, bear.) He felt that such a scheme would have led to inefficiency in the long run and any extravagant or inefficient scheme of social reform had always eventually to be paid for by the masses of the workers and particularly by those who got the lowest wages. Though it was a short cut to temporary populary, it would have been a ' straight cut to wasteful extravagance and mismanage- ment and a very serious breach of trust to the work- people who had to provide through their labour, in co-operation with capital, the money for all social reforms. The non-contributory method woul have wiped out all the friendly societies, and that was what the Chancellor did not wish to do. He recognised the fact that for many years the masses of the people bad been evolving a methoi of dealing with the difficulty of sickness and the unemployment which followed it. No great professor from Oxford or from Germany instituted the idea of a friendly society movement, nor did it or'ginate in any book. It was about the only great social phenomenon that wps never thrashed out by some ereat man in the study. It stood unique in that it was built up in the little villages the suburbs and the hamlets of the country. Through many disoppointments, terrible disasters, and loss after kis., the masses pressed on and built up gradually great concerns like the Manchester Unity of Oddfellnws, the Order of Foresters, Hearts of Oak and other societies. In his view that stood as the most remarkable testimony upon earth to the capacity of ordinary, unskilled, unlettered workingmen to work out their own salvation if they had a fair opportunity. Mr. Jones showed how the Chancellor intended to make use of the existing machinery and said he was also determined to begin by putting security and solidarity behind every society that was to be recognized under the Act. It would be possible for smaller organizations to have all those benefits of safety and yet remain as independent as they were now. They had no need to have a membership of 5,000 or to -amalgamate with larger societies. They could retain their own rights and identity, subject to a very simple provision against bankruptcy. All small societies wishing to work on their own lines and having a surplus at the end of the year would pay half of it into a central county pool, so that if one society had tudden run of epidemic it would be able to draw from the pool to meet the exceptional demand. Every five years,after the quinquennial valuation, the surpluses would be shared out to the societies in the proportion in which they had paid in. Whatever funds the societies had already accumulated belonged to the present mem- bers and would have nothing to do with Mr. Lloyd George's scheme, which began anew. He heard that the MANCHESTER UNITY OE' ODDFELLOWS were still grumbling. He did not know what was quite the latest position but there had been lots of threats that they would not come in and work the scheme. He did not believe a word of it. The Manchester Unity of Oddfellows were not going to refuse a gift of £3,000,000 a year. He knew the officials would have preferred to have had no Lloyd George Bill, They were getting on well under the old conditions, they had been very successful and had built up a substantial</p>
        <p>ic.m.gi urnishing Problem -Solved.-</p>
        <p>concern. The permanent officials of every concern were against a change that caused them a lot of anxiety as to their own position salaries and future. He did not blame them,but the rank and file of the members should keep however their eyes:open and watch what was going to be done by the few to whom they entrusted the conduct of their affairs. Mr. Jones showed how the contributions from the State and the employer would enable societies to give Is. worth of benefit for 7d. as compared with 51d. or 6d. under the existing conditions. For sixpence the worker would would get lid, worth, for fivepence he would receive ten penny worth, and for fourpence. nine penny worth. Mr. Lloyd George had said in effect : ' I am going to compel the whole nation to share risks and any friendly society that takes the workmen's four- pence will be compelled to give him at least ten shillings a week sick pay for six months and five I chillings a week pension EO long as he lives, if be breaks down permanently, the doctor and medicine, and smatoria, and maternity benefits." If the societies later on, by efficient management, could pay more they could do so, and there would be every incentive to them to do so. Every worker would be at liberty to join more than one society so as to get more pay in time of sickness. The local club or village society had the fullest liberty in this respect consistent with a minimum wage in sickness and a minimum , I guarantee against bankruptcy. Mr. Jones went on to point out the benefits conferred by the sanatoria ' clauses and averred that the Insurance Act would be ' the means of making consumption as scarce in this • country as cholera and smallpox are to-day, compared with a generation ago. Referring to the complaints of , small farmers, tradespeople end other small employers, he asked them to consider the sacrifice they were called upon to make from a larger point of view than the effect it might cause for the present ; to look upon it in its wider outlook. Perhaps to-day they were helping others without benefit to themselves but how soon it might be when their own sons and daughters would be recipients of the benefits they or no one else could tell .and surely it was worth while to make such a sacrifke with such a contingenny in vie v. Others complained that the benefits did not begin in the first day of sickness but on the fourth. His answer to this was—Because fourpence won't stand it. He then dealt with the Post Office contribution and pointed out that the number of people who would be compelled to use this method would be very small. Experience taught that only a small percentage were refused by the examining doctor. Mr. Jones replied to three or four questions which were put dealing with the scope of the Act. These questions were not of an antagonistic nature but were asked for the sake of information. A vote of thanks to Mr Jones was moved by Mr J ll Mather, seconded by Rev R W B W'hiteway. On the motion of J D B Smith a hearty vote of thanks was also accorded to Mr. Welford for presiding.</p>
      </div>
